window.CALTPA.deepReviews={"mmsn-literacy": {"id": "mmsn-literacy", "lens": "mmsn-literacy", "thesis": "The sequence has a sound instructional direction: identify meaningful parts, combine their meanings, and apply the strategy in text. The analysis becomes stronger when it confronts the checkpoint’s scoring contradictions, the unverified 35/40 interpretation, and the difference between identifying a part and explaining a whole word.", "version": "The supplied 2025 V1 packet describes three lessons for a Grades 4–5 group and one Grade 5 focus student. The comparison below uses the saved MMSN Literacy V2.1 guide, whose rubric analysis centers on the focus student. V1 descriptors were not verified. Current-guide estimates do not retrospectively score the original submission. The V2.1 planning rubric explicitly requires both literacy and ELD goals in the summative assessment and criteria.", "standards": [["RF.4.3/RF.5.3, including .3a", "Apply sound-symbol, syllabic and morphological knowledge when reading unfamiliar multisyllabic words.", "Word-part manipulation supports the morphology component; explaining a meaning is not direct evidence of accurate decoding.", "Include oral or otherwise accessible reading of an unfamiliar word, not only a definition. Note the support and pronunciation evidence.", "Partial evidence for the broader decoding standard.", "ela", 25], ["L.4.4/L.5.4 and .4b", "Clarify unfamiliar meanings using strategies, including Greek/Latin roots and affixes.", "Prefixes, roots and suffixes are directly relevant. Common English affixes alone do not demonstrate the full Greek/Latin-root demand.", "Ask for a whole-word meaning, the contribution of each meaningful part, and a check against context.", "Direct alignment to morphology; specify the actual roots and grade-level words.", "ela", 44], ["L.4.4c; L.4.6", "Consult reference materials; acquire and use academic/domain-specific vocabulary.", "Lesson 2 lists reference use but mainly describes whiteboards and frames. Discussion and sentence writing offer vocabulary use.", "Show when a learner uses a glossary or other reference to confirm meaning, and evaluate accurate word use.", "Reference consultation is listed but not demonstrated in the plan’s activity.", "ela", 44], ["L.4.5/L.5.5", "Understand figurative language, relationships, and nuances in word meanings.", "Adding an affix and writing a sentence does not necessarily assess figurative meaning or nuance.", "Use an explicit comparison of related meanings or a figurative use if retaining this standard.", "Weak alignment; remove an unsupported mastery claim.", "ela", 44], ["SL.4.1/SL.5.1", "Participate in discussion and build on others’ ideas.", "Partner explanation creates an opportunity; taking a turn alone does not show building on a peer’s contribution.", "Record a question, clarification, or revision responding to another explanation.", "Planned opportunity; outcome evidence absent.", "ela", 35], ["ELD.PI.5.1; Grade 4 counterpart needed for the group", "Exchange and develop ideas in collaborative conversation.", "Frames and word building support this function. “ELD.PI.4–5.1” is a shorthand grouping, not a complete grade/proficiency descriptor.", "Specify grade and proficiency level for identified ELs; assess the communicative contribution and support needed.", "Appropriate function; code/proficiency details incomplete.", "eld", 87], ["ELD.PI.5.6a and .6b", "Explain text ideas through close reading; use morphology, context and references to clarify words.", "¶107 shortens the .6a demand into a general explanation. Morphological word meaning is more directly connected to .6b.", "Include a passage-based response for .6a and a word-in-context explanation for .6b.", "Correct the demand-to-task mapping.", "eld", 88], ["ELD Part II, Grade 5, strand 4", "Expand noun phrases to enrich meaning.", "¶71 incorrectly identifies Part II.4 as a morphology standard. It concerns nouns and noun phrases, not the definition given.", "Use PI.5.6b for the intended morphology connection, selecting the appropriate proficiency descriptor.", "Code/wording mismatch requires correction.", "eld", 92], ["IEP: 80% accuracy in three of four trials", "Apply word-part knowledge across repeated occasions under the stated conditions.", "One 35/40 checkpoint is one occasion with a composite score; it does not establish the IEP’s repeated-trial criterion.", "Use equivalent probes of the actual goal and record independence and trial dates.", "Progress may be discussed; goal attainment is not established.", "case", 44]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Center the Grade 5 learner without treating the group as uniform", [13, 15, 25, 29, 31, 33, 37, 39], "The focus student’s interests, oral reasoning and developing word analysis give the sequence a credible starting point. The packet also identifies language processing, paragraph organization and task initiation needs. Those are distinct targets; a morphology unit cannot establish improvement in all of them merely because the student participates. The focus student is not identified as an English learner, while some classmates are. A language goal still makes sense, but the review should not recast the student as an EL or assign an unsupported proficiency level. Grade 4 and Grade 5 classmates also need appropriate standards rather than a single merged code.", "State the focus student’s specific word-analysis baseline, response mode and prompting conditions. Use the IEP criterion to plan repeated evidence, not to infer mastery from one total."], ["lesson1", "B", "Lesson 1: teach the relation, not a prefix-matching routine", [43, 59, 67, 79, 81, 83, 85, 87, 89], "Explicit modeling of un-, re-, pre- and mis- can make an abstract process visible. Matching cards and whiteboards then allow rapid checks. The key decision is whether students simply combine familiar cards or explain how the added part changes a base’s meaning. Use examples where the relation is transparent before introducing exceptions. A frame should leave the semantic work to the learner: “The whole word means ___ because ___.” Record whether the student supplies the connection or echoes the model. The plan describes staff roles, but does not yet specify a shared prompt hierarchy that protects the distinction between supported and independent performance.", "Use a new, pronounceable real word after the model. Ask the student to read it, identify the prefix, explain the base, combine meanings, and check the result in a sentence."], ["lesson2", "B", "Lesson 2: root analysis needs actual roots and a context check", [95, 103, 107, 115, 117, 119, 121, 123, 125], "The progression from prefix to root can deepen analysis because a familiar affix does not explain an unfamiliar base. However, the plan repeatedly promises grade-appropriate roots without naming the instructional set. Its adaptation paragraph returns to modeling prefixes, which blurs the lesson’s new demand. Select a small coherent family of Greek or Latin roots, model the same root across related words, and show when a literal combination needs checking in context. The cited reference-material standard also needs an actual opportunity to consult a reference. A whiteboard answer identifies what the student thinks; the teacher still needs a rule for when that answer prompts another model or an extension.", "Name the root set and transfer word. Add a brief reference/context check and a criterion for moving from modeled to independent explanation."], ["lesson3", "B", "Lesson 3: distinguish meaning changes from grammatical changes", [131, 139, 143, 151, 153, 155, 157, 159, 161], "The examples careful, quickly and teacher can show different functions of endings, but -er is not a single-purpose suffix: it can mark an agent or a comparison. Likewise, a learner who appends a string of letters has not necessarily formed a real word or explained its grammar. The planned ten-to-twelve-base-word task differs from Part C’s later three-part checkpoint. The account needs to distinguish a practice activity from the final assessment instead of leaving the reader to assume they are the same. Smaller steps, visual references and processing time are appropriate access supports; the final record must state which remained during the independent check.", "Contrast two uses of -er, then ask for a sentence that demonstrates the intended meaning. Identify the actual summative task and use consistent directions and scoring."], ["formative", "C", "A formative check becomes useful when the response changes teaching", [178, 179, 181, 183], "The whiteboard check asks for a part, its meaning, and an explanation of the whole word. This is a useful diagnostic design because it can separate segmentation from semantic synthesis. The plan says results will guide grouping, reteaching and extension, but does not define the decision. For example, identifying re- while defining only the base calls for combining meanings; an incorrect boundary calls for segmentation practice. Those are different interventions. No self-assessment is included, which should remain an accurate description rather than be “repaired” by claiming an activity that did not occur.", "Use distinct error codes for boundary, part meaning, whole-word meaning and language expression. Show how one observed pattern determines the next prompt or lesson example."], ["checkpoint", "C", "Audit the 40-point checkpoint before interpreting its bands", [186, 187, 191, 193, 235], "The retained form labels Part 1 “10 points,” yet also states “Total Points: 20.” Five rows ask for prefix, root, suffix and whole-word meaning, but not every listed word contains all three kinds of parts. A rule must explain how a correctly blank slot is scored. Parts 2 and 3 each total ten, and the overall rubric uses 40. Under that stated denominator, 16 + 9 + 10 = 35 and 35/40 = 87.5%, in the printed Meets band of 28–35. It is not Exceeds, which begins at 36. These arithmetic checks do not verify the item-level score because the retained packet includes a marked overall rubric and blank tasks, not the student’s completed item responses.", "Correct the contradictory section label, specify per-item points including absent affixes, and preserve the student’s completed work. Keep the original artifact visible as the object of critique."], ["teaching", "F", "The strongest episode needs the learner’s actual explanation", [206, 207, 208], "The commentary describes a clear un- plus happy model and a later prompt about whether re-, meaning again, fits a chosen root. That second moment could show assessment guiding instruction, but the chosen word, initial explanation and revised explanation are omitted. The analyst cannot tell whether the learner reconsidered meaning, copied a cue, or merely changed a card. The described partner work provides an opportunity to use oral language; it does not establish what the focus student communicated. No clips are included, so demonstration-based rubric levels remain unassigned.", "Select one complete correction sequence and identify the focus student’s contribution. Explain why the prompt targeted the actual misunderstanding rather than simply producing the expected word."], ["feedback", "H", "Feedback should target the whole-word gap with an actual item", [218, 219, 220, 235, 237], "The feedback has a useful structure: recognize part identification and creation, then ask the learner to combine all meanings. This is actionable because it gives a procedure for the next attempt. Yet the packet provides no actual incorrect definition to establish whether that is the most important error. The reported subscores also deserve attention: the largest loss is four points in the 20-point identification/analysis section, while the separate meaning section loses only one. A total of 35 cannot by itself prove that whole-word definition is the dominant weakness. The section names may conceal multiple criteria, making an item-level explanation essential.", "Quote a completed definition, identify precisely what is missing, model a different example, and have the learner revise the original before attempting a new word."], ["reflection", "I", "Do not let a composite score establish IEP attainment", [227, 228, 230, 232, 235, 239, 241], "The reflection identifies a plausible pattern—parts are recognized more readily than whole meanings—but gives no group count or item distribution. For the focus student, 87.5% on the composite checkpoint is above 80 numerically, yet the IEP requires 80% in three of four trials of the specified skill. The checkpoint includes word creation and sentence completeness, so its total is not automatically the same measure. The missing completed responses also limit causal claims about the effectiveness of frames or hands-on work. The proposed Word Detective activity is most useful if it deliberately tests the gap identified in an actual response.", "Separate the classroom band, criterion-level performance, and IEP progress record. Report the number of qualifying trials and the support conditions without filling unreported trials with assumed scores."], ["family", "I", "Make family feedback understandable and tied to evidence", [243], "The planned progress report and conference can connect school learning with familiar word play at home. The family needs more than a number and a list of supports: explain that identifying a part and combining its meaning are different skills, show an actual example, and describe the next goal in everyday language. The source is a plan to communicate, not proof that a conference occurred or caregivers responded. Invitations should allow the family to use the language and materials available at home; success should not depend on families reproducing specialized instruction.", "Share one verified strength, one concrete next step, and an optional oral activity. Record actual communication separately from the proposed message."], ["followup", "J", "Extension versus reteaching: justify the choice from the gap", [248, 250, 252, 254, 256, 258, 260, 262], "Moving from isolated words to a volcano passage can extend the task because it adds context and comprehension. However, if the learner still cannot combine the parts into a meaning, that component also requires targeted reteaching. The label “extension” does not resolve the instructional question. Explain what is already secure, which component is being repaired, and what new transfer demand is added. Science interest gives the text a purpose, but a high-interest passage must still contain suitable morphological targets. The follow-up should include a new word without the identical teacher model to establish whether the learner can transfer the strategy.", "Plan a brief synthesis model, supported application in the passage, and a new independent word-in-context check. Record both meaning accuracy and the language used to justify it."], ["followup-evidence", "L", "The unforgettable model does not by itself show transfer", [275, 276], "The commentary’s un-, forget and -able example directly addresses synthesis of parts, which is a sensible response to the stated gap. The written record then describes students separating the same modeled word and explaining it with frames. That can show guided application, but it is not yet evidence of transfer to an unfamiliar word in the volcano text. The model must also be accurate about the word’s spelling: unforgettable doubles the final t before -able. The source account does not show a completed independent explanation after support is reduced. Keep the distinction between a well-chosen next teaching move and verified improvement.", "Document a new example in context and the focus student’s explanation without supplying the definition. The missing follow-up video prevents a demonstration-based score."]], "comparison": [[764, 235, "The rewrite presents the three subscores clearly and identifies complete meanings as a next step. That improves readability, but does not reconcile the larger Part 1 loss or provide the completed responses needed to validate the interpretation."], [863, 260, "The rewrite explains why connected text adds demand. It still needs to distinguish extension of secure skills from reteaching the incomplete whole-word explanation."]], "illustration": "Illustrative revision: “The reported 35/40 places the work in the printed Meets band. I cannot treat that composite total as IEP attainment. I will examine the missed items to distinguish incorrect segmentation from incomplete meanings, then use equivalent word-in-context probes under the IEP’s trial and support conditions.” This does not invent missing responses or trials.", "rubrics": [[1, "2", ["Partial — ¶45 names assessments broadly; ¶37 supplies an IEP target but not a precise baseline.", "Partial — the ELA list is broad and the Part II.4 ELD wording is incorrect.", "Supported in planning — morphology and meaning/expression are connected.", "Partial — language goals are meaningful but grade/proficiency alignment is incomplete.", "Supported — each lesson includes a whiteboard or word-building check.", "Partial — the checkpoint samples literacy and written language but does not directly show reading accuracy.", "Partial — the 10/20-point contradiction and absent scoring rules impair interpretability.", "Partial — suffix functions and ELD mapping require correction."], "The plan is substantial, but incorrect standard mapping and inconsistent assessment criteria prevent the full Level 3 comparison.", "A 1 would disregard real alignment and explicit teaching. Multiple checks cannot justify 4 until all foundational constructs are clear."], [2, "3", ["Supported — science, animals and familiar experiences inform examples.", "Supported — visual, oral, written and manipulative access is planned.", "Supported — three lessons progress from prefix to root to suffix.", "Supported — ¶81, ¶117 and ¶153 specify direct modeling and practice.", "Supported — explaining meaning and sentence use engage additional themes.", "Supported in design — frames and discussion connect language with word analysis.", "Supported — ¶89, ¶125 and ¶161 address processing and task initiation.", "Supported — ¶87, ¶123 and ¶159 identify staff roles before, during and after teaching."], "The architecture is coherent despite the separate standard and scoring problems in 2.1. A 3 is a defensible design comparison, with precise root examples still needed.", "A 2 would understate the sequence and adaptations. A 4 requires more explicit incorporation of this learner’s experiences into the actual word/text choices, not a list of possible interests."], [3, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — ¶206 describes a direct model but no video is available.", "Unverified — the focus student’s engagement is not specifically shown.", "Partial — the account matches the prefix lesson."], "The written description can guide a reviewer to the intended model. It cannot establish the required demonstration.", "Precise think-aloud language could support 4 only after the base performance is visible and verified."], [4, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — familiar vocabulary and peer discussion are described; the focus student’s asset use is not traced.", "Unverified — active engagement in the additional theme cannot be observed."], "General group discussion does not establish this learner’s meaning-making performance.", "The 4 addition needs intentional incorporation into actual instruction. Do not infer it from the planning interests."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — cards, whiteboards and frames are described.", "Partial — integrated language practice is narrated but the learner’s response is absent."], "The access design is plausible, but no demonstrated literacy/language performance can be scored.", "Multiple response modes may support 4 when they allow the learner to demonstrate the targeted understanding; availability alone is insufficient."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — ¶208 describes circulating and checking combinations.", "Partial — the re- prompt lacks the initial and subsequent focus-student response."], "The account names a responsive move but does not establish its effect or supply the demonstration.", "A timestamp and a list of assessment methods cannot establish 3 or the multiple-assessment addition at 4 without the response sequence."], [7, "2", ["Partial — the arithmetic is consistent but the underlying answers are unavailable and section scoring conflicts.", "Partial — the claimed main gap is not reconciled with the subscores.", "Supported as a reported feedback strategy — ¶219 gives a concrete synthesis procedure.", "Partial — instructional effectiveness is inferred mainly from totals.", "Supported — ¶241 proposes targeted synthesis work.", "Partial — ¶243 is proposed family feedback without a concrete student example or evidence of delivery."], "The reflection has a useful diagnostic direction and actionable language, but not the complete evidence-backed focus-student analysis required for 3.", "A 1 would ignore the subscores and targeted feedback. Concrete item examples are needed before considering 3–4, and IEP attainment must remain separate."], [8, "Insufficient evidence", ["Supported as planning — ¶254–262 adds connected-text application and responds to the stated gap.", "Unverified — the actual follow-up video is absent; ¶275–276 describes work on the modeled word.", "Partial — the decision relies on broadly described prior results rather than a specific completed definition."], "The written follow-up has instructional merit, but the required demonstrated extension or reteaching cannot be assigned a level.", "The science-interest addition alone does not establish 4. A new independent word-in-context response would strengthen the evidence, without substituting for the missing video."]], "title": "Grades 4–5 word study", "file": "02_MMSN_Literacy_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 MMSN LPA Sample Submission.docx", "quotes": {"13": "[Students' literacy skills vary. With support, several decode single- and multisyllabic words, identify important ideas in read-alouds, and use visuals or context to understand informational text. Oral discussion and shared writing are strengths. Many can write a short, organized paragraph with a frame or organizer. Continuing needs include fluency, vocabulary, main ideas, summaries, textual evidence, and applying word analysis independently. Some also need support beginning a task and sustaining attention during reading and writing.]", "15": "[The English learners are working at Emerging through Expanding levels. Vocabulary previews, visuals, sentence frames, and structured oral practice support them. Most of the class is fluent English proficient, but the integrated language work will also strengthen these students' academic vocabulary and comprehension.]", "25": "[FS is a ten-year-old fifth grader.]", "29": "[English is the language used for FS's literacy instruction and assessment. He has no English-learner, heritage-language, or multilingual identification. He receives support to access grade-level material in English.]", "31": "[FS reads grade-level material with some fluency and can identify main ideas, summarize short passages, and answer literal and inferential questions. He decodes many multisyllabic words and is beginning to use context and word parts to clarify meaning. He writes three-to-five-sentence paragraphs with a topic and some details but needs help developing the support and including a conclusion. Oral language and curiosity about words are strengths. Organizers and frames help him apply those strengths to more complete writing.]", "33": "[FS receives services for Specific Learning Disability and is also classified as ED. His needs affect reading, writing, language processing, independent word analysis, and paragraph organization. Individualized instruction and accommodations help him access grade-level content while developing these skills.]", "37": "[By 02/17/26, FS is expected to determine unfamiliar word meanings in reading and writing by applying grade-level prefixes, roots, and suffixes with 80% accuracy in three of four trials. Student work and teacher records will provide the evidence for that goal.]", "39": "[FS's behavior plan includes planned movement breaks, reinforcement for staying with a task, and a quiet space when he becomes frustrated. He receives specialized literacy instruction and small-group paraprofessional support. Text-to-speech, digital organizers, and word-prediction tools help with comprehension, vocabulary, and paragraph writing. These supports are intended to increase access and independence; he does not require AAC.]", "43": "[The three lessons form a word-structure sequence within our larger literacy unit. We begin with prefixes, move to the meaning carried by roots, and then examine suffixes. Each lesson uses explicit models, guided practice, and checks before independent application. Students will combine these parts to read unfamiliar words, understand vocabulary, and use it in writing.]", "45": "[Observations and screenings identified needs in multisyllabic decoding and comprehension. Classroom assessments and state results also showed difficulty with vocabulary and word analysis. I will respond with explicit morphology instruction, word banks, frames, and differentiated practice. Information from families and other educators will help me choose relevant vocabulary and texts that sustain students' interest.]", "59": "[Reading – Foundational Skills (Word Analysis) CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.4.3 / RF.5.3 ● Know and apply grade-level phonics and word analysis skills in decoding words. Alignment: Students apply prefix knowledge when reading unfamiliar words and working out their meanings. Reading – Vocabulary Acquisition and Use CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4 / L.5.4 ● Determine or clarify the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words and phrases based on grade-level reading and content, choosing flexibly from a range of strategies. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4.b / L.5.4.b ● Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word. Alignment: Students examine the effect of a prefix on the base word. Language – Vocabulary Development CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.5 / L.5.5 ● Demonstrate understanding of figurative language, word relationships, and nuances in word meanings. Alignment: Students describe a prefix’s contribution and demonstrate the resulting meaning in a sentence. Speaking and Listening CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.SL.4.1 / SL.5.1 ● Engage effectively in a range of collaborative discussions with diverse partners, building on others’ ideas and expressing their own clearly. Alignment: Sentence frames support explanations of prefixes during partner and class conversation.]", "67": "[Students will analyze prefixes, roots, and suffixes to explain words and determine the meaning of unfamiliar grade-level vocabulary in reading and writing.]", "71": "[California ELD Standards, Grade 5 — Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways A. Collaborative, 1. Exchanging information and ideas with others through oral collaborative discussions on a range of social and academic topics. California ELD Standards, Grade 5 — Part II: Learning About How English Works B. Expanding, 4. Using knowledge of morphology (e.g., roots, affixes) to determine the meaning of words and to support comprehension of texts.]", "79": "[I will introduce un-, re-, pre-, and mis- and model how to separate a word and explain the prefix's contribution. Partners will match prefix and root cards, record the new words on whiteboards, and explain their meanings orally and in writing. I will circulate, prompt with sentence frames, and respond immediately to errors while recording observations.]", "81": "[I will explicitly define a prefix, name the selected examples, and demonstrate how each changes a root's meaning. Think-alouds will make the reasoning visible. Students will move from guided word building to applying the process themselves, with feedback at each stage.]", "83": "[The whiteboard check will require students to combine a prefix and root, record the word, and explain it with a frame. The construction shows morphological understanding; the explanation shows how students use academic language to communicate meaning. I will use both to plan the next prompt or example.]", "85": "[Students will listen to models, discuss with partners, read unfamiliar words in a short passage, and write words and meanings. Sentence frames will support complete explanations. These collaborative, interpretive, and productive opportunities make language practice part of the morphology task.]", "87": "[Before teaching, I will review IEP goals, language demands, and behavioral supports with paraprofessionals and service providers. During the lesson, paraprofessionals will reinforce frames, support partner work, and prompt regulation while encouraging independence. Afterward, I will discuss oral explanations with the speech-language pathologist to identify any need for further expressive-language practice.]", "89": "[FS will work with familiar, high-interest words through hands-on matching, discussion, and whiteboard responses. I will provide explicit steps, models, sentence frames, and a structured group. These supports address language processing and task initiation while allowing him to show understanding orally and in writing.]", "95": "[CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4 Determine or clarify the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words and phrases based on grade 4 reading and content. ● L.4.4b: Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word (e.g., telegraph, photograph ). ● L.4.4c: Consult reference materials (e.g., glossaries, word lists) to determine or clarify meaning as needed. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.6 Acquire and use accurately grade-appropriate general academic and domain-specific words and phrases. Reading: Foundational Skills ● RF.5.3 – Know and apply grade-level phonics and word analysis skills in decoding words. ○ Use combined knowledge of all letter-sound correspondences, syllabication patterns, and morphology (e.g., roots and affixes) to read unfamiliar multisyllabic words. Language ● L.5.4 – Determine or clarify the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words and phrases. ○ Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word.]", "103": "[Students will locate roots in complex words and use their meanings to explain unfamiliar vocabulary in spoken and written responses.]", "107": "[CA ELD Standard (Grade 5) – Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways ELD.PI.5.6.a – Reading/View Explain ideas, phenomena, processes, and relationships from texts by using key details and domain-specific words.]", "115": "[I will model how to find a root in a longer word and think aloud about its meaning. Partners will identify or underline roots and explain the whole words using frames. As I circulate, I will ask guiding questions, clarify a misunderstanding, and provide immediate feedback.]", "117": "[I will define roots clearly, introduce grade-appropriate examples, and demonstrate how the central meaning contributes to a longer word. Students will first practice with me, then with support, and finally apply the process independently. This sequence makes the morphology strategy explicit.]", "119": "[Students will write the root and complete word on a whiteboard and explain the meaning orally. I will check whether they identified the part accurately and used it to make sense of the whole word. The frame-supported explanation will also show progress toward the language goal.]", "121": "[Models, sentence frames, and guided discussions will help students talk and write about roots. They will analyze words both alone and in short texts, then explain how the root helps with meaning. The task combines reading and interpretation with collaborative and written expression.]", "123": "[I will review objectives, vocabulary, and frames with the paraeducator and speech-language pathologist. We will plan where additional prompts are needed during practice. Afterward, we will compare formative work and oral responses to identify students who need further root-word instruction or language support.]", "125": "[FS will use word cards, whiteboards, and visual examples connected with animals, school, and daily activities. I will model how prefixes work with roots, check understanding often, and provide sentence frames. The structured practice will support processing and independent starting while making oral and written responses possible.]", "131": "[CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4.b Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word (e.g., telegraph, photograph, autograph). CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.5.4.b Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word (e.g., photograph, photosynthesis).]", "139": "[Students will identify suffixes such as -ful, -less, -ment, -er, -est, and -ly and explain how the ending changes a base word's meaning or grammatical form. They will demonstrate this in oral and written responses using the lesson vocabulary.]", "143": "[ELA.ELD.PI.4-5.1 Expanding and using grade-appropriate academic language in reading, writing, speaking, and listening for a range of purposes, including determining word meaning and using affixes to understand new vocabulary.]", "151": "[I will model the suffix in careful, quickly, and teacher and explain what changes when it is added. Students will create new words on whiteboards, then explain them to a partner using frames. I will circulate to check the construction and meaning, reinforce precise vocabulary, and provide help where needed.]", "153": "[I will teach -ful, -less, -ly, -er, -est, and -ment directly, showing how to separate a base from its ending and interpret the result. Think-alouds will model the reasoning before students practice on whiteboards and with partners. They will then apply the strategy more independently.]", "155": "[The planned word-building task uses ten to twelve base words. Students will add a suitable suffix, record the new word, and explain how its meaning or grammatical role changed. They will complete the task independently, using the provided language supports for an oral or written explanation. The product and explanation together assess morphology and academic communication.]", "157": "[Word banks, frames, models, and partner discussions will support the language needed to identify a suffix and explain its effect. Students will practice orally and in writing, both with classmates and independently. I will adjust the amount of support to their responses.]", "159": "[The paraeducator, speech-language pathologist, and I will review suffix vocabulary, expected responses, and planned scaffolds before instruction. During practice, support will focus on access and meaningful student participation. Afterward, we will examine work and assessment results to plan any additional morphology or language instruction.]", "161": "[FS will use engaging vocabulary, hands-on word manipulation, partner work, and choices that connect with his interests. Sentence frames, visual suffix references, smaller instruction steps, extra processing time, and frequent check-ins will support access. Guided practice before independent work will help reduce frustration while drawing on his oral-language strengths.]", "178": "[I will use a whiteboard morphology check during guided practice in each lesson. After modeling a word such as rebuild, transportation, or helpful, I will present a new word orally and visually. Students will identify and underline the relevant part, write its meaning, and explain the reasoning when asked.", "179": "I will review responses immediately, clarify misconceptions, and provide prompts, visual references, or frames where needed while still expecting students to identify the part themselves. Partner explanations will add evidence of language use. These checks will guide reteaching, grouping, additional scaffolds, or extension throughout the sequence.]", "181": "[Students should be able to identify a prefix, root, or suffix, explain its meaning, and connect it with the whole word. I will check the accuracy of their whiteboards and listen to their explanations. For the language goal, I will look for complete, meaningful responses using morphology vocabulary and the available frames. Patterns in those responses will help me decide whether to model again, provide more practice, or move forward.]", "183": "[I did not include a student self-assessment.]", "186": "[Students will complete the teacher-created Word Structure Checkpoint independently. I will read the directions and objective aloud and model a separate example before they begin. During the assessment, I will monitor engagement and use only neutral prompts.", "187": "The assessment has three parts: separating and defining word parts, explaining target words in complete sentences, and constructing a real or realistic word from a bank of parts. The rubric assigns 40 total points for accurate analysis, meaning, sentence use, and logical word creation. I will use the results to identify patterns and plan reteaching or extension, including support for FS.]", "191": "[I will collect each completed Word Structure Checkpoint. It will contain identified parts, written definitions, sentences, and a created word with an explanation. This product gives me concrete evidence of accuracy and language use that can be scored consistently and examined for both class patterns and individual progress.]", "193": "[Progress will appear in accurate identification, complete definitions that combine the parts, and logical word creation. For language, I will look for precise vocabulary and complete explanations. The rubric and written responses will show whether students can apply morphological awareness and communicate the resulting meaning together.]", "206": "[At 0:55–1:25, I invite familiar examples of words with prefixes, connecting the lesson with vocabulary students already know. At 1:25–1:50, my Google Slides model explains a prefix and shows how un- changes happy. Thinking aloud makes the relationship between a word part and meaning explicit while modeling the academic language.", "207": "From 2:02–3:10, partners explain prefixes and share examples. They can begin with their own words and use the frames to develop a clearer response. This draws on oral-language strengths and peer relationships while addressing meaning and language development.", "208": "From 4:45–8:00, students build words with cards, record them on whiteboards, and explain them. I circulate to assess the choices, combinations, and reasoning. I acknowledge accurate use of a prefix and, when needed, return students to its meaning—for example, asking them to reconsider whether re-, meaning again, fits the chosen root. Visual cues, simpler questions, and further examples provide different levels of feedback. Students who are ready explain another example or examine the meaning more fully. These observations let me adjust instruction while students are still working.]", "218": "[I gave the focus student feedback that recognized accurate identification of prefixes, roots, and suffixes and the ability to combine them into new words. I explained that these were strengths to keep using.", "219": "The next step was to explain the whole word rather than the meaning of only one part. I asked the student to say what each part means and then bring those meanings together in a single clear explanation. I also modeled a sentence frame that links the whole-word definition to the meanings of its prefix and root.", "220": "This feedback built on the student's word-building skills while targeting greater precision in meaning and academic language. The spoken rehearsal and sentence frame provided a practical way to improve the next explanation.]", "227": "[Students generally identified word parts and created words successfully. Their discussions also showed growing vocabulary and ability to explain the process. The weaker area was combining all the parts into a precise definition. Some explanations addressed only one part or remained vague.", "228": "The rubric pattern supports that conclusion: most students received full or nearly full credit for identification and creation, while incomplete meanings cost points in Parts 1 and 2. I would therefore continue applying the morphology strategy while giving more attention to a complete explanation of the whole word.]", "230": "[Explicit models and sentence frames gave students a clear process, while cards, whiteboards, and partner exchanges let them apply it actively. Strong performance on word-part identification and creation suggests those approaches supported learning. Students' oral explanations also showed that the activity gave them a meaningful way to use academic language.]", "232": "[I would add guided practice combining the meanings of the prefix, root, and suffix into one definition. An organizer showing all three meanings together would make that synthesis visible. More partner rehearsal before writing would help students develop a complete explanation rather than list separate parts.]", "235": "[FS earned 16/20 for word-part identification, 9/10 for meaning, and 10/10 for creation, totaling 35/40. He can identify and combine parts and use a frame to explain them. Some definitions, however, are only partly complete. The next step is greater precision in bringing all the parts together into the whole word's meaning.]", "237": "[I recognized FS's successful word analysis and creation, then identified complete definitions as the next area of work. Naming each part's meaning before combining them into a sentence gives him a concrete process to follow. The language frame supports a more precise explanation and helps him apply morphology in future reading and writing.]", "239": "[The explicit steps and modeled explanations provided a structure FS could follow. Hands-on construction, whiteboard work, and discussion let him apply the strategy and receive immediate feedback. His strong word creation and knowledge of word structure show the value of those supports. I would retain them while increasing the demand for a complete meaning.]", "241": "[I would add more guided examples of synthesizing all the parts into a precise definition. In a Word Detective activity, FS could break down an unfamiliar word, explain its full meaning aloud, and then write. That sequence would target the gap shown in his assessment while building on his oral reasoning.]", "243": "[I will share the results through a progress report and a parent-teacher conference. Examples from the assessment will show FS's strengths in identifying and creating words and the need for fuller definitions. I will explain how his sentence frames and vocabulary support English expression and comprehension. At home, caregivers can build familiar words with him, discuss new meanings, and encourage complete oral explanations before writing.]", "248": "[ ] re-teaching activity OR [X] extension activity", "250": "[The extension connects with RF.4.3.A and RF.5.3.A, applying sound-symbol knowledge, syllabication, and morphology to unfamiliar multisyllabic words. Students will analyze prefixes, roots, and suffixes in a grade-level passage, explain how the parts contribute to meaning, and use the words accurately in spoken and written sentences.]", "252": "[The language connection is ELD.PI.4–5.1: participating in discussion by listening, taking turns, asking questions, and building on ideas. Students will use academic vocabulary and sentence frames to explain how word parts contribute to unfamiliar meanings, then apply that language when discussing and using the new words in context.]", "254": "[I will model word analysis with a think-aloud and a visual chart. Students will identify unfamiliar words in a grade-level passage, separate the parts on whiteboards, discuss meanings, and write a sentence using an analyzed word. Questions, partner talk, whiteboards, and the final sentence will provide immediate evidence of both morphological reasoning and language use.]", "255": "● For this activity, describe how you will leverage the FS’s—and, if applicable, the additional student’s(s’)—assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.", "256": "[A passage about volcanoes will connect the extension with students' interest in science and discovery. They can use prior knowledge and familiar vocabulary while examining new words. Discussion, frames, and whiteboard work will provide several ways to contribute, making the reading and writing relevant to FS's strengths and interests.]", "257": "● Determine adaptations to address the FS’s—and, if applicable, the additional student’s(s’)—learning needs (e.g., accommodations and/or modifications, language supports, IEP supports/services).", "258": "[FS will have a modeled analysis chart, sentence frames, partner support, and additional guided practice. Highlighters or colored pencils can distinguish prefixes, roots, and suffixes. Oral and written examples, processing time, and immediate feedback will support precise meaning and sentence use while building independence with grade-level text.]", "259": "● Explain why this activity supports the FS’s—and, if applicable, the additional student’s(s’)—literacy progress. Refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 to support your instructional decision making.", "260": "[Earlier work and discussion show that students can identify parts but still need practice applying them independently in context. The extension uses that foundation and asks them to explain complete meanings in authentic reading and writing. It targets vocabulary, comprehension, and precision without restarting skills they have already demonstrated.]", "261": "● Provide an analysis of the recent literacy assessments 3 used in Step 1 and the formative and summative assessments used in Steps 2 and 3 to support your instructional decision making.", "262": "[Work samples, oral reading checks, and observations informed the original sequence. Formative work showed growing accuracy with isolated parts, while the final assessment showed readiness to extend the strategy with support for fuller meanings. I chose an extension so students could transfer those skills to a passage, original sentences, and discussion. The new checks will show whether they can use morphology more independently in context.]", "275": "[From 0:45–1:35, I model how un-, forget, and -able contribute to unforgettable. The think-aloud shows students how to use the parts together rather than stop at identifying them. It connects word analysis with meaning in reading.", "276": "From 1:35–3:30, students separate the word on their whiteboards and explain the meanings with sentence frames. Oral and written practice helps them form complete explanations and use the vocabulary precisely. The activity advances the literacy and language goals by requiring students to apply the strategy and communicate their reasoning.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"764": "Specific evidence from the summative assessment and rubric to describe the FS’s performance is that FS earned 16/20 on identifying word parts, 9/10 on determining word meaning, and 10/10 on word creation, totaling 35/40 points. This demonstrates strong mastery of word structure and word creation, while highlighting a minor gap in fully precise word meaning explanations, which can be addressed with additional targeted instruction.]", "863": "The formative assessment results from Step 2 showed that students were able to accurately identify prefixes, roots, and suffixes in isolation but needed additional support applying these skills independently in context. Those formative assessment results also support my instructional decision-making to select an Extension because they indicated that students were prepared for deeper practice, including using analyzed words in original sentences and discussing meanings with peers, which builds independence and academic language."}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}, "mmsn-math": {"id": "mmsn-math", "lens": "mmsn-math", "thesis": "The strongest feature is access: students can show mathematics through Spanish, gestures, counters, writing, and speech. The central analytical task is to distinguish a correct answer reached with prompts from independent strategy use, and to connect that distinction to the correct Grade 2 standard and each IEP goal.", "version": "The source is a 2025 V1 MMSN Math example with three focus students. Current MMSN Math V2.0 uses one focus student and redistributes the planning rubrics. There is no one-to-one historical rubric conversion. All three learners are analyzed below; the V2 rubric table uses FS1 as an explicitly identified comparison lens, with FS2/FS3 evidence considered separately. It is not a V1 rescore or a claim that the old packet meets current submission requirements.", "standards": [["2.OA.2, not the source’s “Standard 1”", "Add and subtract fluently within 20 using mental strategies; know single-digit sums from memory by year end.", "¶85 supplies the wording of 2.OA.2 under an incorrect number. Number-line work is an intermediate representation, as ¶86 correctly acknowledges.", "Separate accurate supported representation from mental fluency and memory. Four number-line problems cannot establish the entire standard.", "Correct the code in teaching analysis; retain the source wording as an error to examine.", "math", 27], ["1.OA.6, not “1.0A.6”", "Grade 1 addition/subtraction within 20, using strategies; fluency within 10.", "¶92 shifts to a Grade 1 code. This can describe prerequisite instruction, but is not the Grade 2 standard merely because students need foundational support.", "Name the Grade 2 target, prerequisite skill, IEP connection, and pathway toward more efficient strategies.", "Prerequisite alignment; grade-level target must remain explicit.", "math", 23], ["MP1", "Make sense of a problem and persist while checking the approach.", "Predicting direction and revising an incorrect landing point can show perseverance with reasoning. Enjoying a character alone cannot.", "Record the student’s first attempt, response to an error, and revised strategy.", "Planned and described; video enactment unverified.", "math", 8], ["MP5 and MP3", "Use a tool strategically; explain reasoning and respond to reasoning.", "The goal names MP5/MP3 after selecting MP1. Moving a required counter demonstrates tool use, but not necessarily tool choice. A gesture can communicate an argument if its meaning is clear.", "Specify which practice is being assessed. Ask why the direction fits the operation and how the student checks the result.", "Practice selection is inconsistent; observable criteria needed.", "math", 9], ["ALD and IEP learning goals", "Connect operation words, symbols, directions, quantities, and a communicative response.", "Multimodal responses preserve access for FS2 and allow FS1 to reason in Spanish. IEP targets need their actual accuracy, trial and support conditions to judge attainment.", "Record expressive mode, mathematical meaning, support, and accuracy separately. Do not make speech a condition of mathematical success.", "Appropriate access goal; no specific ELD code is cited.", "eld", 57]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Use the individual evidence without turning labels into explanations", [13, 17, 19, 33, 35, 48, 61], "The group description names meaningful interests and available language resources, then differentiates the learners. MAP scores alone do not identify a specific number-line misconception; classroom attempts and IEP monitoring are more useful for this lesson. FS3’s tendency to guess during a tablet assessment is a reason to interpret that result cautiously, not to discard it or assume a different true score. For FS1, the English and Spanish reading scores indicate different performance contexts but do not directly measure addition. The strongest planning inference comes from what students do with starting points, direction, and counted spaces.", "Begin with a short individual attempt and record the exact step requiring assistance. Keep assessment language, response mode, and task conditions visible alongside each result."], ["goals", "B", "Correct the standards chain before interpreting success", [85, 86, 88, 92, 94], "The rewrite improves honesty by acknowledging that a number line is an intermediate support for a mental-strategy standard. It does not correct the remaining mismatch between the selected Grade 2 standard, the Grade 1 code in the goal, and three different mathematical practices. These are not interchangeable labels. A defensible lesson goal would name the Grade 2 direction of learning while specifying that this intervention assesses starting at an addend or minuend, moving the required number of intervals in the appropriate direction, and identifying the landing number. That is a limited, observable contribution toward fluency, not evidence of fluent mental calculation.", "Write a single content/practice goal and an aligned language goal. Explain the prerequisite skill and IEP connection without calling supported number-line performance Grade 2 fluency."], ["routine", "B", "Opening routines should enable mathematical participation", [96, 105, 107, 117], "Check-ins, a visual schedule, and affirmations can make the small-group setting predictable. Character choice gives students a reason to enter the task. These design choices are most convincing when the review explains the barrier they address: uncertainty about the sequence, difficulty communicating feelings, or loss of attention during an abstract representation. The opening is not itself evidence that students understand addition. The planned 6-minute opening, 15–20 minutes of modeling/practice, 10-minute assessment and 3-minute closure total 34–39 minutes. The teacher should check whether that pacing leaves enough time for independent attempts by all three learners without converting the exit ticket into another fully guided activity.", "Monitor both access and learning: note whether the routine helps the student begin, then separately examine the number-line strategy and level of prompting."], ["model", "B", "Teach intervals, starting points, and landing numbers explicitly", [97, 98, 111, 113], "The 7 + 4 model is appropriate for showing a start at seven followed by four intervals to eleven. Counting the starting mark as the first jump would produce an off-by-one error. Recording four as the answer would confuse the change with the final quantity, the very misconception later reported for FS1. Model these as different decisions rather than a single instruction to hop. For subtraction, connect the direction to taking away a positive quantity in this lesson; avoid turning “addition always makes bigger” into an unrestricted mathematical rule. The student can explain by pointing to a start, tracing intervals, and indicating the endpoint.", "Contrast a correct and an incorrect worked example. Ask the learner to repair the error and show why the landing number, not the number of hops, answers the equation."], ["fs1", "C", "FS1: track the prompt that changes the response", [39, 124, 130, 136, 142, 148], "Spanish clarification is a resource for seeing FS1’s mathematical thinking. Explicit vocabulary, repeated demonstrations, and guided movement target the reported difficulty with directions and a developing number-line strategy. The recent monitoring report—three of five correct in two of four trials—does not specify the remaining trials or prompt conditions. It therefore cannot be converted into a complete baseline average. The plan should describe which errors are linguistic and which are mathematical. The later confusion between hops and landing number needs conceptual instruction even if the child can name forward and backward correctly.", "Use the same four-step record across languages: start, operation/direction, interval count, endpoint. Record the first attempt before any correction and the least prompt needed to continue."], ["fs2", "C", "FS2: nonverbal mathematical reasoning is valid evidence", [44, 50, 54, 126, 132, 138, 150], "The plan appropriately removes a speech requirement while preserving the calculation. Pointing, drawing and moving a counter can show the starting point, direction, number of intervals, and answer. The original suggestion that inability to count aloud causes errors is too narrow: students may count internally or use visual grouping. The review should examine the actual error before attributing it to selective mutism. A later whisper is a participation observation, not a required success criterion. The math result should be judged by the communicated reasoning and accuracy under the agreed accommodations.", "Retain nonverbal options during all checks. Add a way to select or write mathematical labels if appropriate, without making oral speech the price of demonstrating understanding."], ["fs3", "C", "FS3: connect regulation supports to observable participation", [63, 65, 67, 128, 134, 146, 152], "Predictable steps and character counters may support attention and willingness to retry. A specific IEP-related behavior, such as using an appropriate request for attention, needs a defined observation rather than a general claim of improved behavior. Part C introduces housing instability and family changes not established in the earlier contextual response. The rewrite preserves that assertion but does not reconcile its provenance. The analyst should not expand it into a diagnosis or assume that it caused a math error. Planning can be justified from the observed frustration and need for predictable routines already documented.", "Identify the observed trigger, the available regulation option, and the return-to-task routine. Separate persistence and positive interaction from accuracy and independent computation."], ["staff", "B", "Collaboration must describe a decision and its follow-through", [23, 115, 199, 234], "The candidate teaches the session alone and reports collaboration before and after it. The aide’s observation that FS3 confused operation symbols is a useful example because it leads to a concrete instructional change: gesture-supported symbol review. The analysis is stronger when it follows that decision to the student’s response and a shared next step. General statements about consulting the speech-language pathologist or counselor remain plans unless the exchange and resulting action are documented. The current one-focus-student rubric also has specific requirements for how support personnel facilitate access during the lesson or support other students; after-lesson communication alone should not be counted as satisfying that construct.", "Document who supplied the observation, what changed, and what evidence was returned. Distinguish planned consultation from a completed collaboration."], ["artifacts", "D", "Read the exit tickets before accepting the aggregate claim", [98, 194, 212, 214], "The retained tickets contain 7 + 13, 13 − 11, 1 + 4, and 17 − 15; the correct answers are 20, 2, 5, and 2. The images show three papers, including a final response of 12 to 17 − 15 on one paper and 0 on another. One paper shows the four correct final answers, but that does not identify how much help produced them. Redacted papers should not be assigned to FS1, FS2 or FS3 from their position in the image. The narrative reports two students at three of four independently and FS1 eventually correct with support. Treat that identification and support history as reported, not visually verified.", "Attach a de-identified key linking each paper to the focus-student label and record initial versus corrected answers. Do not overwrite an independent score with a prompted correction."], ["teaching", "F", "Explain the mechanism of a correction, not only the encouragement", [179, 180, 184, 188, 189], "The commentary includes more informative episodes than a generic account: FS2 points to a starting point and direction; FS1 needs a language prompt; FS3 revisits a movement error. These are useful distinctions among access, strategy and prompting. Still, no actual clips accompany the packet, so the review cannot verify the described interactions or whether the prompt supplied part of the solution. A strong commentary would show exactly how the question redirected attention, then report the student’s next action. Laughter and continuing to try can support an interpretation of persistence, but do not establish that the underlying operation was understood.", "For each selected episode, identify the initial error, teacher question, revised action and later independent opportunity. Keep the communication mode constant enough to compare attempts fairly."], ["reflection", "G", "Correct with help is progress, not independent mastery", [194, 197, 199, 202, 204, 212, 214, 216], "The rewrite makes an important improvement by explicitly distinguishing supported success from independence. Two out of three learners reportedly answered three of four independently: each is 75% on that task, not 75% of the class or mastery across repeated trials. FS1’s eventual four correct answers have different evidentiary meaning because support was required. The earlier IEP trials use different denominators and incompletely reported repetitions, so they cannot be combined with these exit tickets into a valid growth percentage. Language progress also needs its own evidence: communicating through a mode is access; accurately representing an operation in that mode is the learning claim.", "Report per-student accuracy, type of error, response mode and support level. Repeat comparable checks before claiming attainment of a multi-trial IEP goal."], ["next", "H", "Target the specific step and fade prompts carefully", [220, 222, 224, 226, 230, 238, 240, 242], "The next steps appropriately differ: FS1 needs another explicit start/direction/jump/endpoint sequence; FS2 needs consistency with the same nonverbal access; FS3 needs symbol-direction review with regulation supports. A floor number line may add movement, but its larger scale should preserve equal intervals and the same mathematical decisions. More difficult numbers are not automatically a better extension. Comparing an erroneous and a correct solution can deepen understanding without increasing the computation burden. Fading a prompt is useful when evidence supports it; removing Spanish or nonverbal communication would remove access rather than demonstrate greater independence.", "Use a short repeated probe with the same scoring rules and record the least support needed. Keep IEP accommodations and meaningful response choices in place."]], "comparison": [[263, 86, "The rewrite now distinguishes the mental-strategy standard from the intermediate number-line task. This repairs an overclaim, but the standard number and Grade 1 goal reference still need correction in the instructional analysis."], [692, 194, "The reflection now qualifies success by independence and prompting. That is a substantive improvement. The unidentified ticket images and incomplete trial records still prevent independent verification of the claimed per-student progress."]], "illustration": "Illustrative revision: “The final answer alone does not show independence. I will record the initial endpoint, the prompt given, and the revised answer. For the learner who records the number of hops, I will contrast the change with the landing number, then use a new equation to see whether the distinction transfers. Spanish and nonverbal responses will remain available.”", "rubrics": [[1, "2", ["Partial — ¶85 misnumbers 2.OA.2 and ¶92 shifts to Grade 1 and different practices.", "Partial — the number-line goal is coherent as intervention but not aligned consistently to the selected standard/practice.", "Supported — ¶94 links operation language and representation.", "Supported in general — ¶39 and ¶148 connect the lesson to FS1’s math IEP goal; attainment conditions are incomplete."], "For the current FS1 comparison, the central limitation is the broken standard-to-goal chain. The lesson can be appropriate intervention without demonstrating the full grade-level target.", "A 1 would ignore the real mathematical goal and IEP connection. A 3 requires a corrected, coherent goal chain; collaboration cannot by itself justify 4."], [2, "3", ["Supported — ¶130 uses recent IEP monitoring and observed needs.", "Supported — visual models, Spanish and manipulatives provide UDL access.", "Supported in planning — predicting and explaining a direction can elicit reasoning.", "Supported — the visual schedule and predictable routine address participation."], "The FS1 plan is reasonably specific even though the baseline record is incomplete. The analysis judges planned access, not observed instructional effectiveness.", "A 2 would understate the need-to-support link. A 4 needs clearer organization of time, space or materials for FS1 specifically, beyond a generally structured small group."], [3, "2", ["Supported — ¶98 and ¶113 identify the mathematical steps to monitor.", "Partial — ¶23 says the candidate teaches alone; ¶115 describes consultation, not the current rubric’s during-lesson support arrangements."], "Collaboration is not absent, but it does not clearly establish either of the current V2 roles for personnel during instruction. This is also a version-transfer limitation, not a retrospective fault assigned to V1.", "A 3 cannot be assumed from routine communication alone. Purposeful integration supporting 4 is not documented."], [4, "3–4", ["Supported — ¶130 connects adaptation to recent learning.", "Supported — Spanish, drawing and Spiderman draw on FS1’s resources.", "Supported — ¶124 and ¶142 combine mathematical representation and language.", "Supported — ¶148 explicitly connects supports with the IEP math goal."], "The current FS1 adaptation lens is well supported in the written plan. Visual, tactile and oral/gestural routes make 4 plausible.", "A 2 would overlook the specific links. A firm 4 needs distinct multisensory strategies rather than merely multiple display sizes; a research-grounded Level 5 rationale is not established."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Unverified — a safe environment is described but the video is not supplied.", "Partial — the account describes several access routes.", "Unverified — accurate instruction throughout the clips cannot be established.", "Partial — the description matches the planned number-line work."], "The supplied slides and narration establish a coherent design. They cannot establish the demonstrated teaching performance required for a level.", "Do not award 4 because many supports are listed. The supports must be observed in accurate instruction that meets the base constructs."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — ¶184 describes student representations, but the extent of independent reasoning is unverified.", "Partial — ¶188–189 describes monitoring and support without the actual clip."], "The hop-versus-endpoint misconception offers a strong analytical opportunity. The review cannot score how it was handled on video.", "Multiple assessments in a plan do not establish the Level 4 demonstration. An observed correction and a subsequent response are needed."], [7, "3", ["Supported — ¶194 identifies both success and continuing prompting needs.", "Supported — ¶206 links earlier number-line experience to the lesson.", "Supported as reflection — ¶201–202 gives specific participation observations.", "Supported as reflection — ¶208 connects language and interests to access.", "Supported — ¶210 explains why different response modes were needed.", "Partial but explicit — ¶204 connects correction and reconsideration; depth remains limited.", "Supported — ¶194 and ¶197 distinguish accuracy, independence and language.", "Supported — ¶212 explains FS1’s eventual success with assistance."], "The reflection is stronger because it acknowledges the limit of supported success and identifies a particular error. A 3 is a defensible written-evidence comparison, not verification of the narrated events.", "A 2 would discount the concrete distinction between hops and endpoint. A 4 is possible only with a clearer evidence-backed explanation of how assets moved FS1’s math learning forward; 5 is not established by participation alone."], [8, "3", ["Supported — ¶220–222 draws a specific lesson from the exit work.", "Supported — ¶222 and ¶232 connect strategy instruction and language.", "Supported — ¶238 retains supports while aiming to reduce prompting.", "Supported as future plan — ¶234 describes continued sharing with staff.", "Supported — the reported starting-point/hops error justifies targeted reteaching."], "The future FS1 plan addresses the diagnosed step rather than repeating every activity. It needs a more explicit repeated-probe decision rule to establish progress.", "The specificity exceeds 2. Continued use of Spanish and interests suggests the Level 4 addition, but the account needs clearer linkage to the exact new task before a firm 4."]], "title": "Grade 2 addition and subtraction", "file": "07_MMSN_Grade_2_Math_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_VI MMSN MPA (2nd Grade) Sample Submission.docx", "quotes": {"13": "[All three students have experience with English and Spanish, and two are bilingual Latino students. One often counts in Spanish, which gives us another way to work through the mathematics. Their interests include K-Pop Demon Hunters, Spiderman, and cats. I will use those characters as moving pieces on a number line, combining familiar interests with the visual and hands-on approaches that help them learn.]", "17": "[Two students are English learners. One primarily uses Spanish at home and school and is making progress in spoken English. The other prefers English at home but often does not speak at school. Visuals, modeling, and sentence openings will help both students engage with the mathematical language without relying only on an oral explanation.]", "19": "[The students receive special education services under SLD, Autism, and Emotional Disability. They need repeated practice, explicit modeling, and concrete or visual tools. Movement and hands-on work help direct their energy into the task. Some are also learning to ask for assistance through gestures, writing, or responses to an adult. I will plan opportunities for those forms of communication alongside the math instruction.]", "23": "[I will teach this session on my own. Outside the lesson, I regularly exchange observations with the aides who support these students. We coordinate tools and language across the pull-out and general education settings so students encounter familiar strategies. The lesson itself includes visuals, manipulatives, modeling, and guided practice to make that support available.]", "33": "[FS1 expresses ideas more independently in Spanish. In English, they are developing everyday vocabulary, using individual words and short phrases, and learning to read and write CVC words with support. Their MAP reading scores were 154 in English and 163 in Spanish. The overall summative ELPAC score was 1398, described as beginning to develop in listening, reading, and writing. I will pair English instruction with Spanish clarification, a number line, and guided practice.]", "35": "[FS1 can count in Spanish and to 30 in English, and recognizes most numbers through 50. Their latest MAP math score is 161. Drawings and number lines help them solve addition and subtraction problems with assistance. Repeated, hands-on practice will support greater accuracy and confidence within 20.]", "39": "[FS1's math goal involves solving addition and subtraction within 20 with strategies such as objects, drawings, and other visual supports. I will review the vocabulary, model my thinking, and give them time to try the number-line strategy with guidance. Questions and an exit ticket will help me monitor their progress toward using those tools more independently.]", "44": "[FS2 is eight and attends second grade. Selective mutism affects spoken participation. They can take part in the small-group math work through visual tools, objects, and responses that do not require speech.]", "48": "[FS2 recognizes and counts numbers through 100 and solves single-digit addition and subtraction with support. Their MAP math score is 159. A number line and repeated practice improve accuracy. Because counting aloud is not usually available to them at school, I will make the movement of a counter clear and observable, then guide practice as needed.]", "50": "[FS2 receives services under Emotional Disability. Selective mutism limits verbal explanations and oral counting; they usually communicate by nodding, pointing, gesturing, or occasionally whispering to a trusted adult. I will use hands-on and visual tasks with nonverbal response options so I can assess the mathematics without making speech a condition of participation.]", "54": "[FS2 receives counseling for anxiety and social-emotional needs and speech services addressing communication and volume. Their accommodations include extra time, reduced pressure to speak, and nonverbal ways to respond. I will use those supports during math so they can show understanding and continue working toward the IEP goal.]", "61": "[FS3 is working below grade level in number sense. They identify numbers to 50 and need support with larger numbers. Their latest MAP math score was 146. Drawings and a number line help them begin adding and subtracting. I will guide them in choosing an operation, deciding which direction to move, and checking the result.]", "63": "[FS3's eligibility is Autism. They need support with social communication, attention, flexibility, and appropriate group responses. A strong interest can sometimes draw attention away from the current task, and a difficult problem can interrupt persistence. Clear routines, structured lessons, and visual models help them understand expectations and remain involved in the mathematics.]", "65": "[Changes in routine and challenging work can leave FS3 frustrated or discouraged. At times, that leads to negative comments or difficulty staying regulated. I will provide predictable steps, clear expectations, reminders about positive language, and opportunities to pause or move so they can return to the task successfully.]", "67": "[FS3 is working on reducing inappropriate comments and gaining attention through conversation, compliments, or invitations to play. I will reinforce kindness in our daily affirmations, state expectations clearly, and use brief shake-out or movement breaks. These routines support appropriate interaction and participation in math.]", "85": "[Domain: Operations & Algebraic Thinking 2.OA. Cluster: Add and subtract within 20. Standard 1: Fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies. By the end of Grade 2, know from memory all sums of two one-digit numbers.", "86": "The standard calls for mental calculation. My students will use number lines as an intermediate support while developing the foundational understanding needed for that work.]", "88": "[MP1: Make sense of problems and persevere in solving them.]", "92": "[Students will practice addition and subtraction within 20 (1.0A.6), using a number line to increase accuracy (MP5). They will explain or demonstrate the process through their movements on the line, making their reasoning visible (MP3).]", "94": "[Students will connect mathematical terms—including add, subtract, plus, forward, and backward—to a number-line solution. They may communicate the process through speech, writing, gestures, or movement, using the language and actions to show how they reached an answer.]", "96": "[Introduction, 6 minutes: We will begin with our familiar check-in, celebrations, affirmations, and visual schedule. This gives students a chance to express how they feel and see what comes next. We will review number flashcards and the meanings of addition and subtraction, then return to the number line using Rumi, Spiderman, and a cat.", "97": "Modeling and guided practice, 15–20 minutes: I will demonstrate 7 + 4 on the smart board, thinking aloud about the starting number, direction, and four jumps that lead to 11. Each student will then choose a character and solve a problem at the board. They will decide how to move and explain or show their reasoning. For individual practice, they will use a pencil or character on the printed number lines. I will observe and clarify as needed, accepting pointing and gestures as well as speech.", "98": "Assessment, 10 minutes: The exit ticket has two addition and two subtraction problems. I will check the selected operation, direction, number of jumps, and recorded answer, noting the support each student needs. Those observations will guide the next lesson and our work toward their IEP goals.", "105": "[Our predictable opening routine will include an emotional check-in and affirmations about kindness, persistence, and trying again after mistakes. I want students to enter the task knowing that they can share how they feel and take a risk without being embarrassed. These routines support the engagement they need for mathematical work.]", "107": "[Students may choose Rumi, Spiderman, or a cat as their counter. That choice brings their interests into the task and gives them some control over participation. I will present number lines on the smart board, a mat, and the printed worksheet so they can use the same idea at different sizes and in different forms.]", "111": "[I will ask students to predict how a number changes, choose a direction, and decide how many spaces to move. They will explain or demonstrate why their movement fits the operation. Linking a symbol, a change in quantity, and an action on the number line gives them a developmentally appropriate way to reason about a solution.]", "113": "[I will watch starting points, directions, and counted jumps throughout the lesson and listen for the math vocabulary students use. Questions and specific feedback will help them notice and correct an error during practice. The exit ticket will add individual evidence of accuracy and strategy use, helping me plan the next level of support.]", "115": "[Before and after instruction, I will compare observations and student work with the aides and coordinate the language and tools used in other settings. I will also consult the speech-language pathologist about vocabulary and communication supports and the counselor about FS3's regulation strategies. Shared approaches to prompting and modeling will help students use the strategy more consistently and independently.]", "117": "[The check-in gives students practice recognizing feelings and managing frustration. Throughout the lesson, they will communicate a process, follow a routine, and use materials for a purpose. They can do this through speech, gestures, or movement. These habits support independence, while the addition and subtraction skills have practical uses beyond the lesson.]", "124": "[I will teach FS1 the essential vocabulary directly and provide repeated opportunities to use it. Visuals, number-line models, and gestures will connect add and subtract to their symbols and to quantities becoming larger or smaller. Guided repetition will let FS1 practice the English terms while engaging with the mathematics.]", "126": "[For FS2, I will pair explicit modeling with a visible number line. I will think aloud while moving a character, then let FS2 practice the same steps with a chosen counter. The board, mat, and worksheet will carry the strategy through the lesson. This makes the thinking observable without requiring speech and gives me a way to check understanding while reducing pressure related to selective mutism.]", "128": "[FS3 will have a predictable sequence, a visual schedule, and posted expectations. A feelings chart and sentence frame will support the opening check-in. During math, a tangible counter and number line will make the task clear. These supports are intended to help FS3 regulate, anticipate what comes next, and stay involved.]", "130": "[FS1's MAP math score rose ten points to 161. Recent goal monitoring showed three of five problems correct in two of four trials. Their counting and number recognition have improved, but observations show a continuing need for repeated explanations and vocabulary support. I will use modeling, Spanish clarification, and guided number-line practice to build on that progress toward the IEP goal.]", "132": "[FS2's MAP score was 159, and recent IEP monitoring showed three of four correct in one of three trials. I have observed more accurate work when they can use a number line with support. A chosen counter will let FS2 rehearse that strategy and show the process without speaking, while giving me clear evidence of progress.]", "134": "[FS3's MAP math score was 146, although guessing during tablet testing may limit how well that score represents their knowledge. In goal monitoring, they answered four of five correctly in one of three trials. Classroom observations show success with guidance and visual support. I will continue structured number-line practice and consult the counselor about regulation so the opening check-in and math supports address current needs.]", "136": "[FS1 can draw on Spanish while practicing English math language. I will make that connection visible through demonstrations and allow practice in both languages. The Spiderman counter adds a familiar interest to the number-line work and gives FS1 a concrete way to show a solution.]", "138": "[FS2's strength in visual and hands-on work will guide the response options. They may point, gesture, write, or move a cat counter to show the calculation. That familiar interest and the opportunity to work without speaking should support both engagement and clearer evidence of understanding.]", "142": "[FS1 will hear the math terms in my think-aloud, see them connected to symbols and movement, and practice explaining a step with support. Reading and recording numbers will accompany the visual work. Immediate feedback and opportunities to use Spanish will help them access the task while continuing to develop English academic language.]", "146": "[FS3's housing instability, home circumstances, and recent family changes can affect attention and emotional regulation. I will provide a consistent learning environment through predictable routines and visual expectations. Guided practice and clear representations will support confidence and sustained attention, allowing FS3 to engage with both the mathematics and its language.]", "148": "[FS1 will use the number line to practice the addition and subtraction strategies in the IEP goal. I will model explicitly, review vocabulary, and provide guided support. The exit slip will show which steps are becoming secure and where future lessons should focus.]", "150": "[FS2 will solve problems within 20 with a number line and cat counter. I have seen better accuracy with this visual strategy. Making the jumps visible may help with errors that occur when oral counting is unavailable. Repeated practice and nonverbal responses will support the IEP math goal while accommodating selective mutism.]", "152": "[I will support FS3's participation through a feelings check-in, familiar routines, visual expectations, and opportunities for a classroom responsibility. Guided work with Rumi on the number line will connect those behavioral supports to the math task. The aim is to strengthen attention, positive interaction, and confidence in completing the work.]", "179": "[From 0:00–1:30, I begin with our social-emotional check-in. Students can use the feelings visuals and sentence frame, but they may also gesture or use Spanish. At 0:22, FS1 begins explaining happiness in Spanish. At 1:12, I offer FS2 a thumbs-up or thumbs-down response. These options make participation possible without placing the same speaking demand on everyone.", "180": "At 2:13, the familiar characters draw students into the math, especially FS3. Around 2:30, my questions connect the movement to words such as hop, forward, and backward. I watch each student's actions and responses to decide when another prompt is needed. At 3:15, I guide FS3 back to an error through a question; they laugh and keep trying. At 3:55, FS3 thanks FS2, an example of the positive interaction we practice through our affirmations.", "184": "The character choices help sustain interest while students practice. FS3 connects movement and direction in an explanation. At 4:22, FS2 points to the starting place and direction and gestures to show the quantity increasing. At 5:00, FS1 uses prompting to explain that Spiderman moved six spaces backward. These responses let students communicate mathematical meaning through different means.", "188": "During 1:04–3:58, I circulate as students complete the exit slip. FS3 uses Rumi and describes the movement. FS2 uses the cat, pointing and gesturing to show a starting point, direction, and result. FS1 needs additional time and guidance, including encouragement to count in Spanish. These different responses help me see their mathematical thinking without making spoken English the only measure.", "189": "FS2 and FS3 show accurate number-line work, with FS3 needing scaffolds earlier. FS1 requires more immediate support. Observation, questions, repeated modeling, and prompts let me respond to the particular step causing difficulty. The lesson supports progress toward the math and language goals while showing that accuracy and independence are still developing.]", "194": "FS1 initially recorded the number of jumps rather than the landing number. FS3 also needed help with the number of spaces. Questions helped both reconsider the process, while FS2 showed more independent use of the line. Two students solved three of four problems correctly on their own. I see progress from the earlier session and goal monitoring, but the need for prompts means the group has not yet reached complete independent mastery.]", "197": "I checked language understanding through questions and observation during guided and individual work. Every student communicated through at least one mode, although the vocabulary was not consistently accurate or independent. I would continue those access options while adding opportunities to explain in speech or writing and to name a direction more precisely than this way or that way.]", "199": "[The aides' input helped me plan useful supports even though they did not teach in the recorded lesson. One aide reported that FS3 confused the symbols, so I included gestures linking plus and minus with the corresponding movement. FS3 still needed help counting spaces but showed a clearer sense of direction during practice. Coordinating number lines, prompts, and vocabulary also made the tools familiar. Afterward, I shared the remaining needs shown in the guided work and exit tickets.]", "201": "[Our check-in, affirmations, and agenda gave the group a familiar beginning. FS3 shared happiness about a new baby sister, and I responded with a silent cheer. Gentle reminders about hand raising, encouragement after mistakes, and character choices helped students stay involved.", "202": "FS2 volunteered to work at the board, an activity they usually avoid, and whispered in front of the other focus students. That was notable because classmates and the general education teacher had not heard them whisper. These observations suggest that the supportive setting helped students participate and take risks while working through the mathematics.]", "204": "[I watched closely for the step behind an error. When FS1 wrote the number of hops, I asked about the number reached. When FS3 moved too many or too few spaces in Video 2, I asked them to compare the movement with the problem. These questions supported correction without supplying the answer. At the Step 2 moment labeled 5:12, FS2 demonstrated the movement successfully without speaking. The exit work showed that all three could apply the strategy with varying support. Next time, I would check vocabulary understanding more often to help prevent errors.]", "206": "[Part A showed that students already recognized numbers and had some experience with addition, subtraction, and number lines. Their main need was greater accuracy in using the strategy. I therefore focused on the relationship between an operation and its movement rather than reteaching number recognition. Explicit models and guided practice targeted the starting point, direction, and jumps while supporting their IEP math goals.]", "208": "[Students' character interests, bilingual resources, and comfort with movement informed the lesson design. Spanish supported FS1's thinking, while pointing and gestures gave FS2 a strong way to respond. Rumi and Spiderman drew attention to the number-line task; FS3 expressed excitement about Rumi and thanked the character after solving a problem. These choices helped students sustain participation and show understanding through movement, language, or both.]", "210": "[FS1 needed repeated directions and guided visual practice. FS2 needed ways to communicate mathematical understanding without speech. FS3 needed support for attention and regulation. I built the lesson around explicit models, number lines, counters, gestures, and predictable routines so these needs could be addressed within the shared task. The supports gave each student access while keeping the focus on the addition and subtraction goals.]", "212": "[Spanish clarification, gestures, and visual models helped FS1 stay with the mathematics as English language developed. In Video 1, I supplied an English word after inviting a Spanish explanation during the check-in. In Video 3, counting in Spanish supported the calculation. FS1 eventually reached the correct answer on every exit-ticket problem with support. That shows progress in using a strategy and persisting through a problem, rather than independent mastery of every step.]", "214": "[FS2 showed understanding by pointing, moving a counter, and gesturing about quantities becoming larger or smaller. Those options supported the math goal without requiring speech. The exit ticket provides a useful measure of independence: FS2 answered three of four correctly without assistance. The visual strategy and guided practice therefore supported meaningful progress toward the IEP goal.]", "216": "[The routine, visual tools, and positive responses to mistakes helped FS3 remain engaged. In Video 2 at 4:05, a guiding question led them to reconsider an error, laugh, and continue rather than become upset. They solved number-line problems with limited prompting and showed their thinking through movement and language. That persistence supports both mathematical practice and the IEP goal for positive engagement.]", "220": "[I would revisit the strategy with the group. This was only their second number-line lesson, and the observations and exit tickets show that support is still needed for consistent independent work. In Video 3 at 3:22, for example, FS1 needs help with the starting number and the spaces moved. More practice and explicit vocabulary instruction will strengthen the foundation for accurate addition and subtraction.]", "221": "b. For Focus Student 1", "222": "[FS1 needs another supported lesson on the starting point, direction, and number of jumps. Video 3 at 3:22 shows why that practice matters. I would think aloud, connect each term with an action, and have FS1 repeat or demonstrate the step. The goal is to reduce reliance on prompts while strengthening both the math strategy and its language.]", "224": "[FS2 does not need the whole lesson retaught. At 4:45 in Video 2, gestures and pointing show a clear sense of the operation and direction. The exit ticket shows three of four problems completed independently, with support needed on one. I would retain nonverbal options and provide further practice to develop consistency before moving to harder problems. The next-step discussion also considers written ways to show thinking.]", "226": "[FS3 can use the number line when visual cues, routines, and hands-on supports are available. The self-correction at 4:05 in Video 2 shows persistence. I would give a brief review of the addition and subtraction symbols and their directions, rather than repeat all the content. Modeling each operation and asking FS3 to explain or show its meaning should build consistency. I would keep the interests, encouragement, and guided practice that supported engagement.]", "230": "[I will ask students to predict what changes when an operation or number changes—for example, when one quantity becomes 15. Comparing two methods and explaining a choice will extend the reasoning beyond carrying out jumps. Sentence openings will support explanations, and FS3 may write or draw a response. I want students to connect their actions to a reason while becoming more independent.]", "232": "[I will continue linking vocabulary to clear visual and physical examples, as in Video 2 at 5:45. Along with add and subtract, I will reinforce sum and difference through questions and sentence frames used during actual problems. Gestures and home-language connections will remain available. Repeated opportunities to use the terms meaningfully will support both mathematical understanding and communication.]", "234": "[I will keep exchanging progress information with aides and the general education teacher, as I did while planning this lesson. After sessions, I will share effective strategies and the steps that still need work. Coordinating tools, vocabulary, and prompts across settings—and using colleagues' feedback to revise instruction—will help students practice consistently.]", "238": "[FS1 will continue with a labeled number line, counters, and scaffolded practice within 20. I will connect symbols, words, and movements, including Spanish-English links such as total and sum. Sentence frames will support explaining a starting point and the number of jumps. Repeated modeling and gradual reduction of support will address accuracy and independence in the IEP goal.]", "240": "[FS2 will use a large number line, a movable counter, and direction arrows. Pointing and gestures will show the operation and intended movement. Before independent practice, we can circle the starting number and trace the jumps together. These visual cues will support accuracy and persistence without requiring an oral response.]", "242": "[For FS3, I will retain predictable routines, cue cards for the operations, matching gestures, and hands-on work such as a floor number line. Positive reinforcement, movement breaks, and guiding questions will support correction without frustration. These strategies address regulation and engagement while students continue adding and subtracting.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"263": "Note: Although this standard states that students should be able to know all sums within 20 using mental strategies, my students will be focusing on using visual supports such as number lines, in order to build foundational skills in addition and subtraction. ]", "692": "FS1 was able to self-correct and complete the problems correctly, and FS3 demonstrated improved understanding after additional prompts. FS2 consistently used the number line accurately and showed independence. Through the formative assessment and observations, I was able to determine which students needed additional support and how to target interventions. This just-in-time support not only made them realize they made a mistake, but helped them know what they did. I was happy to see that 2 students solved 3 out of 4 problems correctly on their own. Although the students haven’t reached 100% mastery, these strategies were effective because all students demonstrated understanding through movement and showed measurable progress from the previous session and goal monitoring assessments.]"}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}, "ms-historical": {"id": "ms-historical", "lens": "historical", "thesis": "The lesson makes opinion writing accessible through a familiar story, oral rehearsal, and a model. Its most consequential weakness is assessment coherence: the teacher reports that 21 of 23 students met the goal, yet later reports that most wrote two sentences when the stated goal required three to five. The review therefore distinguishes a defensible introductory opinion-and-reason task from mastery of the full writing standard.", "version": "This is the historical 2018 Version 01 Multiple Subject Cycle 1 example, not a current MS Math task. The eight rubric focuses below follow the CTC-authored 2018 guide text available through the linked public mirror. The supplied example’s 2018/V01 identification was checked. The mirror is a secondary host; the current official guides do not govern this retrospective comparison. The brief rubric summaries are paraphrases, and the estimates are instructional judgments, not official scores.", "standards": [["W.3.1a–d", "Introduce a topic and opinion, organize and support reasons, link ideas, and conclude.", "¶95 narrows the lesson to an opinion with a reason and picture; ¶205 correctly reserves fuller organization and development for subsequent instruction.", "Assess the position, relevance of the reason, connection to the story, linking language, and closure separately. A sentence count is a task constraint, not a sufficient quality measure.", "A reasonable introductory portion; full-standard mastery is not established.", "ela", 28], ["SL.3.1b and SL.3.1d", "Follow discussion expectations and explain ideas in relation to the discussion.", "Three partner exchanges offer practice. Taking a turn and agreeing that opinions differ do not establish explanation or responsiveness to a peer.", "Retain an exchange in which a student explains a reason and responds to another view. Note participation separately from reasoning.", "Planned alignment; the actual exchanges are unavailable.", "ela", 35], ["Grade 3 ELD Part I: 1, 5, 11", "Exchange ideas, listen actively, and support opinions at the applicable proficiency level.", "¶93 names these language demands. FS1’s oral rehearsal and sentence starters fit them, but the account does not select and substantiate the relevant proficiency descriptors.", "Collect an oral explanation and subsequent writing. Identify what the student supplies independently and with a frame. Do not equate an overall ELPAC level directly with a named ELD proficiency level.", "Purposeful language supports; proficiency-specific alignment needs clarification.", "eld", [67, 68, 69]]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Use the class profile to make a testable planning decision", [8, 10, 12, 16, 121], "The transition from animal reports to opinion writing gives the new task a meaningful starting point: both require organization, but explaining researched facts and justifying a position are different intellectual demands. The rewrite appropriately describes a foundation rather than universal mastery. Part C reports that 86% met the earlier writing expectations; this should qualify any broad claim about the whole class. The profile also contains a small but visible count problem: the stated birthplaces account for 22 of 23 children. That omission does not determine lesson quality, but it should be acknowledged rather than filled with an invented biography. Weather interests have a direct instructional connection. Demographic categories alone do not establish interests or language needs.", "Check one recent writing sample for each student’s organization and one brief oral opinion for reasoning. Use those results to choose supports; correct the missing demographic count only from an actual record."], ["goal", "B", "Separate the learning goal from the length of the product", [92, 93, 95, 101, 107], "An opinion plus a relevant reason is a manageable entry into third-grade opinion writing. The three-to-five-sentence requirement adds a product expectation, but extra sentences can repeat the same idea without developing it. Conversely, two well-connected sentences can show the intended opinion-and-reason relationship. The source needs a clear distinction between the conceptual goal and the format requirement. Sorting work into below, meets, and exceeds is not a usable rubric until the criteria and boundaries are specified. Without those boundaries, the later class counts cannot be independently interpreted.", "Before teaching, define the evidence: a clear position, a relevant reason grounded in the story, and an understandable connection. Record length separately. Explain which features distinguish meeting the introductory goal from extending it."], ["sequence", "B", "Read-aloud pauses should deepen the quality of reasons", [103, 105, 109, 113], "The sequence moves from listening, to private thinking, to partner rehearsal, to individual writing. This reduces competing demands while preserving the opportunity to form a view. The three pauses can be more than repeated invitations to say whether the town sounds enjoyable. The teacher can ask students to compare early benefits with later consequences, reconsider an initial reason, and decide which detail most strongly supports their position. That progression would develop evaluation using the story. A thumb signal about knowing the word opinion provides a quick confidence check; it does not demonstrate that the student can distinguish a reason from a restatement.", "At the first pause collect a position and reason; at a later pause ask whether new story information changes the reason. Compare the explanations rather than counting enthusiastic responses."], ["fs1", "C", "FS1: oral strength is a resource, not proof of written independence", [44, 46, 50, 139, 197], "FS1’s oral-language strengths, interest in read-alouds, and drawing and writing habits give a coherent basis for rehearsal before writing. Reading the frames aloud reduces a barrier that a displayed scaffold alone might leave in place. The reflection reports that she used three sentence starters. This shows uptake of a support, but it does not identify the opinion or establish whether she generated relevant reasons. A frame can organize a child’s reasoning or allow copying without reasoning. The difference is visible in the content she contributes and her explanation of it. An ELPAC result should be interpreted from the actual report; the account’s move from level 3 to “bridging” is not independently substantiated.", "Preserve her spoken reason and the resulting sentence. Ask her to explain the connection without rereading the model, then fade a frame on a new topic if the meaning remains clear."], ["fs2", "C", "FS2: plan access to classmates as well as the teacher", [56, 58, 68, 141, 198], "The seating orientation and microphone are concrete accommodations tied to the stated hearing need. They preserve the same opinion-writing goal rather than reducing its intellectual demand. The access chain is less complete during partner talk: the teacher’s microphone does not automatically make a peer’s voice accessible. A clear view of the partner’s face, agreed turn-taking, and a check of what FS2 heard would strengthen the plan. The reflection establishes reported participation, but supplies no example of his written opinion or reason. Access is necessary and worth analyzing; it is not itself evidence that the writing goal was achieved.", "Check the hearing-access arrangement during each lesson phase with the appropriate classroom support team. Retain a brief partner response and a written reason, then judge writing with the same content criteria used for classmates."], ["fs3", "C", "FS3: analyze the sequence of support and response", [70, 73, 77, 143, 199], "The plan draws on conversation, drawing, and movement while recognizing attention and work-completion needs without assigning an unverified diagnosis. Part G offers the strongest decision sequence in the case: distraction leads to a workspace change; the paper remains blank; oral explanation and displayed starters then help the student begin and finish. This sequence suggests that relocating alone was insufficient and that composing language may have been a separate barrier. It does not show which support caused completion, because several changed together. Nor does completion show the quality of the reason. The response deserves credit for responsive teaching while keeping those limits visible.", "At the next attempt, offer oral rehearsal before independent writing and a planned workspace choice. Record the reason expressed, the support needed to start, and whether the student completes a relevant written explanation with less prompting."], ["resources", "D", "Read the model and blank handout as instructional artifacts", [115, 133, 135], "The retained pages include a book summary, a model opinion paragraph with sentence openings, and a blank writing-and-drawing handout. The model offers a position, reasons, and closure, which can make the genre visible. It also risks students borrowing the teacher’s position unless contrasting defensible views are welcomed. The half-page format may make the task approachable, but the available space should fit the stated writing expectation. Neither page is completed student work. There is no retained analytic classroom scoring tool that establishes why 15 papers met and six exceeded expectations.", "Discuss what each sentence contributes to the model. Offer a different defensible opinion without supplying all its reasons. Retain actual student papers and apply the announced criteria consistently."], ["video", "F", "A timestamp locates a claim; the exchange establishes its strength", [154, 160, 163, 166, 172, 175, 181, 184], "The annotations distinguish expectations, participation, monitoring, and thinking, but several labels exceed the described evidence. A reminder about sitting primarily manages participation. Recognizing that one may disagree with the teacher supports intellectual safety; it does not yet show higher-order reasoning about the story. Reporting a partner idea could show reasoning if the response includes a justified connection, but the annotation does not preserve that exchange. Chorally naming opinion checks terminology, not the ability to write one. Considering another possible opinion topic is transfer at an introductory level. No actual clips accompany this packet, so the enacted-teaching rubrics cannot receive defensible numeric estimates.", "For each chosen event, identify the teacher prompt, the student’s actual response, the interpretation, and the immediate adjustment. Use the recording to test whether the annotation’s label matches what occurred."], ["reflection", "G", "Connect support to a specific learning result", [191, 193, 195, 197, 198, 199], "The reflection connects the class profile to the lesson design and gives plausible explanations for oral rehearsal, visual models, and flexible participation. Its limitation is that most claims concern access or completion. FS1 used starters; FS2 could hear and participate; FS3 finished after added help. Those are different and useful outcomes, but none supplies a complete analysis of the opinion-and-reason goal. The broad statement that circulation allowed clarification also needs a specific misconception and response. Stronger reflection would preserve both benefits and limits rather than convert every planned support into a success claim.", "For each focus student, pair one support with one observable content or language response. State what remained difficult and what alternative explanation could account for the result."], ["results", "H", "Reconcile the 21-of-23 claim with the stated goal", [95, 203, 208], "The reported categories total correctly: 15 meeting plus six exceeding plus two below equals 23; 21 of 23 is about 91.3%. The two below are described as stating an opinion without a reason, a useful diagnostic distinction. Yet the later admission that most wrote only two sentences conflicts with treating the three-to-five-sentence goal as met by nearly everyone. The most defensible interpretation is that the teacher used a narrower opinion-and-reason criterion in practice. That interpretation must be stated as a possibility until the papers and scoring criteria are examined. The earlier 86% referred to animal reports, so it is not a second score for this task.", "Report content-goal attainment and length compliance separately, using the actual papers. Do not manufacture revised category counts. Explain any mismatch between the announced and applied criteria before claiming class readiness."], ["next", "H", "Use different next steps for missing reasons and emerging organization", [203, 205, 207, 208], "The proposal to confer with the two writers who offered no reason follows directly from the reported evidence. Asking each to explain orally can help distinguish a reasoning need from a writing-production need. The broader Writer’s Workshop sequence appropriately extends toward the full W.3.1 standard through introduction, organization, links, and conclusion. It still needs a quick check to decide which writers require which mini-lesson. Projecting illustrations and adding a word bank improve access; repeating a sentence requirement improves clarity. Neither guarantees better reasoning. A stronger next lesson would judge the relevance and development of a reason before treating added length as progress.", "Begin with a new short opinion task. Group by the demonstrated need: generating a reason, explaining its relevance, organizing reasons, or adding closure. Recheck the same feature after the targeted instruction."]], "comparison": [[13, 8, "The rewrite replaces a broad mastery claim with a more defensible description of prior work. That is an improvement in calibration, but the teacher still needs to connect the reported 86% result to specific planning decisions and avoid treating report writing as evidence of opinion-writing mastery."], [503, 208, "The rewrite clearly identifies the mismatch between the spoken direction and planned length. It does not reconcile that admission with the claim that 21 students met the goal. Better prose makes the problem visible; a defensible analysis requires the applied criteria and actual papers."]], "illustration": "Proposed analysis frame, not an event established by the packet: “The paper states a position but repeats it as the reason. I will first ask the student to explain orally which story event supports that position. If the oral explanation is relevant, I will support its transcription; if it repeats the position, I will compare two story details and ask which one actually supports the claim. A new short response will show whether the distinction transfers.”", "rubricSummaries": ["Appropriate goals, assessment evidence, accessible challenge, scaffolding, thinking, and language.", "FS1 adaptations connect language assets and needs with content access.", "FS2 adaptations address the learning challenge while maintaining the goal.", "FS3 supports well-being and participation in content learning.", "The lesson opening establishes expectations, prior-learning connections, and a supportive environment.", "Teaching develops content thinking and monitors understanding.", "Reflection connects student knowledge, instruction, assessed learning, and next steps.", "Future instruction develops content and language in response to evidence."], "rubrics": [[1, "2–3", ["Supported — ¶95 defines an introductory goal; ¶121 connects prior work.", "Partial — ¶107 and ¶135 lack operational score boundaries; ¶208 conflicts with ¶203.", "Supported — ¶109–117 provides oral, visual, written and movement access.", "Partial — ¶125 offers reasoning opportunities, but the evidence of higher-order thinking remains thin."], "The plan has meaningful strengths, but assessment coherence prevents an unqualified Level 3 judgment. The range reflects the difference between a defensible introductory goal and the unreconciled stated product requirement.", "A 1 would overlook substantial planning. A 4 requires more consistently justified adaptations and assessment evidence; the available record does not support 5."], [2, "3–4", ["Supported — ¶44–52 identifies oral-language and interest resources.", "Supported — ¶139 connects rehearsal, read-aloud frames, a model, and feedback to writing demands.", "Partial — ¶197 reports frame use without the student’s reason or independence."], "The planned support is specific enough for a Level 3 comparison and includes multiple communication modes that may support 4. The upper estimate remains conditional on a clearer language-demand rationale.", "Generic support would be closer to 2. Multiple modes are purposeful here, but the record does not establish a complete top-level UDL analysis or independent language growth."], [3, "3–4", ["Supported — ¶56 and ¶68 identify a concrete hearing-access need.", "Supported — ¶141 preserves the content goal through seating and microphone use.", "Partial — access to peer discussion and resulting writing are not documented."], "The accommodation fits the reported need and maintains an appropriate learning task. The rationale supports consideration above 3, with a remaining gap across the partner-talk phase.", "A low estimate would ignore the clear individual match. The absence of a complete access plan and supporting learning evidence limits the higher-level claim."], [4, "3", ["Supported — ¶70–81 identifies observed needs and interests without diagnosing.", "Supported — ¶143 plans movement, workspace choice, quiet redirection and writing support.", "Partial — the plan could better anticipate task initiation, revealed by the later blank paper."], "The supports plausibly address well-being and content participation. The subsequent response shows why movement alone was not sufficient.", "The specificity exceeds a generic Level 2 approach. A higher judgment requires a more fully justified, proactive connection between the student’s assets, needs, and academic work."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Reported — ¶154 describes an expectation reminder.", "Reported — ¶157 connects earlier writing.", "Unverified — actual opening interactions and environment cannot be viewed."], "The annotations provide locations and interpretations, not the required teaching performance. No numeric estimate is assigned.", "The distinction among levels depends on the quality of the actual opening and interaction; polished annotation cannot establish it."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Reported — ¶166–175 describes evaluation and discussion opportunities.", "Partial — ¶160 and ¶181 concern self-report and vocabulary rather than a full understanding check.", "Unverified — the actual teaching, student reasoning, and adjustments are unavailable."], "The written account contains opportunities for higher-order thinking, but does not demonstrate how deeply students reasoned or how monitoring affected teaching.", "A low numeric score would also overclaim certainty. The needed evidence is the observable exchange and response, not the number of timestamps."], [7, "2–3", ["Supported — ¶191–193 links student information with instructional choices.", "Partial — ¶195 is general, while ¶199 provides a concrete adjustment sequence.", "Partial — ¶197–199 emphasizes access/completion more than goal-quality evidence.", "Partial — ¶203 and ¶208 need reconciliation before class learning is clearly analyzed."], "The reflection provides substantial explanation and one useful in-the-moment decision sequence. The range reflects the unresolved learning criteria and uneven evidence for the three focus students.", "Level 1 would disregard meaningful connections. A stronger Level 3 or 4 requires explicit analysis of what the work demonstrates, including the limits and reasons for adaptations."], [8, "3", ["Supported — ¶203 targets the two students missing reasons.", "Supported — ¶205 extends the full opinion-writing standard.", "Supported — ¶207–208 identifies changes to language resources and directions.", "Partial — next steps for each focus student and measures of impact remain underdeveloped."], "The proposed instruction follows the reported patterns and advances content and language. A Level 3 instructional comparison is reasonable even though the starting counts need audit.", "The specificity exceeds a generic reteach plan. More differentiated next steps and explicit checks of their effects would be needed to justify the higher levels."]], "title": "Grade 3 opinion writing", "file": "01_MS_Cycle_1_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_CalTPAMSCycle1ExampleRevised.docx", "quotes": {"8": "[My third graders have just completed five-paragraph animal reports. They researched a topic and organized their findings with an opening sentence, supporting information, transitions, and a conclusion. That work gives them a foundation for our next writing unit. We will move from explaining a topic to taking a position and supporting it with reasons, as called for in CCSS.ELA-Literacy.W.3.1. I will continue to reinforce the paragraph skills they have already practiced.]", "10": "[Two eight-year-olds are English learners: a Spanish-speaking boy and an Assyrian-speaking girl. Both received an overall ELPAC level of 3. Their oral-language results are stronger than their reading and writing results: speaking and listening were well developed, while literacy skills were somewhat or moderately developed. They participate across subjects with limited differentiation. In planning this lesson, I will provide the light language support described for their bridging level.]", "12": "[The class includes 23 students: five Asian, three Indian, six Hispanic, one African American, and eight Caucasian. I learned about them through conversations, classroom observations, cumulative records, and discussions with my Master Teacher, case managers, and resource teachers. The background information lists 19 students born in the United States and three born elsewhere—Hong Kong, Assyria, and Mexico. Most have grown up in California near XX.", "16": "[Our recent science unit covered clouds, storms, weather, and natural disasters. Students were especially interested in observing weather outside. Reading Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs will connect that interest with our first work on opinion writing. They enjoy listening to stories and can draw on the organization they used in their animal reports when they explain how they feel about the weather in this book.]", "44": "[FS1 came to the United States from an Assyrian background at age two. She has taken the ELPAC during each of her three years at this school. Last year's overall score was 1481, placing her at level 3. Listening and speaking were at level 4, while reading and writing were at level 3. These results point to stronger oral English and continued growth in literacy.]", "45": "b. prior academic knowledge related to the specific content you plan to teach", "46": "[FS1's weather-unit work shows that she understands the clouds, storms, and weather that appear in the read-aloud. Her animal report also shows experience organizing a paragraph and supporting its topic. I will help her apply those skills to an opinion. I do not yet know whether she recognizes the term opinion, so I will explain it in familiar language before asking her to use it.]", "47": "c. social identity (student self-concept derived from a perceived membership in a social group that is evident in classroom interactions)", "48": "[FS1 has two close classmates whom she sees on the playground and at playdates. She is conscientious about her work and consistently follows classroom routines. Her relationships and dependable participation give her a comfortable place in the class.]", "49": "d. cultural and linguistic resources and funds of knowledge", "50": "[FS1 uses English and Assyrian at home and attends an Assyrian-language school. She spends time with friends from that school on weekends. She is an only child. Through the Meet the Student activity and an individual conversation, I learned that she enjoys clay, drawing, the library, music, and learning new things. Read-aloud time is a particular favorite.]", "51": "e. prior experiences and interests related to the content you plan to teach", "52": "[FS1 often chooses to draw or write in her journal during free time. Together with her interest in read-alouds, these habits give her several reasons to engage with an opinion paragraph and its illustration.]", "56": "[FS2 has had hearing loss in his left ear since birth and uses a hearing aid in his right ear. He needs a front seat that places his right ear toward the teacher. My Master Teacher and I use microphones so he can hear instruction clearly.]", "58": "[FS2's animal report included an organized paragraph structure, researched details, transitions, and a closing sentence. His weather work also shows familiarity with the language this story uses. Those experiences should help him enter the lesson. Because I am unsure whether he knows the term opinion, I will give him time during the read-aloud to form a view and think of reasons before writing.]", "68": "[I will use the classroom microphone during instruction to complement FS2's right-ear hearing aid and support his access to the lesson.]", "70": "[FS3 has missed school frequently, and my Master Teacher told me that his father died last year. He often has difficulty sustaining attention, remaining still, and managing impulses. His mother has raised the possibility of ADHD but has not pursued a diagnosis; he has neither an IEP nor a 504 plan.", "71": "He needs frequent check-ins and redirection to finish activities without interrupting classmates. Since beginning tutoring, he has started completing and submitting homework. His records show relative strength in mathematics and continuing needs in reading and language arts. I will use that information to plan support without assuming a diagnosis.]", "72": "b. prior academic knowledge related to the specific content you plan to teach", "73": "[FS3 participated in the weather unit and completed an animal report. His science work suggests that he can follow the weather content in the story. His report needed stronger transitions and a conclusion, so I will pay particular attention to those elements as we develop opinion writing.]", "74": "c. social identity (student self-concept derived from a perceived membership in a social group that is evident in classroom interactions)", "75": "[FS3 is cheerful and often has a joke, story, or action to share. His impulsivity sometimes strains friendships. He especially enjoys one classmate's company, although that peer can become frustrated when FS3 has trouble settling or focusing.]", "76": "d. cultural and linguistic resources and funds of knowledge", "77": "[FS3 is an only child, is Caucasian, and speaks English as his first language. He was born in Hawaii and moved to XX two years ago. Conversation, drawing, comics, and finding books in the school library are important interests and ways he expresses himself.]", "78": "e. prior experiences and interests related to the content you plan to teach", "79": "[The opportunity to talk about an opinion and then write and draw fits FS3's interest in conversation and journal work. I expect those choices to help him engage with the lesson.]", "80": "f. developmental considerations (e.g., social-emotional, typical and atypical child/adolescent development)", "81": "[FS3 appears to be developing appropriately in some emotional areas, but he needs more support than many classmates with attention, movement, and work completion. Calling out or making inappropriate comments can interfere with peer relationships. I will keep those observed needs in mind when organizing participation and independent work.]", "92": "Content Standard(s) CA ELA Standards - Text Types and Purposes 1. Write opinion pieces on topics or texts, supporting a point of view with reasons. CA ELA Standards – Comprehension and Collaboration 1.b Follow agreed-upon rules for discussions (e.g., gaining the floor in respectful ways, listening to others with care, speaking one at a time about the topics and texts under discussion). CA ELA Standards – Comprehension and Collaboration 1.d – Explain their own ideas and understanding in light of the discussion. ELD Standard(s)", "93": "[ELD Standards for Grade 3: A. Collaborative 1. Exchanging information and ideas with others through oral collaborative discussions on a range of social and academic topics. B. Interpretive 5. Listening actively to spoken English in a range of social and academic contexts C. Productive 11. Supporting own opinions and evaluating other’s opinions in speaking and writing.]", "95": "[Students will listen to Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs and follow our discussion routines. At three pauses, they will consider life in Chewandswallow, decide how they would feel about its weather, and explain their view to a partner. They will then write three to five sentences giving their opinion and at least one supporting reason. Their illustration will show a day when a food of their choice falls from the sky.]", "101": "[By the end of the lesson, students should be able to state a view and give a reason for it in a short piece of writing. With support, they should begin using the same approach when responding to another topic.]", "103": "[I will first find out what students know about opinion. Then I will ask whether people can have different opinions about the same thing. Their explanations will show me where I need to clarify the concept before they begin writing.]", "105": "[Students will show their understanding in partner conversations, whole-class sharing, and an individual paragraph. I will help them move from stating a preference to explaining why they hold it, then look for both elements in the writing.]", "107": "[I will listen during the read-aloud and partner talk, ask follow-up questions, and circulate as students write. Those checks will help me respond while learning is taking place. Afterward, I will review the completed paragraphs for the stated opinion, supporting reason, and other lesson expectations, sorting the work into below, meeting, or exceeding expectations.]", "109": "[Students will gather on the carpet for the story. At three planned points, they will think about living in Chewandswallow, find a partner, and discuss their opinions. We will hear several ideas as a class before returning to the reading. This oral rehearsal will prepare students to write independently on the handout. Once the writing is complete, we will return to the carpet to share and consider how opinions apply to other subjects.]", "110": "How will you group students and manage group work to support student learning?", "111": "[I will use my Master Teacher's carpet arrangement, which reflects how students work together and puts the focus students near instruction. During partner talk, I will check that everyone has someone to talk with and a chance to contribute. For writing, students may choose flexible seating. I will monitor those choices and separate combinations that make concentration difficult.]", "112": "Instruction to Support Learning What instructional strategies will support student learning through multiple modalities?", "113": "[The read-aloud and its illustrations will let students hear and see the story. I will also display a model paragraph and sentence openings on the classroom TVs. Discussion will give students a chance to rehearse language, while changing partners will build movement into the lesson. Drawing will provide another way to represent an idea. Flexible seating will help students find workable writing spaces. Across these activities, I will move from explicit instruction and shared practice toward independent writing.]", "114": "What resources, materials, and/or educational technology will you or your students use during the lesson?", "115": "[I will use Judi Barrett's Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs, my opinion-writing handout, a model paragraph, and displayed sentence starters. The handout begins with a prompt about living in Chewandswallow and includes a space to illustrate a chosen food falling from the sky. The Apple TVs will display the writing supports, and my microphone necklace will make the spoken instruction easier to hear.]", "116": "What adaptations and accommodations, including, as appropriate, assistive technologies, will support individual student learning needs beyond the UDL supports built into the lesson?", "117": "[I will check seating choices, confer with the three focus students, and provide feedback tied to the writing criteria. The model and sentence starters will be available to everyone, with particular support for students who need help beginning. If time and FS3's progress require an adjustment, he may explain his opinion and reasons orally. A fidget or privacy screen will also be available to support his concentration.]", "121": "[The month-long informational-writing unit required students to introduce an animal, group related information, provide facts, connect ideas, and conclude. Their reports and my Master Teacher's grading show that 86% met the standard. The remaining students are continuing to revise with small-group support. Opinion writing will draw on the same organizational skills while changing the purpose: students will now state a position and support it with reasons.]", "125": "[Partner talk will give students repeated chances to form, explain, and reconsider an opinion. As they decide whether they would want to live in Chewandswallow, they must interpret events in the story and use those events to support a view. Hearing classmates' ideas will let us compare perspectives and clarify misunderstandings. In the closing discussion, I will ask students to apply the idea of opinion to a new topic, extending the learning beyond the book.]", "133": "[Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs builds on a topic students enjoyed and is appropriate for a third-grade read-aloud. The handout's opening phrase gives them a clear starting point for writing about Chewandswallow. Lined space on half the page keeps the task manageable, and the illustration space gives them another way to represent the idea. The displayed example and sentence starters will make the writing expectations visible.]", "135": "[The initial thumbs check will tell me how much explanation the word opinion needs. Listening to partner conversations and class sharing will show whether students can state a view and support it. The completed paragraphs will provide individual evidence. Sorting them by the lesson criteria will help me distinguish students ready to move on from those who need another explanation or targeted practice.]", "139": "[FS1's stronger oral-language results suggest that discussion can prepare her for the harder writing task. I will provide the handout's opening phrase, a paragraph model, and sentence frames that include transitions. I will read the supports aloud before displaying them. Individual check-ins and feedback tied to the criteria will help her see what is working and what still needs attention. I have shared the plan and materials with her resource teacher and will report how she performs after the lesson.]", "141": "[FS2 will sit near me with a clear view of the illustrations and my face. I will wear the microphone necklace to support his hearing. Listening during partner talk and checking his writing will help me assess his understanding directly. Any feedback or additional guidance will address the opinion-writing goals while maintaining access to the spoken lesson.]", "143": "[FS3 will sit close enough for quiet redirection. Because sitting for more than about ten minutes is difficult for him, the three partner activities will include movement. He may also choose a suitable writing space, including the beanbag or library area. I will check his progress, support transitions and a closing sentence, and give specific feedback. If distractions interfere, I will offer a privacy folder and adjust his workspace.]", "154": "Annotation Text: I remind a student to check his seating position. This establishes the carpet routine: crossed legs, an appropriate sitting position, and attention on me.", "157": "Annotation Text: I connect the new writing task with the animal reports students have already completed. Beginning with familiar work helps them understand the lesson's expectations and gives them an established experience to draw on.", "160": "Annotation Text: I ask students to signal with a thumb if they know what an opinion is. Most indicate that they do, following our earlier read-aloud discussion. Their responses lead me to give a brief review of the term before explaining the writing task.", "163": "Annotation Text: After identifying my own opinion, I ask whether students may disagree with it. They must consider the difference between their judgment and mine and recognize that another opinion is possible.", "166": "Annotation Text: I give students time to decide whether they would want to live in Chewandswallow before speaking to a partner. They use information from the book to evaluate the town and form a view they can explain aloud.", "167": "Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:00:38–0:00:48", "168": "Annotation Title: Setting Expectations for Learning; Creating a Positive Learning Environment; Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking", "169": "Annotation Text: When I notice a student is not participating, I direct him to the classmate beside him. This gives him an immediate partner and a clear way to join the discussion about living in Chewandswallow.", "170": "Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:00:56–0:01:50", "171": "Annotation Title: Monitoring for Student Understanding; Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking", "172": "Annotation Text: I ask students to report ideas from their partner conversations. Their explanations let me check understanding, acknowledge their views, and help them clarify what they mean.", "173": "Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:01:27–0:01:45", "174": "Annotation Title: Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking", "175": "Annotation Text: I invite students with another viewpoint to contribute. Hearing different responses helps the class recognize that people can form distinct opinions about the same situation.", "181": "Annotation Text: I revisit the learning goal and ask students to recall the name of the writing we practiced. Having the class say opinion together reinforces the term and checks their connection to the lesson.", "184": "Annotation Text: I ask students to consider other subjects they could have opinions about. This extends the concept beyond the book and asks them to apply it in another setting.", "191": "[The information in Step 1 helped me connect new learning to work students had already done. Their animal reports provided a foundation for paragraph organization, while the weather unit and their enjoyment of read-alouds guided my choice of book. Their interest in working with peers led me to include repeated think-pair-share opportunities. Together, those decisions made the move into opinion writing familiar and manageable.]", "192": "b. support student access to and engagement with the content?", "193": "[The gradual move from listening and discussion to independent writing gave students time to work with the new concept before recording it. Reading aloud made the story available to students with different reading abilities. Partner exchanges let them rehearse ideas, and the displayed model and sentence starters supported the language demands of writing. These choices followed the needs and interests described in Step 1.]", "195": "[The plan gave me enough room to respond during teaching. Partner conversations and class responses showed where an idea needed clarification. Circulating during writing let me ask guiding questions, scaffold a response, or adjust a student's working conditions. The closing discussion also gave me time to address points that arose in individual conferences. I could make these changes while keeping the lesson focused on its original goals.]", "197": "[FS1 used her stronger speaking and listening skills to develop an opinion before writing. I read the model and sentence starters aloud, displayed them, and checked her progress. Her finished paragraph used three of those starters, showing how the language support helped her complete the task.", "198": "FS2's seating and the microphone supported access to the story and directions. Listening to his partner discussion helped me check his understanding. Those planned accommodations allowed him to participate in the same opinion-writing work as his classmates.", "199": "FS3 needed additional support during instruction and independent work. Near the end of the directions, he was lying on his back. Later, when his table became distracting, I moved him to a less crowded space. His paper was still blank at my next check-in, so I helped him explain his opinion orally and use the displayed sentence starters to begin writing. The planned supports and those immediate adjustments helped him finish the opinion task.]", "203": "[The completed work showed 15 students meeting expectations, six exceeding them, and two below them. Thus, 21 of the 23 demonstrated the lesson goals. I would move the class forward while meeting individually with the two students whose paragraphs stated an opinion without a reason. I would ask each to explain the thinking orally, clarify as needed, and add a supporting detail to the paragraph.]", "205": "[Students are ready to build a more fully developed opinion piece under W.3.1. That means introducing the topic and position, organizing reasons, connecting them with linking language, and providing a conclusion. I would use Writer's Workshop because students respond well to collaboration. Short lessons, purposeful grouping, peer review, revision, and teacher conferences would address one part at a time. An early workshop could focus on the introduction and opinion; the next could develop reasons and connections. Students would gradually assemble a piece that meets the full standard.]", "207": "[I would keep the read-aloud and partner discussions. Reviewing the recording showed me several changes that would improve access and clarity. I would project the detailed illustrations so students at the back could see them, and add a word bank for story and weather vocabulary that students misspelled.", "208": "I would also state and display the three-to-five-sentence expectation. I said several sentences during teaching, and most students wrote only two: an opinion and a reason. Having students repeat the requirement and highlighting it beside the model would make the task clearer. Finally, I would build an opinion anchor chart with the definition and examples students contribute, giving them a visual reference as well as oral practice.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"13": "[This is a third-grade class of students who have mastered the CCSS for third grade of writing informative/explanatory texts to examine a topic and convey ideas and information clearly. The students were required to research and write a five-paragraph essay on an animal of their choice. The content I plan to teach will introduce the students to the next state standard that the class will be focusing on during Language Arts: CCSS.ELA-Literacy.W.3.1: Write opinion pieces on topics or texts, supporting a point of view with reasons. Students have the prior academic knowledge necessary to write paragraphs using topic sentences, linking words and phrases, including details to support their topic, and concluding statements. These elements will continue to be focused on during the Opinion Writing unit.]", "503": "In my Step 1, “Lesson Plan”, I specified the length requirement for the opinion paragraph to be 3-5 sentences. In my Step 2, “Teach and Assess”, I saw that I did not explicitly state the required paragraph length, but instead said “several sentences”. I should have made my directions for the assignment match my Lesson Plan and had students choral back to me the length requirement. Several students wrote 3 or more sentences, but the majority of students only wrote two sentences. One sentence for their opinion and one sentence for their supporting reason. If I were to reteach this lesson, I would make sure to be more specific in my directions and then provide students with a visual of the learning goal. For example, on my sentence starter sheet, I could have included the directions with the length requirements and highlighted or circled the most important parts of the directions. This way the directions would be visible on the classroom TV’s for students to refer to during completion of the activity."}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}, "ms-literacy": {"id": "ms-literacy", "lens": "ms-literacy", "thesis": "This case offers a useful progression from modeled morphology to independent word study and connected text. Its weaknesses are not solved by cleaner prose: several standard labels remain wrong, teacher help during the summative task complicates independence, and the retained materials do not include the completed focus-student sheets described in the narrative.", "version": "The source is a 2025 V1 MS Literacy packet with four lessons, the fourth functioning as follow-up. The full rubric comparison uses the saved 2026–27 MS Literacy V2.1 guide. V1 descriptors were not verified. Current guidance uses a three-lesson unit plus follow-up; the older four-lesson presentation is not silently treated as a current template. Scores are current-guide instructional comparisons, not original-year scores.", "standards": [["RF.3.3a", "Know common prefixes and derivational suffixes and their meanings.", "The affix charts and word construction directly address this demand. Inflectional -ing/-ed should not be presented as if all endings are derivational.", "Ask the learner to identify the part and explain its contribution on a new example.", "Direct alignment; distinguish types and functions of endings.", "ela", 25], ["RF.3.3b and RF.3.3c", "Decode words with common Latin suffixes and multisyllabic words.", "Building and defining words offers useful support, but the written tasks do not directly record accurate reading.", "Include an actual reading attempt on an unfamiliar word and a record of the strategy and support.", "Decoding evidence is incomplete.", "ela", 25], ["L.3.2e", "Use conventional spelling and spelling patterns for studied words and adding endings.", "The source paraphrase drops the connection between studied words and spelling patterns. Tract plus -er does not produce tractor; the example uses -or. Penniless also requires attention to spelling.", "Assess the resulting spelling, the boundary decision, and an explanation of any change.", "Relevant but instructional examples need precision.", "ela", 43], ["L.3.4a, L.3.4b, L.3.4c", "Use sentence context; use known affixes; use a known root as clues to meaning.", "¶91 and ¶116 mislabel .4a as affix meaning, duplicating .4b. Context becomes explicit in the park passage.", "Score a word-part inference and a context-based check separately. Do not credit .4a from isolated affix matching.", "Correct .4a wording and show its actual task connection.", "ela", 44], ["RF.3.4a; RL.3.1", "Read for purpose/understanding; answer questions with explicit text evidence.", "Lesson 4 adds a passage and questions. Teacher fluent reading is a model, not evidence of the learner’s fluency.", "Use the learner’s rereading and an answer linked to a specific passage detail.", "Suitable extension; independent fluency and comprehension remain unverified.", "ela", [25, 19]], ["SL.3.1", "Discuss Grade 3 topics/texts, building on others and explaining ideas.", "Partner talk provides an opportunity; a list of volunteered words does not establish responsive discussion.", "Record how a student responds to or revises an explanation after a peer’s contribution.", "Planned; limited recorded evidence.", "ela", 35], ["ELD Grade 3 PI.1 and PI.5", "Exchange ideas and demonstrate active listening through relevant questions/responses.", "The lesson names the strands but not proficiency-specific descriptors. The focus student is described as Expanding.", "Identify the grade/proficiency demand and record an explanation or question with the level of support.", "Relevant language functions; descriptor details incomplete.", "eld", [67, 68]], ["ELD Grade 3 productive writing strand", "Write explanations using appropriate language and organization.", "Lessons 3–4 cite a general productive statement without a numbered proficiency-specific standard. Written products do not measure all collaborative/listening goals.", "Select a precise writing descriptor and define criteria for meaning, sentence completeness and language support separately from artwork.", "Language goal present; precise standard and assessment mapping need repair.", "eld", 69]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Answer the primary-language question with primary-language evidence", [18, 20, 24, 29, 32, 33, 35, 38, 41], "The focus student’s Spanish, willingness to speak, and interests in soccer and science are credible resources for the plan. But the response to the primary-language literacy question describes progress in English. It therefore leaves the requested Spanish literacy evidence unresolved. Do not infer Spanish reading skill merely because the student speaks Spanish or recognizes some cognates. The mentor teacher’s role is also named without specifying who models, observes or records evidence. These omissions matter because the analysis depends on knowing what the learner can do, in which language, and under whose prompting.", "Add actual primary-language information if available, or state the limit. Assign observation and teaching roles so the final record identifies the support behind each performance."], ["sequence", "B", "Separate the three-lesson unit from its follow-up", [46, 47, 50, 53, 60], "The sequence has a defensible progression: manipulate and read words, explain additional affixes with support, apply the strategy independently, then use context in a passage. The fourth lesson is also described as a response to the third lesson’s assessment. That makes it conceptually a follow-up, even though it appears in the older learning-segment template. Preserve that chronology rather than suggesting the later assessment findings were known before instruction. Current candidates need to organize their own three-lesson unit and follow-up under the current directions, not copy this older structure. The progression should be evaluated by the changing task demand and amount of support, not by the number of activities.", "Make the chronology explicit: what was planned initially, what the assessment revealed, and which later decision changed the follow-up."], ["lesson1", "B", "Tract word building: precision matters in explicit instruction", [66, 68, 76, 77, 79, 81, 83, 85], "The tractor image and pocket chart can link a bound Latin root with meaning. However, the suffix list contains -er while tractor uses -or. The teacher should not imply that any listed affix can be combined mechanically with tract to create a conventional word. Distinguish a valid word, a spelling change, an inflectional ending and a derivational ending. The planned think-aloud is stronger when it includes pronunciation and a sentence that checks meaning, rather than segmentation alone. A fluent peer can provide rehearsal, but can also supply the answer; the focus student needs an individually attributable turn.", "Model one accurate combination completely, then use a new combination that the learner must read and explain. Record whether the chart or partner supplied any part."], ["lesson2", "B", "From affix definition to whole-word meaning", [91, 93, 101, 102, 104, 106, 108, 110], "The pre-, re-, -ful and -less examples support a repeatable analysis routine. The important distinction is between knowing re- means again and explaining what it means to redo a specific action. Word meanings are not always predictable by adding dictionary fragments. Worthful, contributed in the commentary, is uncommon; the teacher should distinguish a plausible construction from conventional usage, perhaps checking a reference and comparing it with familiar alternatives. Some contributed examples also describe sensitive social conditions. Discuss word structure respectfully without turning a term such as homeless into a joke or a presumed description of a classmate.", "Ask for the base meaning, the affix contribution, a complete definition, and a sentence that makes the intended meaning clear. Verify unfamiliar proposed words rather than automatically validating every combination."], ["lesson3", "B", "The summative task needs a clear boundary around assistance", [118, 124, 126, 128, 130, 134], "The six-component word-study task samples identification, definition, sentence use, a visual and additional examples. Modeling with unhappy and careful can clarify directions, provided students are assessed on different words. The commentary describes teacher questions during the supposedly independent task, including help with disappear and penniless. Such feedback is educationally useful, but the resulting work is not equivalent to an unprompted first attempt. Preserve both stages or annotate the support. A high final rating may reflect successful supported learning; it should not silently become evidence of independent transfer.", "Define neutral directions versus content prompts. Record the first response, the prompt, and the revision, and use a new word for the independent check."], ["lesson4", "B", "Connected text adds a real transfer demand", [140, 142, 148, 150, 152, 154, 156, 158], "A Day at the Park can extend isolated word study by requiring students to combine morphology with a story’s meaning. The teacher’s fluent model supports access, but the learning evidence comes from the learner’s rereading, word selection and explanation. Highlighting an affix only shows recognition. A response about recheck should explain what is checked again and why that fits the scene; a response about disorganized should use both word structure and contextual details. Whole-group answers can hide who still needs a frame or a prompt, so the focus student needs a traceable response.", "Collect one independent word-in-context explanation after discussion. Ask the learner to identify the contextual clue that confirms or changes the initial word-part inference."], ["assessment", "C", "The classroom rubric measures six components, not all cited standards", [176, 178, 183, 185, 186, 190], "The retained rubric allocates 0–3 points to six criteria, for 18 total: word selection, part identification, meaning, sentence, visual, and additional examples. Its bands are 15–18 Excellent, 11–14 Proficient, 6–10 Developing and 0–5 Needs Support. These are classroom bands, not CalTPA levels. Word selection’s top two descriptors are very similar, weakening reliable discrimination. Artwork can demonstrate meaning, but drawing detail should not outweigh the semantic understanding. The written form does not directly assess oral decoding, listening or collaborative discussion. Those goals require separate observations rather than an assumption that a high /18 total covers everything.", "Clarify adjacent descriptors, score the meaning communicated by a visual rather than artistic polish, and retain separate evidence for reading and discussion."], ["teaching", "F", "Use the actual error to explain the adjustment", [203, 204, 210, 218, 221, 226, 227], "The account contains useful error-specific episodes: a learner cannot recall sub-, another needs to distinguish appear from disappear, and several learners misunderstand the lower worksheet section. Returning the class to a delightful model responds to a task-interpretation barrier. The analysis should ask whether the problem was unclear directions, missing vocabulary, or conceptual misunderstanding; each implies a different revision. Re-modeling all steps may help, but the account needs the subsequent response to show what changed. Because no teaching clips are supplied, the review can assess the written reasoning but not verify demonstration-based rubric performance.", "For one episode, present the initial response, the diagnostic interpretation, the targeted change and the student’s next attempt. Do not substitute a list of timestamps for that chain."], ["feedback", "H", "Repaint and colorful: preserve meaning while refining register", [233, 234, 235, 236, 237], "The reported repaint sentence communicates the intended meaning, and replacing cause with because is a reasonable written-register revision. It should not be treated as evidence that the student lacked morphological understanding. The reported rainbow sentence and tearful/careful/fearful examples provide a different kind of evidence: conventional use and transfer of a suffix across bases. The feedback is stronger when it states which criterion each feature satisfies and what remains to revise. However, the retained packet contains blank prefix/suffix sheets and the rubric, not the completed focus-student sheets. The quoted examples remain reported student evidence and cannot support an independently verified /18 rescore.", "Show the completed work and criterion marks if available. Separate semantic success, written register and independence in the feedback."], ["reflection", "I", "Reconcile high task performance with the continuing-needs narrative", [244, 247, 249, 252, 254, 257, 259], "The group reflection reports high ratings without counts or a distribution. The focus-student reflection lists broad ongoing difficulties even though the feedback describes strong performance on the selected tasks. These statements can coexist if the task is familiar or heavily supported, but the analysis must explain that relationship. The lower worksheet confusion also suggests a design problem in the instructions, not necessarily a deficit in the learners. Strong reflection would identify which supports were helpful, which obscured independence, and which conclusion is limited to the two selected words. A polished claim that goals were met is weaker than a bounded conclusion linked to the actual product.", "Report criterion-level results, support conditions and the limits of generalization. Connect the planned improvement to the precise problem observed, such as task directions or whole-word synthesis."], ["followup", "J", "Justify the five-student extension and its success criterion", [266, 268, 270, 271, 273, 276, 278, 282], "The extension has a clear purpose: move from isolated affixes to contextual interpretation. Selecting five students, including the focus student, could be appropriate, but the source gives no criterion-level selection rule. A total score alone can conceal a decoding or explanation weakness. Identify the component that is sufficiently secure and the new demand the passage adds. Soccer and park experiences can provide entry, but should not be assumed universal. The most informative check is a new contextual explanation after support, not simply successful participation during the shared reading.", "State the selection criterion and record an individual explanation using both a word part and text evidence. Use that response to decide whether further extension or targeted practice is needed."], ["followup-evidence", "L", "Prompted text reference is not yet independent transfer", [295, 296, 297, 298], "The recheck and disorganized episodes show the intended move toward evidence from text. The latter learner initially gives a broad explanation, then receives a frame and teacher-recorded model. That sequence may support learning but does not establish what the learner can subsequently do alone. The commentary should distinguish the student’s words from the teacher’s refined answer. It also needs to identify the focus student’s actual contribution rather than describing children only by clothing. The actual video is absent, so no demonstrated follow-up level is assigned.", "After the modeled discussion, use a different word and ask the focus student to explain it independently with the same access accommodations. Preserve the response and support record."]], "comparison": [[726, 235, "The rewrite correctly retains the student’s meaningful repaint sentence and frames because as a precision revision. It still needs the completed sheet to confirm the classroom rating and the amount of assistance."], [587, 226, "The rewrite clarifies that the teacher changed instruction after students misunderstood the worksheet. That is stronger causal reasoning about the teaching decision, but no post-adjustment student response is supplied."]], "illustration": "Illustrative revision: “The reported repaint sentence shows the meaning of re- and paint in context. My register feedback would refine cause to because without discounting that semantic success. To judge independent transfer, I would retain the original attempt and ask for a different affixed word after the feedback, recording any chart or teacher support.”", "rubrics": [[1, "2", ["Partial — ¶49 names sources but gives no specific recent response or count.", "Partial — L.3.4a is mislabeled and ELD descriptors are incomplete.", "Supported — word recognition/morphology and language development are connected.", "Supported in purpose — oral/written explanations support literacy; precise proficiency alignment is missing.", "Supported — each lesson contains a check.", "Partial — the written task does not measure every stated reading/discussion goal.", "Partial — the /18 rubric has weak adjacent word-selection descriptors and no distinct oral evidence.", "Partial — -er/-or and inflectional/derivational distinctions require attention."], "The sequence has substance, but standard accuracy and assessment coverage prevent the complete Level 3 comparison.", "A 1 would overlook the connected design. Multiple formative checks cannot carry the rubric to 4 while these base constructs remain incomplete."], [2, "3", ["Supported — ¶74, ¶99 and ¶124 invite familiar vocabulary and language resources.", "Supported — charts, cards, models and oral rehearsal provide access.", "Supported as transfer — Lessons 1–3 form a sequence; Lesson 4 is identified separately as follow-up.", "Supported — direct models move toward guided and individual application.", "Supported — explaining meaning and sentence use engage language development.", "Supported — talk and writing are integrated with word analysis.", "Supported — ¶85, ¶110 and ¶134 describe focus-student supports."], "The instructional sequence is clear when the fourth lesson is recognized as follow-up rather than silently copied into the current unit format.", "The plan is more specific than 2. A firm 4 needs identifiable incorporation of the learner’s language/experience into particular examples, not only an invitation to make connections."], [3, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — ¶203–205 describes modeling and guided construction.", "Unverified — actual student engagement cannot be inspected.", "Partial — the description corresponds to Lesson 1."], "The description identifies what a reviewer should look for, but the teaching demonstration is absent.", "The precise think-aloud addition at 4 cannot be verified from narrative alone, especially where affix accuracy requires checking."], [4, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — volunteered familiar words are described, but actual use of assets is unverified.", "Partial — the discussion suggests language-development engagement without the clips."], "The written account gives relevant examples but cannot establish demonstrated additional-theme instruction.", "Do not award 4 merely because students volunteer words; the intentional connection to resources or experiences must be visible and meaningful."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — visual charts, manipulation and oral practice are narrated.", "Partial — vocabulary explanation is described but actual language use and support are unverified."], "The design integrates language with content, but the required demonstration is unavailable.", "Multiple UDL strategies can support 4 only after the base teaching performance is established."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — ¶218 describes checking individual word analysis.", "Partial — ¶226 describes re-modeling after worksheet confusion, without the subsequent student response."], "The teacher’s decision is understandable; the observable assessment-adjustment sequence cannot be scored from the supplied record.", "Specific feedback is promising, but a 3 or 4 needs demonstrated monitoring and intentional response, not just a narrated correction."], [7, "2", ["Partial — group results lack counts and completed focus-student sheets are absent.", "Partial — broad continuing needs are not reconciled with reported high performance.", "Supported as reported feedback — ¶234–236 names repaint, colorful and a specific revision.", "Partial — effectiveness is asserted without distinguishing prompted from independent work.", "Supported — ¶249 and ¶259 identify better modeling and oral rehearsal."], "The feedback is more specific than the assessment analysis. The full Level 3 comparison remains limited by the missing products and unreconciled claims.", "A 1 would disregard concrete reported examples. A 3 requires criterion-level evidence; 4 needs feedback anchored in verified examples rather than presumed top ratings."], [8, "Insufficient evidence", ["Supported as planning — the passage adds contextual interpretation.", "Unverified — no follow-up video; the account includes teacher-supplied modeling.", "Partial — the five-student selection rests on broadly reported success."], "The extension is pedagogically coherent, but its demonstrated effect and the focus student’s independent response cannot be scored.", "Text familiarity and soccer interests do not establish 4 unless they are intentionally used in the observed follow-up and the base constructs are met."]], "title": "Grade 3 roots and affixes", "file": "03_MS_Literacy_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 MS LPA Sample Submission.docx", "quotes": {"18": "[Five students are identified English learners. Two are at Bridging and need occasional language support as they approach grade-level proficiency. One is at Expanding and needs focused support with academic language. Two are at Emerging; visuals, demonstrations, and structured speaking opportunities help them participate in grade-level instruction.]", "20": "[One student with Autism Spectrum Disorder has a 504 plan. A second student is being evaluated for ASD and has no formal diagnosis at this point. Three students work outside the classroom with an intervention specialist on phonics.]", "24": "[My mentor teacher will teach alongside me.]", "29": "[The focus student is an English learner at Expanding whose primary language is Spanish. He uses his knowledge of Spanish as he develops English literacy. He readily asks questions and shares ideas, showing confidence in classroom conversation. Soccer is an interest, and he connects teamwork on the field with working together in class. Books, science, and adventure also draw him into reading and new vocabulary.", "32": "[He has made steady progress in English reading and writing this year. His reading of grade-level texts is becoming more fluent, and he answers comprehension questions in both small and whole groups. He identifies central ideas and details, summarizes narratives, and connects texts. He is also beginning to use roots and affixes when working out unfamiliar vocabulary.", "33": "He plans with story maps and graphic organizers and writes short, organized paragraphs using complete sentences and grade-appropriate conventions. In discussions, he explains his reasoning and contributes ideas that help classmates learn.]", "35": "[He reads grade-level narrative and informational texts with increasing fluency and understands main ideas, important details, and sequence. With appropriate support, he answers questions orally and in writing. He is learning to identify bases and affixes and use them to decode words and explain meaning. His explanations use simple academic language, though he does not yet connect each affix to its base consistently.", "38": "[He needs continued explicit practice breaking unfamiliar words into meaningful parts and putting those meanings together. Complex vocabulary and directions with several steps sometimes require clarification. He also needs help arranging ideas into connected sentences and paragraphs and using new words accurately with appropriate grammar.", "41": "[As an English learner at Expanding, he contributes willingly to class activities and reads aloud with growing fluency. His next steps include decoding unfamiliar affixed words, using academic vocabulary precisely, and expanding and organizing written sentences. He benefits from practice explaining ideas in complete sentences and from support with multistep directions and independent strategy use.", "46": "[This four-lesson sequence extends earlier work with single-syllable words and basic affixes into multisyllabic word analysis. Phonics and word recognition remain the foundational focus; language development runs through the sequence as students explain meanings aloud and in writing. Integrated ELD support is included in each lesson.", "47": "Lesson 1 uses tract to introduce how a root combines with prefixes and suffixes. I model with a pocket chart before students build words with support. Lesson 2 expands the work to pre-, re-, -ful, and -less through class discussion and guided word study. Lesson 3 asks students to apply their learning independently with pre-, re-, dis-, un-, -ing, -less, -ful, and -able. Lesson 4 extends the work into a passage, where students use both word parts and context to understand vocabulary. The sequence moves from demonstration to supported practice, independent application, and use in connected text.]", "49": "[Earlier observations, vocabulary tasks, writing samples, and formative checks showed that students could read grade-level material but differed in how well they used affixes to determine meaning. Some recognized roots only tentatively; others decoded and explained words more confidently.", "50": "Lesson 1 therefore makes the structure of tract words explicit. Lesson 2 gives students supported practice with additional affixes and oral explanations. Lesson 3 checks whether they can identify parts, spell words, explain meanings, and use vocabulary on their own. Lesson 4 addresses the difficulty some students had transferring word analysis into comprehension. Across the sequence, assessment findings guide the amount of modeling, language support, and independent work.]", "53": "In Lesson 1, I demonstrate word building before students manipulate parts and discuss their meanings. Lesson 2 carries that work into print through worksheets, definitions, and sentences. Students connect recognizable affixes with accurate reading and spelling. In Lesson 3, they choose words, identify each part, explain the whole, write sentences, draw representations, and generate further examples. This independent task shows whether they can transfer the strategy to other words and communicate their understanding.]", "60": "[The lessons build on one another by changing how much support students receive and where they apply the strategy. Lesson 1 makes word structure visible through tract, a pocket chart, and guided word building. Lesson 2 introduces additional affixes and uses a shared recording sheet, discussion, and guided worksheets to strengthen meaning-making. Lesson 3 asks students to analyze and use words independently. Lesson 4 places those skills in a story, requiring students to combine word-part knowledge with context. Each lesson uses the same underlying process while increasing the demands on students' reading and language.]", "66": "[CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of the most common prefixes and derivational suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.3.B: Decode words with common Latin suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.3.C: Decode multisyllable words. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.2.E: Use conventional spelling for high-frequency and other studied words for adding suffixes to base words (e.g., sitting, smiled, cried, happiness).]", "68": "[Students will build and read multisyllabic words from tract, the prefixes re-, dis-, sub-, and de-, and the suffixes -ed, -ing, -ion, and -er. They will explain the contribution each affix makes to the word's meaning.]", "74": "[I will connect word study to students' home languages, community experiences, and prior learning. Subtract, distract, and tractor are examples they may know from mathematics, conversation, or daily life. Starting with familiar words gives them a concrete way to discuss a root and its affixes. Students can contribute their own connections and use what they already know to approach the new vocabulary.]", "76": "[I will introduce tract with a tractor picture and explain its meaning: to pull, drag, or draw. I will then show how adding a suffix produces tractor and explain the roles of prefixes and suffixes. At the pocket chart, I will combine tract with re-, dis-, sub-, de-, -ed, -ing, -ion, and -er, saying the parts, spelling the resulting word, and explaining its meaning.", "77": "Pairs of volunteers will then build words at the chart with my guidance. In small groups, students will use flashcard kits to form words with the same parts and record them on a sheet. Their recorded words and conversations will show how they are applying the strategy.]", "79": "[I will model one step at a time: identify tract, explain its meaning with the picture, add an affix, read and spell the new word, and use it in a sentence. Students will then build words in pairs at the pocket chart while I ask about the affix and the resulting meaning. In small groups, they will repeat the process with flashcards, record the parts, and explain their choices aloud. Seeing, handling, reading, and discussing the parts will connect decoding with meaning.]", "81": "[During guided work, I will observe pairs at the pocket chart and ask them to identify the affix, explain its effect, and use the word in a sentence. I can respond immediately to confusion about spelling, pronunciation, or meaning. During group work, I will listen to explanations and review the recording sheets for accurate roots, prefixes, suffixes, and spelling. These checks will show who is ready to apply the strategy and who needs another model.]", "83": "[An anchor chart will define bases, prefixes, and suffixes, and the tractor image will make tract's meaning concrete. I will pronounce and explain each part as I model word building. Students will then use the same vocabulary in guided discussion and small-group work. The chart and picture will remain available as they explain words, check spelling, and complete their recording sheets. This gives English learners a consistent link between spoken language, print, and meaning.]", "85": "[I will provide the focus student with individual guidance at the pocket chart so he can identify each part and explain the resulting word. During small-group practice, he will work with a peer performing at or above grade level. The definition chart and tract picture will remain available for checking meaning and spelling. These supports build on his prior knowledge and willingness to participate while making word analysis more manageable.]", "91": "[CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.R.F.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of most common prefixes and derivational suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.A: Determine the meaning of a word formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.B: Determine the meaning of new words formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.C: Use a known root word as a clue to the meaning of an unknown word with the same root word.]", "93": "[build words with pre-, re-, -ful, and -less, contribute examples in class discussion, and explain word meanings on a guided word-study sheet.]", "99": "[I will invite students to offer words they know from home, school, and community life. Preorder may connect to a family's shopping, while helpful may describe someone they know. Partners and groups will discuss these connections and explain meanings to each other. I will also draw on familiar routines, hobbies, and community experiences. An anchor chart will help students connect the words they contribute with their structure and meaning.]", "101": "[I will introduce pre- as before, re- as again, -ful as full of, and -less as without. On the anchor chart, I will write examples such as preorder, prearrange, redo, refresh, careful, helpful, fearless, and hopeless and model sentences that show their meanings.", "102": "The class will generate more examples. I will ask students to explain the affix, identify the base's meaning, and use the whole word in a sentence. Students will then complete two guided worksheets, one for prefixes and one for suffixes, choosing words from the banks and writing their own definitions. The anchor chart will remain available for spelling and meaning.]", "104": "[I will explicitly name each target affix and demonstrate its meaning through a familiar word and a complete sentence. My think-aloud will connect the base, the affix, and the whole word. During class practice, students will propose examples and explain their reasoning with guiding questions. They will then work with a partner on the prefix and suffix sheets, using the word banks and chart to write definitions. The repeated sequence makes the analysis visible before students try it themselves.]", "106": "[The class discussion will let me hear whether students understand the target affixes and can apply them to new examples. I will ask about the affix, the base, and a sentence using the word, then clarify errors as they arise. During worksheet practice, I will review spelling and definitions and listen to partners' explanations. Together, the oral and written evidence will guide further support or a more demanding task.]", "108": "[I will consistently model the terms prefix, suffix, base word, and meaning and connect them to examples on the chart. Class discussion will give students practice explaining how an affix changes a word. The worksheets will extend that language into written definitions. English learners will work with peers reading at or above grade level so they can rehearse explanations and learn from a partner's language as they complete the task.]", "110": "[The focus student will have an anchor chart, modeled examples, and clear steps for connecting affixes with meaning. I will check his understanding during both guided and individual work. I will invite him to draw on familiar words, including words from his home language, and connect examples to his experiences. Partner talk will give him a supported place to practice the academic language before writing.]", "116": "[CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.R.F.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of most common prefixes and derivational suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.A: Determine the meaning of a word formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.B: Determine the meaning of new words formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.C: Use a known root word as a clue to the meaning of an unknown word with the same root word.]", "118": "[choose a word, identify and explain its base and affix, show its meaning in a sentence and picture, and produce other words with that affix.]", "124": "[Students will use the words and shared recording sheet from Lesson 2 as a starting point. The new banks include pre-, re-, dis-, un-, -ing, -less, -ful, and -able. I will encourage connections to home-language words and cognates. Students may use sports, games, family experiences, or hobbies in their sentences and pictures so that the vocabulary connects with something meaningful to them.]", "126": "[Students will complete two word-study sheets as the summative task. I will first model the process with unhappy for prefixes and careful for suffixes: identify the parts, explain the word, write a sentence, and draw its meaning. Students will then work individually with words from the banks. They will analyze each chosen word, provide a sentence and visual, and generate further words using the same affix. The completed work will show how they apply the skills taught in the earlier lessons.]", "128": "[Using the chart, I will think aloud through unhappy and careful. I will show where the affix occurs, identify the base, explain how the parts combine, and demonstrate a sentence and picture. Each step will be named and modeled before students complete their own sheets. This provides a clear process for word analysis without leaving students to infer the task from directions alone.]", "130": "[Lesson 2's sheets provide guided practice and formative evidence; Lesson 3's sheets ask students to apply the strategy independently as a summative task. Students will select words from the banks or shared recording sheet, identify the base and affix, and explain the whole meaning. Their sentences and visuals will show whether they understand the words in use. Additional examples with the same affix will show whether they can transfer the pattern.]", "134": "[Charts, step-by-step models, and guided examples will support access to the task. For the focus student, I will preteach vocabulary and check in during independent work. I will also invite connections with familiar words from home and daily life. These supports are intended to help him use his existing language knowledge as he analyzes and explains new words.]", "140": "[CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.R.F.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of most common prefixes and derivational suffixes CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.4.A Read grade-level text with purpose and understanding. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RL.3.1 Ask and answer questions to demonstrate understanding of a text, referring explicitly to the text as the basis for the answers CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.SL.3.1 Engage effectively in a range of collaborative discussions (one-on-one, in groups, and teacher-led) with diverse partners on grade 3 topics and texts, building on others' ideas and expressing their own clearly.]", "142": "[read a grade-level passage with fluency, explain affixed words in context, and support comprehension answers with evidence from the text.]", "148": "[The focus student and other students showed understanding on the summative word-study task, so I planned an extension that applies the same skills in a story. A Day at the Park includes family interactions, sports, and a park visit. These familiar situations allow students to connect new words with their own experiences while taking on a more demanding reading task.]", "150": "[We will read A Day at the Park together. I will model fluent reading and pause to explain my thinking about affixed words. Students will return to the passage to mark examples such as prepacked, skillful, useless, recheck, and disorganized. We will then discuss the words and answer comprehension questions using both their parts and clues from the story. This extends the earlier word study into reading for meaning.]", "152": "[I will first model fluent reading as students follow the passage. Then I will guide them to mark prefixes and suffixes and examine how those parts affect meaning. During the shared comprehension work, individual students will explain their reasoning. I will prompt them to refer to both the word structure and the surrounding text. This keeps the strategy explicit while giving students practice using it in context.]", "154": "[I will watch how accurately students mark affixes and listen as they explain the words and answer comprehension questions. Their responses will show whether they can connect the affix, the base, and contextual clues. I will also attend to their oral language and use of vocabulary. These observations will guide clarification and further practice.]", "156": "[I will model the academic language of word analysis during reading and discussion. Marking affixes will provide a visual reference. Students will explain meanings in complete sentences and point to supporting clues in the passage. Guided conversation will combine listening, speaking, and reading practice while supporting English learners' access to the text.]", "158": "[The familiar park setting will give students background knowledge to draw on. The focus student will receive clear steps, repeated models, and visible markings of prefixes and suffixes. I will use prompts and sentence frames when he needs help connecting context to meaning. These adaptations support word recognition and language while keeping the reading task appropriately challenging.]", "176": "[In Lesson 2, I will use the whole-class word discussion as a formative check. Students will propose words with pre-, re-, -ful, and -less and explain the effect of each affix. I will ask about the affix's meaning, the base's meaning, and how they fit together. Their oral explanations will show whether they understand the structure and can use the vocabulary. I can then clarify misconceptions and revisit difficult terms before students begin independent work.]", "178": "[I expect students to identify the four target affixes, explain them, and use the resulting words accurately in spoken sentences. As students share, I will listen for correct analysis and clear language, ask follow-up questions, and respond to errors. These checks address the literacy goal of word recognition through morphology and the ELD goal of explaining ideas with academic vocabulary.]", "183": "[The summative task takes place in Lesson 3, before the extension. Students will independently complete word study using banks containing pre-, re-, dis-, un-, -ing, -less, -ful, and -able. They will identify the base and affix, explain the whole word, use it in a complete sentence, draw its meaning, and produce other words with the same affix. This work will show their application of word analysis and language skills.]", "185": "[The assessment checks whether students can identify bases and affixes, combine their meanings, and use the resulting words in sentences, visuals, and further examples. These tasks address the listed RF.3.3.A and L.3.4.A–C goals for word analysis and vocabulary.", "186": "For language development, I will examine how students explain meaning and use vocabulary in context. Their oral and written explanations connect word structure with academic language and the collaborative and interpretive language goals identified in this assessment description.]", "190": "[I will look for accurate identification of parts and an explanation that connects the affix with the base. Sentences, pictures, and further examples should agree with the word's meaning. Clear vocabulary in written definitions and sentences will also indicate progress in academic language. These features will show how well students connect decoding, understanding, and expression.]", "203": "[Video Clip 1 shows the modeling and guided practice from Lesson 1. I used the gradual-release sequence to make word analysis explicit before students tried it themselves. At 00:03–00:24, I explained dis-, tract, and -ion while building a word and thinking aloud about how the parts contribute to meaning. The affix chart gave students a stable visual reference. At 00:25–1:05, think-pair-share allowed them to propose tract words and rehearse explanations with a peer.", "204": "At 01:37–02:21 and 2:39–2:58, the student in pink built subtracting. I guided her through its prefix, root, and suffix and connected the parts to the whole meaning. When she could not recall sub- at 02:22–02:39, I directed her to the chart and acknowledged her successful use of it. At 03:00–03:44, the student in black built distracting and worked through its parts with me. At 03:45–04:24, we compared distract and distracting; her sentence also gave me a chance to address verb tense and the form distracted.", "205": "Modeling, hands-on construction, questions, and immediate feedback linked word recognition with meaningful language use. Students could see the process, discuss it, and practice explaining it with support.]", "210": "In Clip 2 at 00:00–00:07, we read the definition of -less together. At 00:08–00:31, a student contributed homeless and identified the suffix and its meaning. At 00:42–01:29, another explained fearless and used her father's willingness to enter the dark as an example. At 01:32–02:02, a student explained hairless and distinguished the whole word from hair. Other contributions included numberless at 02:44–03:11 and brainless at 3:36–3:49, with a scarecrow as the example. At 04:37–05:02, we moved to -ful; a student offered worthful and explained it through the meaning full of worth.", "218": "At 00:07–01:03, the student in blue explained disappear as something being gone. I asked him to consider appear by itself, acknowledged his explanation, and referred him to the chart for spelling and meaning. At 01:30–02:35, I helped the student in black analyze penniless by asking about penny and -less and reminding him where a suffix occurs. At 02:48–03:05, the student in blue generated disagree as another dis- word. At 03:58–04:35, a student explained colorful by distinguishing color from the complete word, and I reminded her to check spelling.", "221": "[My feedback focused on the particular part of the analysis each student needed to revisit. At 00:07–01:03, I asked the student working with disappear to consider appear separately and then connect dis- to the changed meaning. At 01:30–02:35, I used questions and the chart to help the student working with penniless identify the base and suffix. At 03:58–04:35, I checked the explanation of colorful and prompted attention to spelling.", "226": "[Clip 4 returns to Lesson 2, when students were working in pairs. I noticed that several did not understand the lower portion of the sheet: writing a definition and sentence and supplying a synonym and antonym. I brought the class to the rug and modeled the task with delightful.", "227": "At 00:28–03:08, students helped read the model definition and sentence and offered other -ful words, synonyms, and antonyms. I confirmed accurate contributions, clarified meanings, and restated the steps when needed. This adjustment gave students a concrete example to follow when they returned to their paired work. It also connected the word-analysis task with the language needed to explain and use the vocabulary.]", "233": "[After reviewing the focus student's Lesson 3 prefix and suffix sheets, I provided individual feedback on both successful work and the next step. I linked the feedback to identifying word parts and explaining meaning with academic language.", "234": "For word selection and identification, I recognized the student's appropriate choice of repaint and accurate identification of paint and re-. The student understood re- as again and connected that meaning to the whole word. I explained how this strategy can help with unfamiliar vocabulary. The definition met the rubric's Excellent criterion because it accounted for both the prefix and the complete word. I encouraged the student to make that connection explicit in future explanations.", "235": "The sentence, “I had to repaint my wall cause it was dirty,” conveyed the meaning of repaint. I acknowledged that success and asked the student to replace cause with because to make the written language more precise. The detailed picture of repainting a wall also supported the definition and met the highest visual criterion. I explained that a clear picture can help communicate and check a word's meaning.", "236": "On the suffix sheet, the student identified color and -ful and connected full of with the meaning of colorful. I recognized the accurate sentence, “The rainbow was colorful,” and the matching rainbow illustration. Tearful, careful, and fearful provided further evidence that the student could apply the same suffix to other bases.", "237": "By addressing each component, I made the feedback manageable and specific. The student could see which strategies were working and had a clear language revision to make. This supported word analysis and written explanation while maintaining confidence.]", "244": "[The completed prefix and suffix tasks showed strong overall understanding. Most students identified bases and affixes accurately, and many connected the parts in a complete definition, used the words appropriately, and generated further examples. A few confused similar affixes or made spelling errors, but these were limited rather than a general pattern of misunderstanding. Most students earned high rubric ratings across the components, supporting my conclusion that they had met the learning goal.]", "247": "Lesson 3 brought the components together in individual word-study work. Students identified parts, defined words, wrote sentences, drew representations, and generated examples. My check-ins and feedback helped address confusion during the task. Across the lessons, visuals, oral rehearsal, peer work, and explicit demonstrations connected word structure with meaning and academic language.]", "249": "[I would add more sentence use to the Lesson 1 group activity so students did more than record the words they built. I would also include additional small-group discussion during guided practice, giving students time to compare meanings and explain their reasoning before working individually. For Lesson 2, I would model the full worksheet more clearly at the outset, especially definitions, sentences, synonyms, and antonyms. These changes would improve oral rehearsal and make the written expectations clearer.]", "252": "[The focus student's continuing needs include unfamiliar multisyllabic words, consistent analysis of affixes, and accurate academic vocabulary in speaking and writing. He sometimes applies an affix meaning too broadly or confuses similar parts. He also needs practice organizing complete sentences and connected paragraphs and using new vocabulary with appropriate grammar. Visuals, sentence frames, think-alouds, and guided discussion help him apply the strategies and explain his thinking more clearly.]", "254": "[I recognized his successful word choices, identification of bases and affixes, definitions, examples, sentences, and pictures. I then identified a manageable next step: refining written academic language, including using because in place of cause. I also encouraged him to make the connection between the affix and base explicit in explanations. This feedback tells him what to keep doing and what to revise in his next response.]", "257": "His summative work correctly identified bases and affixes and included definitions, sentences, and pictures that showed understanding. Feedback on because addressed the precision of his written language. Together, these features suggest that structured word analysis and language support helped him apply vocabulary knowledge while identifying a next step in expression.]", "259": "[I would provide more oral rehearsal with sentence frames before he writes, especially when an explanation requires precise academic language. His assessment showed good understanding of word parts but room to improve sentence expression. I would also offer more complex affixes as an extension and structure partner or small-group work to include timely feedback. This would build on his success while helping him carry word-study skills into comprehension and writing.]", "266": "[Students will use prefixes, suffixes, and context to explain vocabulary in grade-level text and apply that analysis during reading and discussion.]", "268": "[Students will explain affixed words in complete sentences, support meanings with contextual clues, and use academic vocabulary in discussion with peers.]", "270": "[I will model fluent reading of A Day at the Park, including pacing, expression, and attention to affixed words. Students will then reread independently and mark examples such as prepacked, skillful, useless, recheck, and disorganized. We will discuss their selections and answer comprehension questions together.", "271": "I will ask students to connect an affix's meaning with evidence from the story, such as the role of pre- in prepacked or clues that explain disorganized. I will check their markings, listen to explanations, and review comprehension answers. These checks will show whether students can use word parts and context together and where clarification is needed.]", "273": "[The focus student's Spanish knowledge offers a way to connect English word parts with familiar language patterns. The story's soccer and park experiences also connect with his interests and everyday life. I will invite him and his peers to use those connections when discussing vocabulary.", "276": "[I chose a familiar setting to make the passage accessible while keeping the vocabulary work challenging. The focus student will receive clear steps, repeated models, and visual cues from marked word parts. During discussion, guided questions and sentence frames will help him connect context with meaning and express a complete explanation. These supports address his need for structure and language rehearsal while extending his word-recognition skills.]", "278": "[I selected five students, including the focus student, whose Lesson 3 word-study work showed that they were ready for an extension. A Day at the Park asks them to transfer the analysis from isolated words into a passage. They must use affixes, consider context, and explain their reasoning with evidence. Working with this small group allows me to provide focused questions and feedback as students take that next step in comprehension and academic language.]", "282": "The Step 3 word-study products showed that most students could identify parts, explain meanings, and apply words in sentences, visuals, and further examples. The focus student also demonstrated these understandings while needing more precise written language. This evidence supported an extension for students ready to apply morphology in connected text. Lesson 4 therefore retained language support but added contextual clues and text-based explanations, linking assessment findings with the next instructional step.]", "295": "[The clip shows the small-group extension for students whose word-study work demonstrated strong understanding of bases and affixes. They had already practiced re- and dis- in guided and individual tasks. Here, the next step was to use that knowledge with contextual evidence while reading.", "296": "At 00:00, the student in blue reads Question 4 about Noah rechecking the hallway. I ask him to explain re- and return to the story for evidence. Questions about Noah's action and what he is looking for help him connect checking again with the search for his sister's shoes.", "297": "At 01:10, the student in red reads Question 5 about disorganized. I direct him to locate the word and reread its sentence. His first explanation is broad, so I prompt him to consider dis- and the surrounding clues. I record the response on the chart and model a more precise explanation with a sentence frame.", "298": "The activity extends the earlier work because identifying an affix is only the first step. Students must explain its effect in a particular story and support their interpretation with evidence. Guided questions and complete-sentence practice connect vocabulary and comprehension with academic language and prepare students to use the strategy with less support.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"726": "For Sentence Usage, I highlighted the student’s well-written sentence: “I had to repaint my wall cause it was dirty.” I provided actionable feedback by affirming that the sentence clearly showed the meaning of the word repaint, then guided the student to revise the sentence by replacing cause with because to strengthen academic language and grammar. This feedback supported the student’s language development while maintaining confidence in their writing.", "587": "[ In video clip 4 (Lesson 2), students were completing their word study worksheets in pairs. As I walked around, I noticed that several students needed support to complete the bottom section of the worksheet, where they were expected to write their own definition, create a sentence using the word, and identify a synonym and antonym. To address this, I gathered the students at the rug for whole-group instruction and explicitly modeled what was expected using the example word delightful, which includes the suffix -ful. I explained that students were expected to create a definition for the word, use it in a sentence, generate additional words using the same suffix, and identify synonyms and antonyms. In minute (00:28 - 03:08) to actively engage students, I invited a few to come to the front and help me read the modeled definition, read the sentence aloud, and contribute examples of words using the suffix -ful, as well as appropriate synonyms and antonyms. As students shared, I reinforced correct responses and addressed misunderstandings by clarifying meanings, scaffolding understanding with guided questions, and restating expectations as needed. This approach helped students strengthen their phonics and word recognition skills and their academic language, while giving them a concrete example to guide their paired work on the worksheet. This adjustment strengthened students’ phonics and word recognition through morphology, including prefixes, suffixes, and base words, while supporting academic language development. Actively involving students and providing scaffolded support allowed them to successfully apply these skills to their word study worksheets, reinforcing both literacy and language development. ]"}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}, "ms-math": {"id": "ms-math", "lens": "ms-math", "thesis": "This is a promising measurement lesson with a strong reasoning routine. Its main weakness is the distance between an engaging plan and the thin record of what individual students learned. Estimating, measuring, choosing a tool, and explaining a comparison must be evaluated separately.", "version": "The supplied 2025 Version 1 sample uses three focus students. The full rubric comparison below uses the saved 2026–27 MS Math V2.0 guide. Original V1 descriptors were not supplied or verified; these are V2 instructional comparisons, not retrospective V1 scores. The original example and its directions remain intact.", "standards": [["2.MD.1 (also styled 2.MD.A.1)", "Select and use an appropriate tool to measure length.", "Part B ¶67–78 offers rulers and ten rods; the objects create a real measurement task. A ten rod is a useful benchmark only if its actual length is verified.", "Check zero alignment, equal intervals, end point, recorded unit, and the reason for choosing the tool. The blank table cannot establish student accuracy.", "Direct task alignment; learning outcome unverified.", "math", 28], ["2.MD.3 (2.MD.A.3)", "Estimate length using standard units.", "The estimate-before-measure sequence makes this distinct from reading a ruler. This lesson samples centimeters; it does not demonstrate work with every unit named in the standard.", "Record the estimate before showing the ruler result. Ask which known length informed it and why a revision is reasonable.", "Appropriate lesson-sized portion of the standard.", "math", 28], ["MP5", "Use tools strategically, recognizing what each tool can and cannot show.", "Choice between a ruler and rods is an opportunity for judgment, but offering two tools alone does not show strategic use.", "Ask a student to choose for a short object and a longer object, justify the choice, and explain how the tool helped check an estimate.", "Planned; independent strategic reasoning is not documented.", "math", 9], ["Academic language goal; no ELD code cited", "Explain the estimate/measurement relationship with meaningful vocabulary.", "Part B ¶73 names terms; ¶90–91 adds reasoning quality. The task needs a relationship such as “My estimate was lower because…” rather than isolated labels.", "Collect a brief explanation and note whether it was independent, frame-supported, or supplied after a teacher model.", "ALD goal is present. A specific ELD proficiency descriptor has not been identified in this case.", "eld", 57]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Start with mathematical evidence, not a language label", [13, 15, 29, 33], "The class description identifies bilingual communication and collaborative problem solving as resources. The planned use of Spanish to reason before explaining in English is therefore purposeful. However, Part A emphasizes arithmetic, while the lesson requires length estimation and unit iteration. Arithmetic competence does not by itself show whether a child aligns at zero, counts intervals, or understands a centimeter. A brief prelesson measuring task would provide a closer basis for planning. FS1’s reported overall ELPAC result and domain results should be checked against the actual score report; neither the review nor the rewrite should repair a questionable record by guessing.", "Keep language proficiency and mathematics separate. Record one measurement attempt and one explanation, including the support used, before deciding whether FS1 needs vocabulary support, mathematical instruction, or both."], ["warmup", "B", "Which One Does Not Belong: protect the mathematical reasoning", [75, 82, 88], "The four diagrams allow different defensible choices, which can reduce the pressure to guess the teacher’s answer. The instructional value lies in a student naming a mathematical property: unequal units, a nonzero starting position, or a different length. A child choosing the triangle because it looks different is participating, but has not necessarily reasoned about measurement. Follow that observation with a comparison of intervals. The visual containing a nonzero start is especially useful: the final numeral is not automatically the object’s length. Invite students to test that claim with the same object shifted along a ruler. This turns a visual sorting task into an explanation of distance.", "Collect two contrasting explanations and ask which evidence in the diagram supports each. Record who notices units and starting positions without a prompt."], ["estimate", "B", "Estimation should use a reference, not reward lucky guesses", [71, 76, 80], "The ten rod gives children a reference for judging a notebook or another object before measuring. This is stronger than asking for a number without a basis. The source pictures show a notebook first without, then with, a rod. Those displays can reveal how additional information improves an estimate, provided the first estimate is recorded before the reference appears. Do not score a close guess as stronger reasoning than a somewhat less close estimate with a sensible benchmark. Also confirm that the manipulatives are physically ten centimeters long; base-ten value and physical length are different properties. Image scaling on a slide is not proof of the real object’s centimeter length.", "Use separate notes for the numerical estimate, the reference used, and the explanation of revision. Preserve the first estimate so improvement can be examined."], ["measure", "B", "Measuring the five objects: define the length being measured", [78, 88, 90], "The sticky note, book, spoon, paper clip, and pen provide varied end points. That variety can expose useful misconceptions, but only if the dimension is specified. For a spoon, distinguish the straight distance from tip to handle from tracing its curved edge. For a book, name the edge to measure. Selecting only whole-centimeter objects, as the reflection claims, should be verified with the actual materials; a classroom object need not land exactly on a marked unit. Comparing partners’ results should lead to checking alignment and intervals, not accepting any number because estimates may differ. Estimates can vary; correctly specified measurements should agree within the precision of the tool.", "Model one measurement from zero and one from a shifted start. Ask students to explain why the counted intervals, rather than the final numeral alone, determine the length."], ["fs1", "C", "FS1: connect bilingual resources to the precise explanation", [96, 102, 108, 114, 171, 191], "The adaptation combines visual models, Spanish-English connections, and partner rehearsal. These supports address the stated problem of language obscuring otherwise accessible mathematics. The reflection reports that FS1 compared an estimate and a measurement, but does not give the explanation or identify the amount of prompting. Participation is credible as a reported observation; independent mastery of mathematical English is not established. Her next step can remain linguistically ambitious while using Spanish to clarify the reasoning. A vocabulary list alone would not reveal whether she understands why an estimate is high or low.", "Save one explanation in the student’s available language and the subsequent English explanation. Compare mathematical meaning, target vocabulary, and prompting separately."], ["fs2", "C", "FS2: resolve the difference between checking carefully and starting too quickly", [40, 42, 98, 104, 116, 173, 193], "Part A describes careful checking and detailed explanation but also a hurried start on word problems. These can coexist: a learner may revise well once engaged yet misread the initial task. The analysis should name that distinction rather than describe her generally as careless. Timed quiet thinking and partner explanation can help her identify what is being estimated before reaching for a ruler. The next-step decision to practice estimation is appropriately narrower than repeating measurement. What is missing is a specific estimate, the reference she used, and what changed after a prompt. Without those details, a conclusion about improved estimation is weak.", "Give a new object. Ask FS2 to name the dimension, select a reference, estimate, explain, and only then measure. Compare this with the original attempt under similar support conditions."], ["fs3", "C", "FS3: combine challenge and regulation with an observable plan", [49, 51, 100, 106, 118, 175, 195], "Challenge, movement, and a break are plausible responses to strong reasoning and variable attention. The reflection’s descriptions of stretching and resting after finishing are more concrete than a general statement that the child was engaged. They do not, however, show that the extension deepened his mathematics. Helping classmates can be valuable, but should not replace his own opportunity to investigate. A targeted plan needs the extension problem, how he can request a break, and how he returns to the task. Avoid treating quiet compliance as the only sign that a well-being support worked.", "Offer an investigation such as measuring a longer object with a short tool, then documenting how successive placements avoid gaps or overlaps. Record his reasoning and use of the planned regulation options."], ["artifacts", "D", "The resources show the task, not completed learning", [124, 129, 134, 139], "The retained material includes rulers, rods, objects, the estimate/measurement table, and instructional slides. The table has two numerical columns but no space for a benchmark, tool choice, or explanation. Consequently it samples only part of the combined content/practice/language goal unless paired with recorded observation. These are largely blank resources and photographs, not a scored set of student measurements. Their presence establishes what was available for teaching; it cannot corroborate the reflection’s claims of accuracy or readiness to move on.", "Keep the two numerical columns and add a short explanation or teacher observation code for tool choice and reasoning. Collect completed work from the focus students and a representative range of the class."], ["teaching", "F", "A timestamp list needs a student-response-to-adjustment sequence", [153, 155, 157, 158, 159], "The commentary names useful moments: a misconception during the warm-up, attention to measurement rules, and difficulty measuring the spoon. But most entries report what the teacher did rather than what the student said or changed. “I clarified” does not identify the error; “I checked” does not identify the criterion. Select a few high-value episodes and reconstruct the sequence from the actual recording: initial response, interpretation, teacher action, and student response afterward. The supplied packet contains commentary, not the videos, so this review cannot verify the events, classroom atmosphere, content accuracy throughout the clips, or the effectiveness of a prompt.", "Use the spoon episode to show what was being measured, the student’s initial method, the corrective question, and the subsequent attempt. Keep the distinction between a described event and a viewed event explicit."], ["assessment", "G", "Do not collapse four outcomes into “most met the goal”", [167, 169, 171, 173, 175, 183, 185, 189], "The reflection gives plausible explanations of how tools and discussion supported access, but supplies no class denominator, criterion counts, completed measurement records, or verbatim focus-student explanation. The reported pattern—FS2 stronger in measuring than estimating—is instructional information worth preserving. It is not a verified subscore. Similarly, a thumbs check captures confidence or perceived uncertainty, not necessarily measurement competence. Evaluate estimating, measuring, tool reasoning, and language as four related outcomes. A student may accurately measure yet be unable to justify a reference-based estimate; another may explain well while misaligning the tool.", "Build a small evidence matrix with one row per student and separate criteria, including support level. Use it to justify who needs reteaching and who is ready for extension."], ["next", "H", "Make the differentiated next steps testable", [189, 191, 193, 195, 199, 201], "The next-step pattern is sensible: FS1 needs more precise English explanation, FS2 needs reference-based estimation, and FS3 needs challenge. The group can continue practice instead of automatically repeating the entire lesson. Nevertheless, the claim that most students have mastered the work needs the missing records. The proposed higher-order thinking—analyze, connect, evaluate, create—remains a list of verbs until attached to a task. A useful next lesson would require choosing a tool for an unfamiliar object, making a benchmark-based estimate, measuring, and explaining any discrepancy. This keeps the content, practice, and language demands connected.", "Define success before teaching: correct alignment and unit, a plausible benchmark, and an explanation of the difference between estimate and result. Preserve accommodations while fading only the prompts that are no longer needed."]], "comparison": [[328, 154, "The rewrite usefully names the familiar phrases used to unpack mathematical language. It still needs the student’s explanation after that support; clearer teacher narration has not supplied evidence of student learning."], [461, 193, "The revised next step distinguishes estimation from measuring and avoids a blanket reteach. That is a stronger instructional decision, but the actual estimate and error pattern still need to accompany it."]], "illustration": "Illustrative revision, not a reported event: “I will compare FS2’s initial estimate with the reference she names before measuring. If she measures accurately but cannot explain her estimate, I will retain measurement practice and teach the use of a known centimeter length as a benchmark. I will then check a different object without the same prompt.” This improves the decision rule without inventing a result.", "rubrics": [[1, "2–3", ["Partial — ¶15 and ¶80 connect earlier learning, but no recent measurement response is supplied.", "Supported — ¶67–69 identifies Grade 2 length standards and MP5.", "Supported — ¶71 joins estimation/measurement with tool use; require a tool-choice explanation.", "Supported — ¶73 links language to the mathematical task.", "Supported in planning — ¶82 makes different defensible explanations welcome.", "Supported in planning — ¶84 uses bilingual reasoning and collaboration.", "Supported in planning — ¶88 asks students to justify estimates and examine alignment.", "Supported in planning — ¶90–91 gives content and language indicators.", "Supported in planning — ¶78–86 offers concrete, visual, oral, and written access."], "The design is substantially developed, but recent learning evidence is broad. A 3 depends on accepting the stated measurement experience as a clear basis for the plan; the more conservative reading is 2.", "A 1 would overlook the explicit goals and coherent task. Collaboration resembles the Level 4 addition, but cannot override an incomplete Level 3 foundation. No developed second-content-area lesson supports 5."], [2, "3–4", ["Supported — ¶102 connects FS1’s language access needs with observed word-problem difficulty.", "Supported — ¶108 uses drawings and bilingual reasoning.", "Supported — ¶114 plans reading, listening, rehearsal, and an explanation."], "There is a clear need-to-support connection. The evidence is about planned access, not a demonstrated learning effect.", "A 2 understates the specificity of visuals and language work. Multiple communication modes support considering 4; 5 is not justified by an explicit research-based rationale."], [3, "3", ["Supported — ¶104 distinguishes task processing from mathematical foundation.", "Supported — ¶110 uses practical comparison and explanation.", "Supported — ¶116 addresses deliberate sequencing and alignment."], "The adaptation targets the identified task-entry problem. It should include a specific recent estimate rather than rest on general observations.", "The match is clearer than 2. Two distinct multisensory strategies targeted to FS2, rather than general activity variety, need clearer specification before 4. Research justification for 5 is absent."], [4, "2–3", ["Partial — ¶106 reports greater engagement with challenge but gives no dated or detailed example.", "Supported — ¶112 uses investigation and independent exploration.", "Partial — ¶118 offers challenge and breaks without a fully specified regulation plan."], "The idea is plausible but the trigger, task, and return-to-work routine remain broad. This is stronger than no adaptation and weaker than a fully evidenced individual plan.", "Do not award 4 simply for mentioning breaks; a targeted proactive plan must be clear. A 3 is possible only if the general observations adequately establish the need."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Unverified — ¶152–159 describes a supportive environment; clips are unavailable.", "Unverified — tools, bilingual support and discussion are described but not observed.", "Unverified — complete mathematical accuracy cannot be checked from timestamp summaries.", "Partial — commentary broadly matches the planned measuring lesson."], "No performance level is assigned because this rubric requires demonstration as well as description. The written account gives relevant material to inspect when videos become available.", "Neither a low score for absent supplied media nor a 3–5 based on narration alone is warranted. Examine the complete clip, including how estimates and measurements are distinguished."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Unverified — ¶153 and ¶158 refer to reasoning without the students’ actual explanations.", "Partial — ¶185 names an adjustment but omits the misconception and subsequent response."], "The spoon episode could support an assessment-to-adjustment argument. The present record does not establish its effect or the cognitive demand students actually sustained.", "The Level 4 addition requires demonstrated multiple assessments informing adjustment; a timestamp count is not that evidence. Real-life discussion alone does not establish 5."], [7, "2", ["Partial — ¶167–185 describes successes but offers few concrete limitations.", "Supported — prior routines and tools inform the reflection.", "Partial — participation is used as evidence of the environment’s effect.", "Partial — bilingual and collaborative assets are linked to access, not individual results.", "Partial — UDL mechanisms are explained without completed products.", "Partial — higher-order thinking is asserted, not traced through a student explanation.", "Partial — content/practice/language results are not disaggregated.", "Partial — all three focus students are discussed, but most outcome claims lack artifacts."], "The reflection is relevant and coherent, which rules out a no-evidence judgment. It does not meet the full Level 3 demand for clear analysis across these elements.", "A 3 would require specific student evidence and a clearer account of what did not work. The Level 4–5 additions do not compensate for that missing analysis."], [8, "2–3", ["Supported — ¶189–195 identifies different next steps.", "Partial — group learning is described as achieved without a criterion record; ¶201 addresses language.", "Supported — FS1 language, FS2 estimation, and FS3 challenge receive distinct responses.", "Partial — the decision pattern is relevant but few specific examples justify readiness."], "The differentiated plan is stronger than a general promise to practice. The evidence for moving on is not yet equally strong.", "A 2 fits the thin justification; 3 becomes defensible with the specific measurements and explanations. Future interests alone do not establish 4 before the base reasoning is complete."]], "title": "Grade 2 measurement", "file": "04_MS_Grade_2_Math_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 MS MPA (2nd Grade) Sample Submission.docx", "quotes": {"13": "[Students participate in a dual-language program and use both Spanish and English to work through ideas. That flexibility gives them several ways to explain their mathematics. They also enjoy collaboration and hands-on tasks, especially opportunities to test an idea with classmates.]", "15": "[The class has been working on place value, addition and subtraction within 100, and explaining a strategy with evidence. Students recognize that a problem can be approached in more than one way. They choose methods they understand, use drawings to represent the work, and are beginning to include more mathematical vocabulary in their explanations.]", "29": "[FS1 is Hispanic and has lived in the United States since birth. She has taken the ELPAC four times since entering school in 2022. Her 2024–2025 overall result was 4; the reported domain results were 2 in listening and writing and 3 in speaking and reading. Her Spanish reading level moved from 12 to 14 during the year, with level 14 described as the end of first grade.]", "33": "[FS1 needs support with the language of word problems. Vocabulary previews, sentence frames, and visuals will help her focus on the mathematical situation. Connecting terms with Spanish will also let her draw on familiar language while noticing how the two languages express the same idea.]", "40": "[FS2 understands basic addition and subtraction and checks her work carefully. When a detail no longer fits her reasoning, she revises it. She also explains her steps thoroughly to others and asks questions to make sure they understand.]", "42": "[FS2 needs a more deliberate start when approaching word problems. A consistent process for rereading, identifying the task, and planning steps will help her slow down before calculating. That structure should help her organize the numbers around the meaning of the problem.]", "49": "[FS3 reasons confidently, notices patterns, and uses strategies such as decomposing numbers and choosing friendly numbers. He checks whether an answer makes sense and explains his process in detail. He can clarify questions and connect ideas across content areas.]", "51": "[FS3 needs meaningful challenge to sustain engagement. I will connect problems to familiar situations and offer tasks that ask him to extend his reasoning. Opportunities for movement and purposeful breaks will also help him stay ready to participate.]", "67": "[CCSS.MATH.2.MD.A.1 (Measure the length of an object by selecting and using appropriate tools such as rulers, yardsticks, meter sticks, and measuring tapes) and CCSS.MATH.2.MD.A.3 (Estimate lengths using units of inches, feet, centimeters, and meters)]", "68": "• one Mathematical Practice (MP):", "69": "[MP5 (Use appropriate tools strategically)]", "70": "b. Develop a Math Content and Practice Learning Goal: Based on the age-/grade-level strand/standard and math practice you selected, write one developmentally appropriate math learning goal that includes both math content and practice (see examples on pages 12–13 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide).", "71": "[Students will estimate and measure classroom objects in centimeters, using a ruler or base-ten materials strategically. This work addresses 2.MD.A.1, 2.MD.A.3, and MP5.]", "72": "2. Develop a Math ALD Learning Goal: Based on the relevant math academic vocabulary needed for the lesson, create a math academic language development goal that supports the math content and practice learning goal (see examples on page 13 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide).", "73": "[Students will explain an estimate or measurement in simple sentences, using terms such as estimate, measure, length, centimeter, and ruler during the activities.]", "74": "3. What will the students do in the math lesson?", "75": "[I will begin with a ten-minute Which One Doesn't Belong warm-up showing four images of measured shapes. Students will think quietly, discuss a choice with a partner, and explain it to the class. We will consider reasons for each image rather than look for a single acceptable answer.", "76": "In the first activity, students will estimate lengths in centimeters. They will draw on their knowledge that a ten rod is 10 centimeters and use it as a reference for judging whether an estimate is too short, too long, or about right. In the second activity, they will measure classroom objects and compare the results with their estimates. Discussion and the recorded work will make their reasoning visible.]", "77": "a. What instructional materials and/or resources will you use in the math lesson? Why?", "78": "[Students will use 30-centimeter rulers, 10-centimeter rods, pencils, worksheets, and classroom objects. The rods provide a familiar reference as students move toward a standard measuring tool. The worksheets organize the estimates and measurements, while real objects give students something concrete to compare and measure.]", "79": "4. What UDL-focused instructional strategies will you use during the math lesson to support student learning? Describe how you will: a. Connect the content of this lesson to students’ recent math learning.", "80": "[Students already know that a unit cube measures one centimeter and a ten rod measures ten. I will connect those familiar base-ten materials to a ruler and reinforce centimeters as a standard unit. The estimating work will also extend their earlier practice judging whether a quantity is reasonable.]", "81": "b. Create a safe, positive learning environment.", "82": "[I will value different explanations during the open-ended warm-up and make clear that each image can be justified. During measurement work, I will describe an estimate as a reasoned guess that may need revision. This should help students share ideas without treating an initial estimate as a failure.]", "83": "c. Leverage your students’ assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.", "84": "[Planned partner talk will let students use the language that best helps them reason, with support for making connections across Spanish and English. Measuring real objects will draw on their interest in trying things themselves. Collaboration will give them opportunities to compare approaches and explain what they notice.]", "85": "d. Support the math ALD of your students.", "86": "[I will model measurement vocabulary accurately, ask students what the terms mean, and connect the words with the tools and actions. Sentence frames will help students organize an explanation while still supplying their own mathematical thinking.]", "88": "[I will ask students to justify why an estimate is high, low, or reasonable and reconsider it after using a familiar reference. We will also examine what happens when tools differ or an object is not aligned at zero. These questions ask students to evaluate the measurement process as well as the answer.]", "90": "[I will look for complete estimates, appropriate units, and correct alignment of the object and tool. Students still developing the skill may omit units, estimate incompletely, or measure inaccurately. Meeting the goal means applying the measurement process correctly; stronger evidence includes revising an estimate and explaining the comparison that prompted the change.", "91": "For language, I will distinguish vague statements from explanations that use the vocabulary and frames meaningfully. More developed responses will connect an estimate with a reference—for example, recognizing that an object is longer than two ten rods—and explain why the estimate changed.]", "96": "[FS1 will have images, ten rods, rulers, and sentence frames throughout the lesson. If a spoken explanation is difficult to follow, the concrete and visual information will give her another way to reason. The frames will help her communicate an estimate without having to construct all the English language on her own.]", "98": "[I will give FS2 explicit directions and reminders to pause before acting. Comparing a range of estimates in Activity 1 will require her to consider reasonableness. During Activity 2, I will return her attention to the measurement process and how to record it. These prompts will help her organize the task before trying to finish it.]", "100": "[FS3 will have extension tasks after the initial partner work, including finding differences between estimates or measured lengths. These challenges will give him a reason to extend his thinking. Hands-on activity, movement opportunities, and check-ins about whether he needs a break will also help sustain engagement.]", "102": "[Observations, formative work, and individual interviews show that FS1 can solve numerical and visually represented problems. Word problems and oral directions become more accessible when I clarify the language. The planned visuals and Spanish-English connections respond to that pattern while building vocabulary.]", "104": "[FS2's observations, assessments, and interview show the needed mathematical foundation, along with a tendency to begin before fully processing the task. I will prompt a step-by-step approach and use partner discussion to give her time to compare, explain, and revise her reasoning.]", "106": "[FS3's work and interviews show strong reasoning, and I have observed greater engagement when a task offers a new challenge. Extensions will ask him to apply that strength. The practical activities and movement will help him maintain attention while he explores the ideas.]", "108": "[FS1 already uses drawings and concrete models to solve problems. This lesson makes those strengths useful through measuring tools and visual examples. She will also be encouraged to use the language that supports her thinking, with help connecting it to English. That combination supports the content goal and her developing academic language.]", "110": "[FS2's interest in practical situations will be useful when estimating and measuring real objects. Comparing her own and a partner's estimates will encourage her to pause, check reasonableness, and ask for clarification. Those exchanges will also give her a purpose for using the math vocabulary.]", "112": "[FS3 enjoys exploring and testing ideas. Estimating an object's length before measuring it gives him a problem to investigate, while the extensions offer further challenge. Explaining his choices will connect that independent exploration with the language goal.]", "114": "[FS1 will read the worksheet directions, hear vocabulary modeled, and discuss the difference between an estimate and a measurement. Examples of high, low, and reasonable estimates will make the terms concrete. Partner discussion and sentence frames will help her turn that understanding into a spoken explanation.]", "116": "[The sequence asks FS2 to pause, identify the task, try a method, and explain it. Hearing a partner's thinking will give her another opportunity to check or revise the process. During measurement, the need for accurate alignment will reinforce the value of moving through the steps carefully.]", "118": "[FS3 will have hands-on choices, extension problems, and access to a break. Estimating and testing a length should hold his interest, while comparisons will offer added challenge. I will check how he is feeling and provide space to stretch or rest when needed so he can return ready to work.]", "124": "[Students will measure in centimeters with rulers and ten rods.]", "129": "[The worksheet asks students to estimate object lengths and then record their measurements.]", "134": "[Students will measure a sticky note, a small book, a spoon, a paper clip, and a pen.]", "139": "[A projected slide deck will guide the warm-up and activities. Its tasks correspond to the worksheet.]", "152": "[00:16, 00:21, and 00:23: I move students from quiet consideration of the warm-up into familiar partner talk, listening to their interpretations and interactions. At 00:31, I invite whole-class responses. At 00:52 and 00:56, I restate an idea and recognize shared observations. At 01:01 and 01:22, I invite another choice and make the explanation clear to the class.", "153": "01:48, 02:12, and 02:39: My questions draw attention to units, an emerging misconception, and how the image represents measurement. At 02:48, I bring in the remaining choice. At 03:19 and 03:43, I recognize an extension and reconnect an explanation to the visual. At 03:54, students recall earlier measurement learning. At 04:00 and 04:23, I emphasize that different choices can be justified, supporting participation without fear of a single wrong selection.", "154": "04:31 and 04:37: I organize worksheet collection by rows and use students' home language to clarify. At 05:01 and 05:10, students think independently and then follow a structured partner exchange. At 05:12, I provide targeted language support, followed by class sharing at 05:28. At 05:56, I connect the vocabulary with a familiar phrase, too short.", "155": "06:00, 06:02, 06:04, and 06:13: I repeat the cycle of individual thought, low-pressure partner discussion, bilingual support, and whole-group sharing. At 06:33, I connect about right with the familiar idea of making sense. At 06:40 and 06:42, I ask about measurement rules and listen more closely to emerging bilingual students' reasoning.", "156": "07:02 and 07:06: I make the material accessible and connect it with previous learning. At 07:22, I link adjust with change. Students reconsider their estimates at 07:32, with additional language support at 07:38. I check time and support needs at 07:50 and invite explanations at 07:58. At 08:29, I use the visual to clarify; at 08:41 and 08:55, I state the goal and revisit vocabulary.", "157": "09:04, 09:18, and 9:38: Before students leave the carpet, I explain partnerships and have them consider how they will work together and what steps come next. At 09:51, I circulate. At 10:16, I review work with students, and at 10:41, I remind them that estimates may differ. At 10:49, I return to the precision and rules needed for measurement.", "158": "11:23 and 11:25: I check understanding and continue the process with other objects. At 11:30, I address difficulties measuring the spoon. At 11:51, students can raise differences in their results or ask for clarification. At 12:07, 12:13, and 12:28, I invite reflection on the lesson in a supportive discussion.", "159": "13:15: I recognize progress toward the goal. At 13:23 and 13:42, students connect the work with everyday life and possible use beyond class. At 13:51, I make sure a classmate's contribution is heard. At 14:09, I connect different measurement approaches with previous learning. At 14:26, I restate an outside-school application. Finally, at 14:39, I use the thumbs check to notice students who may still feel uncertain.]", "167": "[The different tools and representations helped students work toward estimating and measuring in centimeters. Rulers and ten rods made the ideas concrete and gave students practice selecting an appropriate tool under MP5. I observed their work, listened to explanations, and reviewed what they recorded. Those checks showed how they were using estimates, alignment, and measurement and helped me provide support during the activity.]", "168": "2. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students’ progress toward meeting the math ALD learning goal? How did you monitor and support students’ progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.", "169": "[Quiet thinking time and structured partner talk gave students space to prepare an explanation before sharing with the class. Sentence frames and guiding questions supported that preparation. I connected unfamiliar terms with words students already understood and modeled the vocabulary in context. Listening to their discussions helped me judge how well they were using the language to explain the mathematics.]", "170": "3. Analyze the effectiveness of the adaptation(s) used to support each focus student’s learning needs: a. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 1’s language learning needs effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?", "171": "[FS1 used the visual tools to support her understanding and participated in discussions about estimates that were high, low, or reasonable. She also used language to compare an estimate with a measurement. The combination of home-language support, English models, and concrete representations helped her show the content knowledge while continuing to develop mathematical English.]", "172": "b. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 2’s math learning need(s) effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?", "173": "[The practical task and timed discussion steps helped FS2 slow down. Accurate measurement required attention to alignment and the process, while individual thinking time and partner explanation gave her a reason to check her reasoning. Those supports improved access to the content and language goals, even though estimation still needs targeted practice.]", "174": "c. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 3’s well-being and/or behavior in the math lesson effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?", "175": "[FS3 stayed highly involved, explained his reasoning, responded to peers, and helped clarify confusion. He also used the available breaks: stretching while on the rug and resting his head after finishing the worksheet while others continued. The combination of challenge, hands-on work, and opportunities to regulate supported his participation and progress.]", "176": "4. How did the safe, positive learning environment you created support the students’ ability to engage in the math lesson?", "177": "[Students were willing to offer an idea, hear another approach, and add to classmates' explanations. Treating different perspectives as useful helped them take risks during discussion. Working together also gave them support when they needed another way to understand the task.]", "178": "5. How did the students’ information you provided in Part A (Written Narrative: Contextual Information) inform your math lesson planning and teaching? a. Prior math learning", "179": "[I built on students' experience with Which One Doesn't Belong routines and measurement activities. Familiar formats let them focus on the new connections among tools and units. During teaching, I explicitly recalled earlier learning, clarified where needed, and checked how students were managing the activities.]", "180": "b. Students’ assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests", "181": "[Knowing that all students use more than one language led me to plan varied representations and opportunities for language support. Their interest in collaboration and practical work shaped the activities. I also included time to connect measurement with their lives beyond school so their experiences could contribute to the lesson.]", "182": "c. Students’ learning needs", "183": "[Students could choose tools that made the process clear to them, including rulers and ten rods. I selected objects with whole-centimeter lengths so partial units would not become an additional demand. Structured discussions supported language needs, and I remained flexible about pacing and the amount of help students required.]", "184": "6. How did you respond intentionally to your students and adjust your instruction to provide “just-in-time” support to advance their math development?", "185": "[I used observations and discussion to identify a misconception as it arose. When students needed clarification, I modeled the process again or rephrased the explanation. Responding at that point helped them continue with a clearer understanding rather than carry the same error into the next object.]", "189": "[Most students demonstrated the estimating and measuring goals, using tools, units, and alignment appropriately and sometimes extending the work through comparisons. I would continue with a review and more practice rather than repeat the whole lesson. Their explanations about high, low, and reasonable estimates provide a foundation for moving forward.]", "190": "b. For Focus Student 1", "191": "[FS1 understands the estimation and measurement concepts and does not need the lesson retaught. She would benefit from more support in explaining that understanding fully in English. I would build on the reasoning she can express in her primary language and strengthen the connections to English vocabulary, including language for comparisons.]", "192": "c. For Focus Student 2", "193": "[FS2 showed stronger measurement skills than estimation skills. I would provide focused estimation practice rather than reteach all the content. A deliberate sequence of looking, comparing with a reference, estimating, and explaining would help her slow down and judge the task before responding.]", "194": "d. For Focus Student 3", "195": "[FS3 completed the work accurately, used the language confidently, and helped classmates. His questions and ideas extended measurement into everyday situations and comparisons. He does not need reteaching; further challenge will help him apply and deepen what he knows.]", "199": "[I will ask students to analyze a problem, connect it with other learning, evaluate a method, and explain why the method makes sense. Creating their own situations or representations will extend that reasoning. Connections with mathematics outside school will give those tasks a meaningful context.]", "201": "[I will model vocabulary and restate students' ideas using mathematical language, then provide sentence frames when needed. Discussion will continue to move from private thinking to partner talk and whole-class sharing. Over time, I will reduce those scaffolds so students can show greater independence in both reasoning and language.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"328": "05:28 - Here I am providing students with an opportunity to share with the whole group. They previously had individual and partner talk time to help them prepare and process their thoughts. 05:56 - Here I am helping students make connections within their academic vocabulary by tying in language that they may be more familiar with, such as \"too short.\"", "461": "For Focus Student 2 [FS2 demonstrated partial understanding of estimates, with a strong understanding of measuring lengths. She was confident in her ability to make comparisons and follow precise rules, but would benefit from more targeted support in making accurate estimates. I don’t think it would be necessary to reteach the whole lesson; however, a review and targeted practice would be of support. During this time, I think it would be important to specifically create steps or a process that forces her to slow down and consider what she’s being asked"}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}, "ss-c1": {"id": "ss-c1", "lens": "ss-c1", "thesis": "The lesson provides thoughtful access to a difficult informational text, but its scaffolds sometimes do the central-idea reasoning for students. The strongest analysis distinguishes participation, vocabulary use, independent comprehension, and the full demand of RI.7.2.", "version": "This is the supplied 2025 V1 English example. The comparison uses the saved SS Cycle 1 V2.0 rubrics for 2026–27; the original V1 descriptor set was not verified. Numerical ranges below are explicitly current-guide instructional comparisons. They are not original-year or official scores.", "standards": [["RI.7.2", "Determine at least two central ideas, analyze their development, and summarize objectively.", "¶64 asks for one central idea; ¶66 asks for two or more. The board and four choices emphasize selecting one. That is a useful scaffolded step, not evidence of the entire standard.", "Require two defensible ideas, their development across the text, and an objective synthesis. A selected answer plus copied evidence does not establish development.", "Partial coverage and inconsistent goals.", "ela", 57], ["ELD.PI.7.10b.Ex", "Write increasingly concise summaries using complete sentences and key words.", "The summary frame supports organization, but the retained frame refers to events and a character or situation. Those narrative terms fit this informational argument poorly.", "Judge accuracy, selection of key information, concision, objectivity, and the student’s own wording.", "Relevant standard; revise the frame to match the text type.", "eld", 112], ["ELD.PI.7.1.Br", "Sustain and contribute to discussion, including building on others’ ideas.", "The source uses a Bridging discussion code for FS1 while reporting EL level 3. Those labels should not be treated as a direct placement conversion.", "Record a relevant contribution and a response to a peer. Choose support intensity from observed language use, not a single test label.", "Discussion opportunity present; proficiency match needs evidence.", "eld", 109], ["Academic vocabulary: suggests, indicates, represents", "Use language to express a defensible relation between evidence and interpretation.", "The language goal is meaningful when the verb introduces an actual explanation. Filling a frame with indicates is not automatically analysis.", "Score the relationship being expressed and the accuracy of the text evidence separately from vocabulary presence.", "Explicit language target; assessment criteria not supplied.", "eld", 113]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Reconcile the roster before interpreting percentages", [13, 17, 19, 61], "Part A describes 32 students. The baseline data describe 33: 22 beginning, eight developing, and three secure. The counts total 33, and their precise proportions are 66.7%, 24.2%, and 9.1%. This may reflect a roster change or a different assessment population, but neither is established. The record also distinguishes four current English learners and two reclassified students, then calls all six English learners. Preserve the distinction because current service needs cannot be inferred from a combined label. These are analytical issues, not merely proofreading: the baseline and outcome cannot be compared responsibly until the population and categories match.", "Report the date, denominator, and inclusion rule for each assessment. Explain any difference from the taught class and keep RFEP status separate from current EL identification."], ["fs1", "C", "FS1: preserve Grade 7 thinking while supporting access", [29, 31, 33, 89, 96, 102, 108], "A STAR grade-equivalent result is not a prescription to replace all seventh-grade work with a second-grade curriculum. The relevant classroom evidence is his partial summaries, interest in books, and need for clarified directions. Short visible steps, oral rehearsal, and vocabulary models can reduce the reading and language burden while keeping the task focused on central ideas. Basketball becomes an instructional asset only if it helps him understand a relationship in the selected text; naming the interest in Part A is insufficient. The plan should also distinguish understanding a printed passage from explaining an idea after hearing it read aloud.", "Use an accessible reading of the same passage, then ask FS1 to select a supporting detail and explain how it develops an idea. Record which prompts supported the explanation."], ["fs2", "C", "FS2: decoding strength does not remove a comprehension need", [40, 42, 91, 98, 104, 110], "The apparent contrast between a strong STAR result and difficulty with central ideas is instructionally useful. Topic recognition, fluent reading, and synthesizing meaning are different performances. Chunking and the organizer directly address that distinction; preferred seating and speech-to-text address access and production. Drawing can make a relationship visible, but it needs a bridge to evidence in the article. Do not credit the accommodation itself as proof of comprehension. The student’s generated explanation, including edits after speech-to-text, is the relevant evidence.", "Ask him to connect each chosen detail to an idea and explain why a less relevant detail was excluded. Keep assistive technology available while assessing the reasoning it helps him express."], ["fs3", "C", "FS3: connect predictability to both participation and learning", [49, 51, 94, 100, 106, 112], "The plan recognizes conversational strengths and offers a purposeful role after absences. That is more respectful and useful than treating family circumstances as a diagnosis. A predictable re-entry routine should specify how missed reading is made available and how the student can seek clarification. A discussion-leader role can support belonging, but it should not become a reward contingent on performing cheerfulness or suppressing all humor. The later 83% language and 70% summary results suggest different instructional needs; social ease and precise vocabulary do not guarantee an organized summary.", "Maintain the check-in and provide a brief catch-up text map. Then teach selecting and organizing key information, using her stronger oral language to rehearse the summary."], ["goals", "B", "Resolve one central idea versus two developed ideas", [62, 64, 66, 68, 69], "The content goal narrows RI.7.2 to a central idea and a summary, while the language goal carries the more complex requirement to analyze two ideas. This reverses the intended relationship: language should support the content reasoning rather than carry an unassessed extra content demand. A single lesson may legitimately develop part of a standard, but the writer must state that scope and avoid claiming full mastery. The four options can help students begin evaluating an interpretation. They should eventually generate or revise their own idea and explain its development across the article, rather than treat the teacher’s alternatives as the only possible meanings.", "Choose a clear lesson-sized goal and corresponding criteria. If claiming RI.7.2 fully, include two ideas and their development in the student task and assessment."], ["materials", "D", "The worksheet frame must fit informational argument", [71, 73, 79, 81], "The retained “Always Wanting More” materials include a cover/excerpt, central-idea options, two worksheet pages, and a photograph of the board. The summary frame asks for main events and refers to a character or situation. That language can push readers toward narrative retelling even though the text is informational. Likewise, listing three quoted “key textual evidence” statements for students can support entry, but it can also substitute teacher selection for the student’s evidence judgment. A more useful organizer separates claim or idea, relevant detail, and explanation of how that detail develops the idea. Academic verbs then serve a clear purpose.", "Replace story terminology with author, central ideas, supporting details, and development. Include a space for a student-selected detail and a reason for excluding an alternative."], ["teaching", "F", "The narrowed-choice response is a diagnostic finding", [134, 135, 138, 139], "The closing student reportedly explains the choice by saying the teacher eliminated two alternatives. That response establishes the effect of a scaffold, not independent comprehension. It is one of the most informative moments in the packet because it shows why the next lesson needs evidence-based justification. The rewrite correctly recognizes this limit. Preserve it rather than turning every instance of participation into proof of deep learning. The timestamps 44:41, 3:07:22, 5:04:80, and 28:84 use inconsistent formats and cannot be reliably located without the actual clips. No video was supplied for this review, including the support said to occur outside the camera’s view.", "Verify timestamps against the final clips. After narrowing options, ask the learner to identify a passage and explain why it supports the remaining choice better than another interpretation."], ["assessment", "G", "Class percentages do not reveal the scoring criteria", [143, 145, 147, 149, 151], "The reported outcomes separate content and academic language, which is valuable: about 65% reached 75% in content, compared with about 80% in language. However, the rubric, point totals, scored summaries, and class denominator are absent. The percentages therefore cannot be independently recalculated or translated into RI.7.2 mastery. FS1’s 72%/76%, FS2’s 82%/78%, and FS3’s 70%/83% reveal a plausible pattern of stronger language than summary performance for FS1 and FS3. They do not identify which summary criterion was missed. No TPA candidate score should be inferred from these classroom percentages.", "Add the classroom scoring criteria and annotated work. Report whether each score reflects an individual response or a shared group product and which supports were available."], ["reflection", "G", "Distinguish an effective support from a causal claim", [147, 149, 151, 153, 159, 161], "The reflection explains how frames, organizers, accommodations, and roles could support access. Its weakness is that each support is treated as effective largely because the student completed work or earned a percentage. A single postlesson score does not isolate the contribution of speech-to-text, a checklist, or a group role. Stronger analysis would cite the actual sentence or revision and explain the plausible mechanism cautiously. It would also examine the cost of providing answer choices: a scaffold that enables entry can simultaneously conceal whether the learner can identify an idea without it. The paragraph about just-in-time support remains general because it does not follow one misconception through correction and a subsequent independent attempt.", "Pair each effectiveness claim with a concrete student response and a limitation. Use “consistent with” or “appears to have supported” when the evidence cannot establish causation."], ["next", "H", "Reteach justification, then check independence", [165, 167, 169, 171, 175, 177], "The revised next steps are stronger than the reflection because they respond to a specific problem: choosing without explaining. FS1 needs language-supported justification; FS2 needs deeper evidence connections; FS3 needs organization while retaining participation supports. These decisions should lead to different tasks, not identical extra worksheets. For the class, model how an author develops an idea across two locations, contrast a strong and a weak explanation, and then use a short new passage without the same four options. Keep accommodations in place; independence refers to the reasoning rather than withdrawal of access supports.", "Collect a brief individual explanation after the model. Use it to decide who needs another scaffold, who can summarize independently, and who is ready to compare the development of two ideas."]], "comparison": [[553, 138, "The rewrite turns the eliminated-options episode into a meaningful limit on the learning claim. That is a substantive improvement in interpretation. It still needs the learner’s text-based explanation after the follow-up."], [563, 143, "The rewrite uses progress language more cautiously than a blanket success claim. The reported percentage remains unaudited because the criterion-level scores and population are not supplied."]], "illustration": "Illustrative revision: “The learner explained the selection by referring to the choices I had removed. That supports a claim about participation with scaffolding, not independent identification of a central idea. Next, I will ask for a passage-based explanation before offering alternatives. I will compare that response with the supported attempt to decide whether the choices can be reduced.” No later student outcome is invented.", "rubrics": [[1, "2", ["Partial — ¶61 gives a baseline with a different denominator from Part A.", "Supported — RI.7.2 is an appropriate Grade 7 standard.", "Partial — ¶64 targets one idea while the standard and ¶66 require two.", "Partial — the language goal adds content demand beyond the stated content goal.", "Supported in planning — ¶75 and group expectations support participation.", "Partial — interests are named but their role in the selected article is often unspecified.", "Supported in planning — ¶81 asks for justification and comparison.", "Partial — ¶83 describes monitoring but the scoring criteria are missing.", "Supported in planning — reading, oral rehearsal, frames, and technology provide access."], "The plan has meaningful supports and a relevant text task. The unresolved goals and evidence criteria prevent a clear Level 3 comparison.", "A 1 would disregard the coherent learning activity. Group collaboration cannot justify 4 until all Level 3 elements are clear; naming UDL checkpoints is not evidence for 5."], [2, "3", ["Supported — ¶96 connects recent summaries and direction-clarification needs.", "Supported — reading interests inform group participation; the basketball link remains proposed.", "Supported — ¶89 and ¶108 provide visible directions, vocabulary rehearsal, and written frames."], "The adaptation targets both understanding directions and explaining a relationship. The grade-equivalent score alone would not justify it, but the classroom observations strengthen the case.", "This exceeds a vague Level 2 plan. Multiple modes could support 4 when their use is clearly individualized; the evidence does not establish a research-based Level 5 rationale."], [3, "3–4", ["Supported — ¶98 separates reading strength from comprehension and attention needs.", "Supported — ¶104 uses drawing and an established reading partnership.", "Supported — ¶91 and ¶110 link chunking, an organizer and speech-to-text to the task."], "The plan is unusually specific about the barrier and preserves the comprehension goal. Visual organization, reading and oral production make 4 plausible.", "A 2 understates the clear match. A firm 4 requires explicit distinct multisensory strategies tied to FS2’s needs; 5 would require the additional research justification."], [4, "3", ["Supported — ¶100 uses absences and observed participation needs.", "Supported — conversation and a purposeful role build on strengths.", "Supported — ¶112 provides predictability and re-entry check-ins."], "The approach addresses belonging without reducing the academic target. Its rationale is stronger than a generic behavior reward.", "A 2 would miss the individualized connection. A fully specified proactive routine with triggers and follow-through would strengthen a 4; 5 is not supported."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Unverified — the commentary describes expectations and reassurance but the videos are absent.", "Partial — frames, read-aloud and discussion are described.", "Unverified — accuracy of instruction across the clips cannot be checked.", "Partial — the narrated activity broadly matches Part B."], "The documents support discussion of the design, but cannot establish all demonstrated teaching constructs.", "Do not award a level from percentages or teacher narration. A 4–5 claim requires the visible enactment and the relevant added constructs."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — the narrowed-choice episode may indicate reduced demand rather than deep reasoning.", "Partial — monitoring and redirection are narrated, but the student’s subsequent response is unavailable."], "The critique identifies a real diagnostic issue, yet the required demonstration cannot be scored without clips.", "Neither choosing an option nor receiving teacher correction automatically meets 3. Multiple monitored responses informing adjustment would be needed to consider 4."], [7, "2", ["Partial — successes are described, but the impact of narrowed choices is clearer in Part H than G.", "Supported — ¶155 links earlier summary work to planning.", "Partial — ¶153 uses outcome percentages as evidence of the environment’s effect.", "Partial — ¶157 names asset use without a student example.", "Partial — the mechanisms of frames and group work are plausible, not independently corroborated.", "Partial — no sustained student explanation establishes higher-order thinking.", "Partial — separate percentages are reported but criteria and products are absent.", "Partial — all focus students are considered, but causal claims exceed the evidence."], "The reflection offers relevant distinctions but not a complete evidence-based analysis. Its specificity is below the full Level 3 comparison.", "A 1 would ignore the identified patterns. A 3 needs criterion-level work and clear limitations; Level 4–5 additions cannot substitute for them."], [8, "3", ["Supported — ¶165 explicitly learns from selection without justification.", "Supported — ¶175 and ¶177 connect evidence reasoning with language.", "Supported — ¶167–171 differentiate the three students’ next steps.", "Supported — Clip 3’s narrated choice explanation and the reported score pattern justify the proposed focus."], "The written application makes a defensible instructional decision even though the results and clip remain reported evidence. This score concerns the quality of the documented next-step reasoning.", "A 2 would understate the explicit link between the diagnostic response and reteaching. Future asset use remains broad, limiting 4; the proposed independent check would strengthen the plan."]], "title": "Grade 7 English", "file": "06_SS_Grade_7_English_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 SS Cycle 1 (7th Grade English) Sample Submission.docx", "quotes": {"13": "[The class includes 32 students from Hispanic, Caucasian, African American, Filipino, and Asian backgrounds. I learned about them through records, conversations, and consultation with my site support provider and resource teachers. Most were born and raised in California; two were born in Mexico. Their activities include athletics, art, music, and practical trades. These experiences bring varied perspectives into our reading and discussions. Classroom lessons have given students opportunities to recognize their own cultures and learn about others.]", "17": "[Four students are working at EL level 3 and need continued practice with vocabulary and grammar. Visuals, supported texts, and explicit language instruction help them connect new concepts with what they know. Two students have been reclassified as fluent English proficient. I will differentiate the work to provide language scaffolds where needed and additional complexity where students are ready, drawing on the cultural and linguistic resources all six bring.]", "19": "[The class description identifies six English learners, ten students with IEPs, and two with 504 plans among the 32 students.]", "29": "[FS1 is American Indian and lives in a shared-residency arrangement. His 2024–2025 English learner placement is level 3. On the STAR Reading assessment during the first month of school, his result was grade 2, month 8. These results indicate a need for language and reading support with seventh-grade work.]", "31": "[FS1 asks questions and participates, and his earlier single-sentence summaries show progress in identifying important information. His work and STAR results suggest that he can engage with this lesson when support is available. He often needs directions clarified individually, so I will check that he understands the task before expecting independent work.]", "33": "[FS1 needs practice using words such as demonstrates, suggests, and indicates to explain a relationship between evidence and an idea. I want him to use the terms meaningfully in discussion and writing, rather than only recognize their definitions. The connection to ELD.PI.7.1Br is participation in discussion through relevant questions, information, and responses to others.]", "40": "[FS2 previously attended an Honors class because of his reading level. Teacher observations and administrative review suggested that a setting with more support would better meet his comprehension needs. He can identify a topic and details but needs help explaining how they form a central idea and developing that understanding in a summary.]", "42": "[FS2 has ADHD and a 504 plan. His beginning-of-year STAR Reading result was grade 7, month 9. He sits near the front facing the teacher's desk and has access to speech-to-text on a Chromebook and headphones when appropriate. These accommodations support attention and comprehension despite his stronger reading-level result.]", "49": "[FS3 completes work productively and contributes strong academic language in discussions. Her early-year STAR result was grade 6, month 6. She comprehends texts well when they match her reading level. She is kind, enjoys humor with peers, and often seems particularly comfortable interacting with adults.]", "51": "[FS3 moves between family settings under a joint-custody arrangement and often describes her grandfather as her main provider. She has also had noticeable absences. She seeks acceptance and brings energy and humor to the class, while showing resilience as she adjusts to changing circumstances. I will provide consistency and individual check-ins after absences so she can catch up without feeling singled out.]", "61": "[The STAR Reading information identifies a clear need for instruction on central ideas. In the reported group of 33, 22 students, or 66%, were beginning to understand the skill; eight, or 24%, were developing it; and three, or 9%, showed secure understanding. Students have practiced with leveled Freckle articles selected through STAR information, using Chromebooks and headphones, and have worked both independently and collaboratively.", "62": "This lesson continues that practice with structured choices and support. The content standard is RI.7.2: determining two or more central ideas, analyzing their development, and providing an objective summary. The language connection is ELD.PI.7.10.b.Ex, which addresses increasingly concise summaries using complete sentences and key words. Choice and appropriate reading support are intended to help students engage with the task.]", "64": "[Students will identify a central idea in an informational text and write an accurate, objective summary. They will communicate the important information in their own words and use complete sentences and academic vocabulary. This brings the comprehension focus of RI.7.2 together with the summary-writing focus of ELD.PI.7.10.b.Ex.]", "66": "[Students will examine how two or more central ideas develop, identify relevant evidence, and explain the connections using words such as suggests, indicates, and represents. They will apply that language in an objective summary so the relationship between evidence and interpretation is clear.]", "68": "[I will introduce the goal, display four central-idea choices, and model a sentence frame. After clarifying central idea and objective summary, I will organize group reading. Students will annotate, discuss meaning, and ask one another questions while I circulate and offer targeted prompts.", "69": "Groups will select an option and record two or three supporting pieces of evidence. Sentence starters will be available when a group needs help beginning. Volunteers will then present their central idea and summary, and I will give focused feedback or clarify a misunderstanding.]", "71": "[Students will read a printed informational article from the Collections textbook. Paper copies allow annotation and notes during group reading. Four possible central ideas provide a starting point for analysis, and sentence frames support the written summary. These materials let students focus on connecting an interpretation with evidence while discussing the text with peers.]", "73": "[Group reading and discussion build on students' earlier collaborative text work. They can hear an idea, test it orally, and then write. The four choices narrow the initial task without removing the need to evaluate evidence. Sentence frames support organization for English learners and developing writers while leaving them responsible for what the summary says. These choices reflect the collaboration and representation supports identified in UDL 8.3 and 1.2.]", "75": "[I will make group participation predictable and supportive, with space to ask questions and share an unfinished idea. Choice options and sentence frames provide guidance when students are unsure how to begin. Reading, discussion, and writing offer several ways to engage. The intention, consistent with the UDL supports identified as 4.1 and 5.4, is to make academic risk-taking more comfortable.]", "79": "[Students will encounter the vocabulary while reading, hear it in discussion, and practice it in writing. I will model how a word such as suggests connects a piece of evidence to an interpretation. Frames and rehearsal will help students move toward a clear explanation rather than simply insert an academic term. These opportunities support the engagement and reflection described in UDL 8.4 and 9.3.]", "81": "[I will ask students to explain how central ideas relate, why a detail matters, and which evidence best supports an interpretation. I will also ask them to condense the article's main point into two sentences and consider how the meaning would change if an important idea were removed. These questions require justification, attention to the author's choices, and analysis beyond selecting an answer.]", "83": "[I will compare what students say during group work with what they write independently. Their discussions will show how they identify and test a central idea; the summaries will show evidence use, organization, and academic language. Quick questions and redirection to the text will provide feedback during the task. The combined evidence will help me identify who is ready to move forward and who needs more practice.]", "89": "[I will give FS1 short, visible directions and a checklist. Before the task, we will review the target vocabulary, and sentence frames will support his written explanation. A role in a supportive group will give him a reason to practice the language aloud. I will check his understanding individually and clarify directions when needed so he can concentrate on comprehension.]", "91": "[FS2 will read the article in smaller sections with guiding questions. A graphic organizer will help him connect the topic and evidence to a central idea. An active role, such as group reader, will draw on his interest in books. He may use the shared sentence frames, with prompts to extend his explanation. Speech-to-text will help him record an idea before editing it, reducing a writing barrier while retaining the comprehension work.]", "94": "[FS3 will have a purposeful group role, such as discussion leader or summarizer, that uses her confidence in speaking. I will check that her humor supports the group rather than distracts it. Predictability, reassurance, and a clear responsibility will help her feel included while she continues developing comprehension and constructive peer relationships.]", "96": "[FS1's STAR result of grade 2.8 and EL level 3 support the need for scaffolds with seventh-grade texts and language. His work shows that he can identify important ideas and write a short summary, but directions often need individual clarification. The checklist, smaller steps, vocabulary preview, and conferences respond to those observed needs while building on his effort and interest in learning.]", "98": "[FS2's STAR result of grade 7.9 shows reading strength, while classroom work shows difficulty connecting details to a central idea. My observations and those of his case managers also identify attention needs. Preferred seating, speech-to-text, and headphones support access; shorter reading sections and an organizer address the particular comprehension task. I want to challenge his reading while giving him a structure for understanding it.]", "100": "[FS3's grade 6.6 STAR result, completed work, and classroom contributions show a solid foundation, especially with appropriately leveled texts. Her home circumstances, absences, and need for acceptance also point to the importance of consistency. A meaningful group role and check-ins will support connection to class while keeping the academic expectations in place.]", "102": "[Basketball and sports can provide familiar examples for FS1 when discussing a central idea. His enjoyment of books and library activities also gives him material he wants to talk about. I will use those interests to create opportunities for explaining a text with demonstrates, suggests, and indicates.]", "104": "[FS2 may draw a representation of the central idea before writing. This uses his interest in art to organize comprehension. His reading habits and productive relationship with FS1 will also support evidence-based discussion. Their shared book interests give them a natural setting for practicing the academic vocabulary.]", "106": "[I will connect FS3's interest in travel, food, and personal stories with opportunities to discuss a text. A structured role will let her contribute her ideas and feel included while practicing academic explanations. Her enthusiasm for conversation can become a resource for both comprehension and language development.]", "108": "[FS1 will encounter the vocabulary in a model, hear it in discussion, practice it orally, and use it in writing. Short directions and sentence frames will reduce the work of organizing a sentence so he can focus on what the text means. Group participation and individual feedback will help him use the terms for a real explanatory purpose.]", "110": "[The four central-idea options will focus FS2's attention on evaluating evidence. Preferred seating, manageable reading sections, and an organizer will support concentration and comprehension. Speech-to-text and headphones will remain available when appropriate. Together, these supports let him use his reading strengths to develop a fuller summary.]", "112": "[FS3 will have a predictable, supportive environment and structured opportunities to use her speaking strengths. Discussion roles, frames, and connections to her interests can help her direct her social energy toward the task. I will continue checking in after absences and provide consistency so she can participate without feeling left out.]", "134": "[I begin by reviewing the reading and inviting clarification about possible central ideas. At the moment labeled 44:41, I read and point out the four choices. From 1:16–2:00, I explain both worksheet pages and their sentence frames. At 2:30, I connect the choices to the paragraph's final sentence.", "135": "At 3:07:22, I encourage students to share their reasoning with the group. At 3:22, I remind them that I will circulate and help. When group work begins at 3:55, I listen for understanding and participation. At 3:57, I redirect a group that is drifting from the task; at 4:48, I clarify for another group. The audio at 5:04:80 records further support outside the camera's frame.", "138": "[At :15, I recognize that class is nearly over but still invite answers so students receive feedback that day. At 28:84, one student says he selected his central idea because I had eliminated two alternatives. His response shows participation, but it also tells me that he needs more support explaining a choice from the text itself.", "139": "At 50:93, I redirect the class as the period closes. The bell at 1:25:47 interrupts the discussion, so I return to it the next day. Listening to students at the close gives me information for that follow-up and helps preserve a sense that their thinking matters.]", "143": "[The lesson helped most students progress toward identifying a central idea and writing an objective summary. Group discussion let them work through the text before writing, and sentence frames supported organization. I monitored conversations and reviewed the written responses. Sixty-five percent earned at least 75% on the content assessment; other students showed partial success. These results support continuing the approach while targeting the explanation and writing that remain difficult.]", "145": "[The language goal asked students to connect evidence and ideas using precise terms. Modeling, sentence frames, and opportunities to talk before writing supported that work. About 80% earned at least 75% on the academic-language assignment, with partial progress among the remaining students. Reading, listening, discussion, and writing provided several opportunities to use the vocabulary in context, consistent with the planned UDL supports.]", "147": "[FS1's checklist, short directions, vocabulary preview, sentence frames, and individual conferences helped him participate and complete the task. He earned 72% on the objective summary and 76% on academic language. Those results show useful progress while also indicating that the skills are not yet fully secure. I would retain support and provide more practice using the vocabulary to explain an idea independently.]", "149": "[Smaller reading sections, guiding questions, an organizer, and access to his accommodations helped FS2 focus on meaning. Speech-to-text gave him a way to record ideas before revising. He earned 82% on the summary and 78% on academic language. The structure appears to have supported both comprehension and expression while allowing him to use his reading strengths.]", "151": "[FS3's role in discussion and the predictable support after absences helped her stay connected to the lesson. She earned 70% on the summary and 83% on academic language. Her stronger language result shows that she can use precise vocabulary, while the summary result points to a need for more organization and focus. I would continue the social-emotional supports alongside that targeted academic work.]", "153": "[Small groups gave students room to ask questions and try an idea before speaking to the class. Clear choices and frames reduced uncertainty, while reading, talking, and writing offered different ways to engage. I circulated, recognized effort, and used brief checks to provide help before frustration grew. The 65% at or above 75% on the content assessment, along with partial success elsewhere, suggests that the environment supported participation and progress.]", "155": "[Students already had experience finding central ideas, writing summaries, and discussing texts through gallery walks and Socratic Seminars. I built on those routines with both collaborative and individual work. Their earlier summaries and feedback helped me identify where the new lesson needed stronger support for explanation and academic language.]", "157": "[Students' cultural backgrounds and interests guided my attention to varied perspectives and texts about society and culture. Their preference for collaboration shaped the group activities, while independent options supported students who work more comfortably alone. I wanted students to draw on familiar experiences while encountering ideas beyond their own.]", "159": "[Earlier work showed that students needed more consistent use of demonstrates, suggests, and indicates. I made those words part of the models, frames, and discussion prompts. Partner and group work also provided practice taking turns, asking relevant questions, and building on another person's idea, connecting with ELD.PI.7.1Br.]", "161": "[I watched for where students became stuck and responded with a model, a shorter explanation, a written frame, or encouragement to speak. When vocabulary use was unclear, I supplied an example in context. During discussion, I supported quieter students and redirected attention to the text. These immediate adjustments let students continue working on the central idea rather than remain blocked by the language or directions.]", "165": "[I would reteach the connection between a central idea and the evidence that supports it. Many students could choose an answer, especially with four options available, but some could not explain why it fit. Others still relied heavily on frames. The closing exchange in Clip 3 helped make that need visible. I would model an evidence-based explanation and provide practice with gradually less support while keeping scaffolds available where needed.]", "167": "[FS1 needs focused practice using academic vocabulary to explain a central idea. His 72% summary result and 76% language result show progress and continuing need. I will keep the short directions, checklist, vocabulary preparation, and conferences, then gradually reduce the language I supply. A small group with supportive peers can give him repeated opportunities to rehearse an explanation before writing it independently.]", "168": "c. For Focus Student 2:", "169": "[FS2 does not need the full lesson repeated. His 82% summary and 78% language results support more targeted work on connecting evidence to the central idea and explaining that connection in depth. I will retain attention supports while asking him to move beyond naming the topic or repeating a detail.]", "170": "d. For Focus Student 3:", "171": "[FS3 needs practice organizing and focusing a summary, rather than a complete reteaching of the lesson. Her 83% language result is a strength to build on, while the 70% summary result identifies the next area of work. I will keep the predictable environment and purposeful discussion roles and add focused summary practice so academic growth continues alongside social-emotional support.]", "175": "[I will have students compare interpretations, analyze how two pieces of evidence connect to an idea, and evaluate which support is stronger. They will need to explain a judgment rather than simply locate a matching detail. These tasks will deepen the reading work and give students a clearer purpose for an objective summary.]", "177": "[Academic vocabulary will remain part of both discussion and writing. I will model words in context, provide frames, word banks, or organizers when needed, and give feedback on the precision of an explanation. Listening to conversations and reviewing writing will help me decide when to strengthen or reduce the language support.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"553": "[In video clip 3, I built a positive learning environment by making sure students had the chance to share their thinking and by giving them feedback before the class ended. At :15, I realized the period was almost over, but I still wanted to hear student answers and provide same-day feedback. I knew this was important because it showed students that their voices matter and it gave me the information I needed to plan future lessons and reteach where needed. At that same moment, I asked the class to quiet down because they were starting to get restless as the bell was about to ring. Normally, I give one minute to pack up, but on this day, I mistakenly let the work continue until the bell. Even though this was a misstep, my focus on hearing their ideas helped sustain a positive environment because students could see that their learning was valued. To actively engage students, I used UDL-focused strategies that gave them multiple ways to approach the central idea task. At 28:84, one student shared his central idea answer and explained that he picked it because I had told him two of the other options were wrong. For this particular student, narrowing the options from four to two provided the right level of scaffolding to help him move forward without feeling overwhelmed. Even though he still needed support in explaining his reasoning, this approach showed how giving students different levels of access points can help them engage with the lesson at their own pace. These strategies promoted higher-order thinking because students weren’t just picking an answer, they were asked to explain why their choice made sense. Even though the student at 28:84 needed extra support, the act of justifying his answer pushed him to think more about evidence and reasoning, which directly tied into the content-specific learning goal of identifying and supporting a central idea through a summary. The strategies also supported the ALD learning goal because students were practicing how to explain their thinking with academic language. When I asked them to share not just their answer but also the “why” behind it, they had to use academic words and sentence structures. Even when answers weren’t fully developed, the opportunity to speak and practice the language was valuable. To monitor progress, I used questioning and active listening. By asking for answers at the end of class :15, I could immediately check for understanding and know which students might need reteaching. At 28:84, listening to how a student explained his choice gave me insight into where he needed more support. At 50:93, I provided redirection as the class was winding down, which kept the focus on learning rather than behavior. Finally, I adjusted my instruction by carrying the unfinished class discussion into the next day. At 1:25:47, when the bell rang and we had not finished, I knew it was important to give students closure, so I made sure to pick it up right away the following day. This adjustment showed students that their work mattered and that I wasn’t going to rush through their learning just because the period ended. Since I included various learning points of scaffolding, feedback, redirection, and continuing the lesson, this supported students in making progress toward both their content knowledge and their academic language development.]", "563": "[In section 1.b of Step 1–Part B, the content-specific goal was for students to find the central idea of an informational text (RI.7.2) and write an objective summary using academic language (ELD.PI.7.10.b.Ex). The lesson was effective in helping most students make progress toward this goal because it gave them clear strategies and support to succeed. Students practiced identifying the “main point” of the text and then used sentence frames to organize their ideas into a strong summary. In section 4.a of Step 1–Part B, the UDL focus helped make the task accessible for everyone. Group work allowed students to talk through the text, listen to one another, and build confidence before writing (UDL 8.3). Providing four possible central ideas gave students a starting point, which reduced frustration and helped them practice using evidence without getting stuck. Sentence frames (UDL 1.2) gave students, especially English learners and struggling writers, a clear structure for expressing their ideas. I monitored progress by listening to group discussions, checking how students used the sentence frames, and reviewing their written summaries. The results showed growth: 65% of the class earned a 75% or higher on the assessment, while others showed partial success. Academically, students improved their ability to find and explain the central idea, and emotionally, they grew by working in groups, supporting one another, and feeling more confident in their reading and writing."}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}, "ss-c2": {"id": "ss-c2", "lens": "ss-c2", "thesis": "The unit moves coherently from number representation to operations and contextual problems. The strongest evidence points to a transfer problem: students can explain procedures with support but do not consistently execute them independently. The marked test also reveals scoring and item-reference issues that the prose alone conceals.", "version": "The source is the 2025 V1 SS Cycle 2 rational-number example, with four unit lessons. The comparison uses the saved 2026–27 SS Cycle 2 V2.1 guide, which specifies a three-lesson unit and explicitly assesses both content and ELD in the summative criteria. V1 descriptors were not verified. Current-guide comparisons are separated from the historical packet’s requirements and do not constitute a V1 rescore.", "standards": [["7.NS.2d, not “7.NA.2.d”", "Convert rational numbers to decimals by long division and understand termination or repetition.", "Lesson 1 directly teaches conversion, but the cited domain NA is incorrect. Decimal-to-fraction conversion is related prerequisite/representation work, not the whole demand of .2d.", "Use a terminating and repeating example and ask why a repeated remainder produces repetition.", "Strong task connection; correct the code and include conceptual explanation.", "math", 56], ["7.NS.1", "Extend addition/subtraction to rational numbers, including representation and interpretation.", "Lesson 2 emphasizes sign rules and calculation. Number-line interpretation and subtraction as adding the inverse need explicit attention.", "Use the pool-distance item to distinguish signed position from distance and justify |2 − (−15)| = 17.", "Procedural alignment; broader conceptual demand only partly developed.", "math", 55], ["7.NS.2", "Extend multiplication/division to rational numbers, using properties and interpreting results.", "Lesson 3 models products, quotients and signs. A rule recital does not explain why dividing by a fraction uses a reciprocal.", "Ask for a representation or inverse check as well as a calculation.", "Direct procedure alignment; conceptual evidence limited.", "math", 56], ["7.NS.3", "Solve mathematical and real-world problems using the four rational-number operations.", "Lesson 4’s game score and the test’s pool/loss problems require interpretation.", "Require an equation, an answer with sign/unit, and an explanation of its meaning.", "Relevant application tasks; written language criteria need strengthening.", "math", 56], ["ELD.PI.7.1.Ex and PI.7.5.Ex", "Contribute to discussion and demonstrate active listening through detailed questions/responses.", "Partner talk and teacher questioning provide opportunities. A silent calculation test cannot directly measure all these oral goals.", "Record a relevant question, paraphrase or response during discussion, with support level.", "Planned opportunities; separate oral evidence is needed.", "eld", [109, 110]], ["ELD.PI.7.6a.Ex and PI.7.6c.Ex", "Explain text relationships; use morphology/context/reference/visual information to clarify language.", "Word problems can support close interpretation, but highlighting operation words alone is insufficient. The situation, not a keyword, determines the operation.", "Ask students to explain the relation among quantities and justify the sign and operation in context.", "Partial alignment; distinguish interpreting language from producing an explanation.", "eld", 110], ["Mathematical reasoning; no MP code explicitly selected", "Justify a method and check reasonableness.", "Questions about operation choice can elicit reasoning beyond memorized rules.", "An inverse calculation, number line or contextual check makes the reasoning inspectable.", "Useful analytical lens, not an additional standard claimed by the source.", "math", 8]], "sections": [["context", "A", "Describe the language of instruction and the actual baseline", [12, 14, 16, 20, 27, 31, 33, 41], "“NA” does not answer which language is used for instruction. That matters in a unit whose goals include listening and mathematical explanation. The focus student’s oral reasoning, sports interests and attention supports can inform planning without treating ADHD as the automatic cause of every error. A prior B− average and a broad statement that integers were successful do not reveal whether fraction equivalence, division setup or sign interpretation are secure. The later conversion difficulty suggests that a brief targeted baseline would have been more useful than assuming integer success transfers to every rational-number procedure.", "Specify instructional language and collect a conversion, a signed-number explanation and a contextual interpretation before the unit. Record independent work separately from supported oral responses."], ["lesson1", "B", "Conversion: teach why the numerator is the dividend", [49, 51, 53, 55, 59, 61, 63, 65], "Ordering numbers can activate useful magnitude knowledge before long division. The model then needs to connect a/b with a ÷ b, rather than teach a placement rule without meaning. The later misconception—putting the larger number inside—shows why examples should include a numerator smaller than the denominator. The follow-up’s 8/2 example alone would not discriminate that misconception because the numerator is also larger. Use 3/4 or 3/11 and estimate the quotient’s size first. Guided notes can reduce copying demands for FS, but the independent attempt must still reveal whether he chooses the setup correctly.", "Contrast 8/2 with 3/4, then ask the learner to explain the placement before calculating. Include a repeating result and connect the repeating remainder to the decimal pattern."], ["lesson2", "B", "Addition/subtraction: distinguish rules from signed quantities", [71, 73, 75, 79, 81, 83, 85, 87], "The volcano and elevation settings can make positive and negative positions meaningful. Repeating “same signs” and “different signs” can support recall, but may conceal whether students understand subtraction as adding an opposite. A learner who subtracts 2 from 15 in the pool problem may be ignoring reference position, not simply forgetting a sign rule. A number line with −15, 0 and +2 makes the two distances visible. The language work should explain what each number represents before selecting an operation. This also creates a useful reason to distinguish position, change and distance.", "Have students annotate the quantities, draw the reference point, write an equation and explain why the distance is nonnegative. Use a new above/below context to check transfer."], ["lesson3", "B", "Multiplication/division: separate conversion, operation and sign errors", [93, 95, 97, 101, 103, 105, 107, 109], "A wrong quotient may arise from an incorrect mixed-number conversion, failure to use a reciprocal, a multiplication error, or an incorrect sign. Those errors require different feedback. The example’s later work on −3 3/5 ÷ 2 7/10 contains an annotation about improper fractions, indicating that the initial representation needs attention. Asking only whether the signs match can miss that earlier problem. The rain-barrel and descending-hiker contexts should connect the result to a rate or change, not merely decorate a calculation. Partner explanation is useful rehearsal, followed by a comparable individual problem to test whether the steps transfer.", "Use a short error-analysis grid: representation, operation, computation, sign and contextual meaning. Ask the learner to check the quotient by multiplication."], ["lesson4", "B", "Model the situation before searching for operation words", [115, 117, 119, 121, 123, 125, 127, 129], "The game awards +2¼ for a correct response and −½ for an incorrect one. This gives students a reason to combine multiplication and addition, provided they explain how the number of each response contributes to the total. A keyword strategy can fail: “total” need not always mean adding every visible number, and “loss” identifies a signed quantity rather than an operation by itself. The boat-lock and trivia examples should elicit a representation of the changing quantities. Language supports are most useful when they help students express those relationships, not just name product or quotient.", "Ask for an equation with labeled quantities before calculation and a sentence interpreting the final result. Change one condition and ask the learner to predict how the equation changes."], ["formative", "C", "The checks must cover the whole unit and both kinds of goals", [143, 145, 150, 152, 154, 156], "The seven-minute independent practice followed by discussion can distinguish first attempts from supported explanations, if both are retained. The source later admits that formative review emphasized later operations while conversion gaps remained. That is an alignment problem: a cumulative assessment requires cumulative sampling. The 20-item test contains conversions, comparisons, operations, and two contextual questions. It does not directly measure turn-taking, asking detailed questions or oral paraphrasing. Interpreting a word problem supplies some receptive-language evidence, but it should not stand in for every ELD goal named in Part B.", "Use brief cumulative checks and a separate observation of mathematical explanation. Define how each item or observation addresses a stated content or language goal."], ["test", "G", "The marked 9/20 paper needs an item-level audit", [182, 183, 191, 192, 199, 203], "The retained paper is marked 9/20, or 45%. That is distinct from the reported class average of about 77%. The displayed deductions appear to total 11 points, consistent with the recorded total, but some unmarked answers need review. Question 9 appears to give −2/3 where −5/3 + 2 1/3 = +2/3. Question 16 shows $22 for a requested quotient representing a loss; −110/5 = −22 is the signed quotient, although “$22 loss” could communicate the contextual magnitude if explicitly stated. The paper does not clearly supply that qualifier. This review therefore flags potential scoring inconsistency rather than silently replacing the teacher’s score under an invented rule.", "Apply one documented partial-credit rule to every item, distinguish magnitude from signed representation, and retain both the recorded total and any justified revised total."], ["item-reference", "I", "Resolve question 5 versus question 7", [199, 203], "One paragraph names question 5 as successful contextual work; another names question 7. The actual test shows that question 5 is a comparison of negative mixed numbers, while question 7 is the pool-distance problem. The paper contains 17 feet for question 7, supporting the latter reference. Question 5 appears to use the wrong comparison symbol. This is a material contradiction because the reflection uses the item as evidence that contextual modeling worked. The rewrite has preserved both references rather than resolving them. The analysis must identify the conflict and use the visible item when discussing the instructional inference.", "Correct the analysis reference to the actual item and explain what 17 feet does—and does not—show. It supports the result on that problem, not mastery of every contextual operation."], ["teaching", "F", "A rule reminder is not always a conceptual adjustment", [168, 171, 172, 174, 175, 177, 178], "The commentary describes re-modeling a decimal/fraction problem, questioning operation choices and responding to calculation errors. These are useful teaching moves, but a pause to repeat a rule may address recall without clarifying the concept behind it. The analysis should trace a learner’s initial explanation and subsequent response to determine which occurred. The time label 00:87 is not conventional minutes:seconds; it needs checking against the actual clip rather than automatic conversion. No clips were supplied, so the quality of demonstrated discussion and adjustment cannot be scored.", "Choose a response that reveals the misconception, explain why the adjustment fits it, and show the student’s next attempt. Verify all timestamps against the final video."], ["feedback", "H", "Feedback needs a procedure and a meaning check", [182, 183, 184], "Circling signs and using a checklist can help a student notice features that were missed while rushing. Those supports should complement an explanation of why the sign is appropriate. Otherwise a learner may circle a negative symbol and still apply the wrong rule. The feedback also distinguishes partial-credit sign errors from work that did not begin correctly, which could be useful if applied consistently across the test. It should name an actual item and ask the student to revise it, then solve a similar problem independently. Do not assume every blank or erroneous response was caused by attention rather than missing understanding.", "Pair the checklist with a number-line, inverse-operation or context check. Collect the revision and a new independent attempt under the planned 504 accommodations."], ["reflection", "I", "The evidence supports a transfer concern, not a single cause", [191, 192, 194, 196, 199, 201, 205], "The class error descriptions are more informative than the average: larger-number division setup, distance across zero, unnecessary common denominators, and confusion about quotient. Each suggests a different instructional response. The focus student’s contrast between supported discussion and independent work makes gradual release and cumulative retrieval plausible priorities. Rushing may contribute, but the record does not isolate its causal role. The 45% paper also cautions against describing the learner’s understanding as secure solely from oral participation. The next instruction should retain strengths in explanation while checking whether the underlying procedure and interpretation are available without the same prompts.", "Report errors by criterion and condition. Use supported oral reasoning diagnostically, then compare a short independent response before deciding what to reteach."], ["followup", "J", "Reteach conversion with a discriminating example", [212, 214, 216, 217, 219, 221, 223, 225], "Selecting conversion for reteaching is justified by the early test items and the missed formative coverage. Connecting the fraction bar to division is conceptually stronger than repeating a placement rule. However, 8/2 does not challenge a learner who believes the larger number always goes inside. Follow it with a fraction less than one and ask for an estimate and setup before division. Decimal-to-fraction work should connect the final place value to the denominator and then to equivalence, not simply attach zeros mechanically. Sports contexts can motivate practice, but the immediate diagnostic target is the representation.", "Use 3/4 and 3/11 after 8/2, then a terminating decimal. Ask the learner to explain the setup, calculate, and check magnitude independently."], ["followup-evidence", "L", "Whole-class correct responses do not establish FS transfer", [236, 237], "The commentary explicitly challenges the larger-number misconception and revisits place value after an incorrect answer. That is a coherent response to the assessment. It still does not identify the focus student’s answer or provide a later independent conversion. A class chorus can conceal individual uncertainty. The written account can justify the next teaching move, but cannot establish that FS now carries out the sequence independently. The absent video also prevents evaluation of the demonstrated reteaching construct.", "End with an individually attributable setup, solution and explanation on a new fraction and decimal. Record the prompt level and use the result to plan further practice."]], "comparison": [[612, 199, "The rewrite makes the conflicting item attribution visible but does not resolve it. The retained paper shows that the contextual success is question 7; question 5 is a comparison task. The new analysis corrects the inference while preserving the supplied prose."], [586, 192, "The revised explanation names a specific division misconception rather than only a low score. The follow-up still needs an example with a numerator smaller than its denominator to test whether that misconception has changed."]], "illustration": "Illustrative revision grounded in the retained artifact: “Question 7 shows 17 feet for the distance between −15 and +2. Question 5 is a different task, comparing negative mixed numbers, and should not be cited as contextual success. I will use the conversion errors to plan reteaching, then check a fraction below one so that a larger-number-first strategy cannot produce the correct setup by accident.”", "rubrics": [[1, "2", ["Partial — prior integer results are named without a specific diagnostic baseline.", "Partial — 7.NA.2d is miscoded; other content and ELD standards are relevant.", "Supported — the four lessons have explicit content goals.", "Supported in planning — language goals connect discussion and interpretation to mathematics.", "Supported but incomplete in coverage — each lesson has checks, but conversion was not adequately revisited.", "Partial — the written test samples content and receptive language but not all stated ELD goals.", "Partial — partial credit is described, not fully specified or consistently evidenced.", "Partial — item references and scoring interpretation require repair."], "The unit has clear mathematical intent but incomplete assessment alignment and evidence criteria. These prevent the full Level 3 comparison.", "A 1 would ignore the coherent tasks. A 4 cannot follow from multiple checks while the summative and scoring constructs remain incomplete."], [2, "2–3", ["Supported — sports, games and science contexts offer relevant entry points.", "Supported — visuals, oral models, notes and breaks support access.", "Version difference — the source has four lessons; current V2 specifies three. The progression is coherent but is not a literal current-format match.", "Supported — students solve, discuss and apply the operations.", "Supported in planning — explanations and vocabulary are integrated.", "Supported — FS’s oral strengths and attention needs inform notes, partners and breaks."], "A 3 describes the substantive design if separated from the current lesson-count requirement. A literal V2 comparison remains limited by the four-lesson structure.", "A 2 is the conservative current-format comparison. Contexts suggest the asset addition at 4, but no higher level is assigned while the base version mismatch is unresolved."], [3, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — ¶168–178 describes reasoning questions, but some are rule recall.", "Partial — the narration matches the planned unit; clips are absent."], "The account supports analysis of intended cognitive demand, not a score for demonstrated deep learning.", "A correct procedure alone does not establish 3; responsive conceptual development at 4 requires the actual student-teacher exchange."], [4, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — game/sports contexts are described.", "Partial — visuals, partner talk and notes are described.", "Unverified — actual student engagement cannot be inspected."], "The design is relevant to student interests, but demonstration and description must both be available for a level.", "A familiar context does not automatically establish the intentional incorporation required for 4."], [5, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — mathematical terms and discussion are narrated, but no actual language-development interaction can be viewed."], "The question is whether language instruction helps students express and understand mathematical relationships. The packet cannot demonstrate that performance.", "Vocabulary modeling alone does not establish 3. The additional asset rationale at 4 and translanguaging at 5 are not demonstrated by the available record."], [6, "Insufficient evidence", ["Partial — independent practice, circulating and discussion are described.", "Partial — re-modeling is described without a complete before/after student response."], "The written decision sequence is plausible. The observable monitoring and adjustment cannot be assigned a level without clips.", "Multiple checks in a plan do not establish 4; a clear pattern and immediate differentiated response would also be needed for 5."], [7, "2", ["Partial — the class average and focus-student paper provide evidence, but scoring and item references conflict.", "Supported in part — specific misconceptions are identified, but some causal explanations remain speculative.", "Supported in part — circling signs and a checklist provide concrete actions; item-level consistency needs review.", "Partial — supported participation is sometimes used to infer independent learning.", "Supported — ¶196 and ¶205 propose cumulative and independent checks."], "The reflection is stronger when it names errors, but the unresolved score audit and question-reference conflict prevent a clear full Level 3 analysis.", "A 1 would disregard substantial diagnostic information. A 3–4 requires reconciled item evidence and feedback anchored to it, not just a class average."], [8, "Insufficient evidence", ["Supported as planning — conversion reteaching responds to identified gaps.", "Unverified — the follow-up video and FS’s independent response are absent.", "Supported in part — early test errors justify conversion; the initial 8/2 example does not discriminate the misconception."], "The plan has a clear purpose and a better conceptual explanation. Its enacted effectiveness and required demonstration cannot be scored.", "Sports contexts may support the Level 4 addition, but cannot substitute for the base demonstration and an evidence-grounded follow-up decision."]], "title": "Grade 7 rational numbers", "file": "05_SS_Cycle_2_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 SS CalTPA Cycle 2 Sample Submission.docx", "quotes": {"12": "[About half the class identifies as Caucasian; other students have Hispanic, Black, Asian, and Middle Eastern backgrounds. Spanish and Chinese are the most common home languages among bilingual students. The class includes students receiving English-language support and one RFEP student with strong oral communication. Most families are middle class and actively support school achievement. These varied cultural, language, and family experiences give students resources to draw on in discussion and practical mathematics.]", "14": "[Most students have completed sixth-grade mathematics and know the basic operations. Accuracy is less consistent when they work without a calculator or multiplication chart. Word problems are another area of need: students often require help identifying relevant information, understanding the question, and representing the situation mathematically. The lesson sequence will address these demands alongside rational-number procedures.]", "16": "[One identified English learner speaks Spanish.]", "20": "[NA]", "27": "[FS comes from a large blended family and values respect. He enjoys talking and working with peers and can explain his ideas comfortably aloud. Sports and games are strong interests, especially when a problem involves scores or statistics. Purposeful interaction and movement will help connect those strengths with his 504 supports and the mathematics.]", "31": "[FS completed sixth-grade math with an average of B–. He brings knowledge of whole numbers, fractions, decimals, ratios, proportions, and early algebra. He learns new ideas quickly and reasons well, particularly when working with a teacher or partner. Independent work is harder to sustain, even when he can explain the same process accurately in conversation.]", "33": "[FS's ADHD affects attention, impulse control, and the ability to remain seated for long periods. He may call out or lose interest when he understands a concept before classmates are ready to move on. Organization and completing work at home are also concerns. His oral reasoning is a strength, but he sometimes substitutes everyday terms for mathematical ones, such as top and bottom for numerator and denominator. He needs explicit vocabulary practice alongside attention and work-completion supports.]", "41": "[Earlier integer assessments showed that students could apply the four operations. I will build on those procedures as the number forms expand to fractions and decimals. This preserves a familiar foundation while increasing the demands of calculation and practical problem solving.]", "49": "[CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NA.2.d: Convert a rational number to a decimal using long division; know that the decimal form of a rational number terminates in 0s or eventually repeats.]", "51": "[Students will use long division to express fractions as decimals and place value to express decimals as fractions in simplest form.]", "53": "[ELD.PI.7.5.Ex: Listening actively: Demonstrate active listening in oral presentation activities by asking and answering detailed questions, with occasional prompting and moderate support.]", "55": "[Students will listen to conversion explanations and ask or answer detailed questions using the mathematical vocabulary needed to describe the process.]", "59": "[Students will begin by ordering positive and negative fractions and decimals independently. Volunteers will explain their choices, and I will use questions or a number-line model to clarify comparisons. I will then demonstrate fraction-to-decimal conversion through long division. Students will try a problem and explain the steps. Next, I will model converting a decimal to a fraction using place value and reducing the result. Discussion and immediate feedback will address errors as they appear.]", "61": "[During independent conversion and ordering practice, I will circulate, review work, and ask students to explain a decision. I will model again when needed and ask students who are ready to justify a comparison or strategy more fully. Whole-class review will let me assess both the procedure and the language used to explain it. Those observations will guide additional support or extension.]", "63": "[I will connect familiar integer vocabulary with rational and irrational numbers, then introduce terminating and repeating decimals through examples. As I model long division on the board, I will explain each step using the terms students need. Questions and student explanations will give them opportunities to use the language while doing the mathematics.]", "65": "[Clear visuals, step-by-step models, and consistent vocabulary will support the class. FS will have guided notes with partly completed examples so he can attend to the process without an excessive reading or copying demand. Purposeful partner interaction will let him explain his reasoning, remain engaged, and receive clarification while practicing the skill.]", "71": "[CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NS.1: Apply and extend previous understandings of addition and subtraction to add and subtract rational numbers.]", "73": "[Students will calculate sums and differences involving integers, fractions, and decimals.]", "75": "[ELD.PI.7.1.Ex: Exchanging information/ideas: Contribute to class, group, and partner discussions by following turn-taking rules, asking relevant questions, affirming others, adding relevant information, and paraphrasing key ideas. ELD.PI.7.5.Ex: Listening actively: Demonstrate active listening in oral presentation activities by asking and answering detailed questions, with occasional prompting and moderate support.]", "79": "[A volcano problem will ask students to consider the distance between a summit and an underground reservoir. Another example will combine gravity and thrust acting on a rocket. These contexts connect rational-number operations with interests in Earth science, natural events, and physical systems.]", "81": "[The warm-up will revisit comparisons and integer addition and subtraction. We will recall the meanings of integer and rational number, then apply familiar sign rules to fractions and decimals. I will model the examples and give students practice using the same reasoning. Circulating during work will let me check understanding and provide support where a step is unclear.]", "83": "[Students will solve projected examples in their workbooks. I will check accuracy and ask how the signs helped them decide what to do. Their explanations will show both procedural understanding and use of the lesson vocabulary. Feedback will identify correct reasoning and help students revise an error or make an explanation more precise.]", "85": "[I will revisit integer and rational number and display terms including sum, difference, absolute value, and commutative property. Modeling the vocabulary while working through examples will connect what students hear with what they see. Discussion will provide opportunities to use the terms in their own explanations.]", "87": "[I will combine visible examples, oral explanations, and opportunities to respond. FS may take a one- or two-minute break, such as getting water or briefly walking outside, when needed for attention and regulation. I will read directions and problems aloud and use partner interaction to support reasoning and engagement. Consistent vocabulary and guided steps will help students follow the process.]", "93": "[CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NS.2: Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division and of fractions to multiply and divide rational numbers.]", "95": "[Students will find products and quotients involving integers, fractions, and decimals.]", "97": "[ELD.PI.7.1.Ex: Exchanging information/ideas: Contribute to class, group, and partner discussions by following turn-taking rules, asking relevant questions, affirming others, adding relevant information, and paraphrasing key ideas. ELD.PI.7.5.Ex: Listening actively: Demonstrate active listening in oral presentation activities by asking and answering detailed questions, with occasional prompting and moderate support.]", "101": "[The descending-hikers example connects changes in elevation with physical activity and familiar outdoor situations. A rain-barrel problem connects division with water draining over time. These settings give students a way to picture the quantities before working with the abstract rational-number operations.]", "103": "[We will review integer multiplication and division in the warm-up and revisit the distinction between integers and rational numbers. I will model a hiking problem and emphasize how the integer sign rules carry into rational-number multiplication. Students will highlight that connection in their workbooks. After the division model, they will solve examples independently, compare with a partner, and explain the procedures using the lesson vocabulary.]", "105": "[I will review workbook solutions and ask students how they determined the sign of a product or quotient. Sharing solutions at the board will make their reasoning available for feedback. This will help me distinguish a computational error from uncertainty about the rule while checking the language used to explain the operation.]", "107": "[I will review integer and rational number and model product, quotient, and related operation vocabulary in context. Displayed terms and worked examples will support comprehension. Partner and whole-class explanations will require students to use the vocabulary while describing a method and responding to questions.]", "109": "[Visible steps, spoken directions, and consistent terminology will provide several ways to follow the lesson. FS may use a one- or two-minute regulation break when needed. Partner work will draw on his oral reasoning and social strengths, while guided examples and immediate clarification support sustained attention to the calculation.]", "115": "[CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NS.3: Solve real-world and mathematical problems involving the four operations with rational numbers]", "117": "[Students will interpret practical situations, choose among the four operations with rational numbers, and solve the resulting problems in context.]", "119": "[ELD.PI.7.6.A Ex: Explain ideas, phenomena, processes, and text relationships (e.g., compare/contrast, cause/effect, problem/solution) based on close reading of a variety of grade-level texts and viewing of multimedia, with moderate support. ELD.PI.7.6.C Ex: Use knowledge of morphology (e.g., affixes, roots, and base words), context, reference materials, and visual cues to determine the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words on familiar and new topics.]", "121": "[Students will use context, operation words, and visual information to clarify unfamiliar language in a word problem and explain which mathematical operations fit the situation.]", "123": "[The examples involve a boat being lowered through a lock and Kevin's score in a trivia game. Engineering systems and games provide concrete settings for interpreting changing quantities. A shared word bank and sentence frames will support explanations using terms such as unit rate, total score, increase, decrease, and change in temperature.]", "125": "[Students will begin with independent review of rational-number operations while I check work and ask about reasoning. We will discuss selected answers before I model how to interpret the first contextual problem. For the second example, students will read, highlight important language, and discuss their reasoning with a partner before class discussion. I will circulate, give feedback, and scaffold the interpretation where needed.]", "127": "[Independent example problems will show how students interpret the language and select an operation. I will ask them to explain that choice as I review their work, then use shared responses to assess class understanding. Accuracy and reasoning together will provide evidence for the content and language goals.]", "129": "[Vocabulary practice will be built into the calculations and word-problem discussions. I will ask students to explain an operation choice or the sign of a result and support them with models, partner talk, and written responses. The aim is to connect mathematical language with the reasoning it expresses.]", "143": "[Students will spend about seven minutes independently solving examples of rational-number multiplication and division. We will then review their answers and explanations as a class.]", "145": "[I will look for accurate sign rules, fraction simplification using the greatest common factor, and division by multiplying by the second fraction's reciprocal. While circulating, I will check both the written process and the student's explanation. A random selector will broaden participation during review. Questions about same or different signs will help students reason through an error, and feedback will reinforce accurate use of numerator, denominator, product, quotient, and reciprocal.]", "150": "[The cumulative assessment will contain 20 free-response questions across the four lessons. Two will convert fractions to decimals, including one terminating and one repeating result; two will convert decimals to simplified fractions; and two will compare rational numbers using the appropriate symbol. The test will also include eight computation problems, four integer sum-and-difference problems, and contextual questions using language such as above, below, distance, loss, share equally, and quotient. Together, these tasks assess procedures and interpretation in practical situations.]", "152": "[Students will demonstrate conversion, comparison, and the four operations with rational numbers, including their application in word problems. The language component involves interpreting mathematical terms and translating a written situation into symbols or equations. The assessment will therefore show procedural knowledge alongside the ability to understand and represent the language of a problem.]", "154": "[I will examine each student's written solutions and the steps used to reach them. I will check signs, operation procedures, simplification, and the representation of a word problem. Looking beyond the final answer will help me distinguish a minor calculation error from uncertainty about the method or meaning.]", "156": "[For conversion, I will check that the numerator becomes the dividend and that decimal place value is represented correctly. Fraction work should show common denominators where needed, appropriate simplification, and use of a reciprocal for division. Decimal calculations should show correct alignment or placement, and mixed numbers should be converted when the method requires it. I will also look for correct interpretation of signs and terms such as distance and quotient, since those language decisions affect the chosen calculation.]", "168": "[Students begin by reading the highlighted rule in their workbooks. At 00:87, I ask how integer addition and subtraction connect to other rational numbers; at 1:13, partners discuss that connection. At 1:36, students record a response while I read it aloud. I circulate at 2:04 to check the written explanations and clarify as needed.", "169": "At 2:58 and 5:18, questions about same and different signs return students to the reasoning behind the rule. I model the terms at 3:42 and 6:15, then have students record the rules and vocabulary at 3:58 and 6:24. The combination of discussion, listening, writing, and feedback reinforces both the procedure and the language used to explain it.]", "170": "Video Clip 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.", "171": "[Independent work showed that some students still needed help adding and subtracting rational numbers, so I model the process again. At 00:20, I use a problem with one decimal and one fraction and explain the need for a common form. At 00:35, I explain why I prefer a decimal representation for that example. At 00:46 and 00:50, I draw attention to place value and ask students to name the quantity represented.", "172": "At 1:35, we revisit the sign rules. When students cannot recall a rule, I pause and restate it. These questions give me immediate information about understanding and let me connect the correction to precise mathematical language.]", "173": "Video Clip 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.", "174": "[The game awards +2¼ points for a correct response and −½ point for an incorrect one. This familiar scoring context gives students a reason to combine rational numbers. At 00:11, I record and mark important words; from 00:18–00:37, I ask how those words relate to the calculation. At 00:44, a student explains the operation, and I reinforce the connection with multiplication.", "175": "At 1:08, I acknowledge the reasoning that a total requires a sum. At 1:23, students receive a similar problem with a different player and the same scoring rules. While circulating from 1:41–3:00, I ask targeted questions when a student becomes stuck. This lets me assess whether students can transfer the reasoning and use the problem's language to support their decisions.]", "176": "Video Clip 4 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.", "177": "[Students first attempt the unit-review problems independently, then explain their work during a discussion using a random name selector. At 00:10 and 00:18, my questions focus on how to begin and convert a mixed number to an improper fraction. At 00:42, another student identifies a common denominator. At 00:58, I record the work while modeling numerator and denominator.", "178": "From 1:17–1:53, I use smaller guiding questions when a student hesitates. At 2:44, I respond to a calculation error, then ask at 2:54 why addition is appropriate. These exchanges make the steps and reasoning visible and provide immediate support for both calculation and mathematical explanation.]", "182": "[You showed that you remember when to add or subtract integers and rational numbers. Some answers used the correct operation but had a sign error, so I deducted half a point for those mistakes. Before combining the numbers next time, circle their signs. That visual reminder can help you keep positive and negative values straight.", "183": "I deducted a full point on problems where you appeared unsure how to begin. In class and partner work, you have explained these ideas successfully with support. On the test, some steps were lost. We will practice carrying out the sequence independently, and I will give you a short checklist of the rules for operations with rational numbers.", "184": "Converting fractions to decimals was also difficult for you and several classmates. I will revisit long division with the class and provide additional guided practice through the conversion steps. Explaining your thinking aloud is a strength, so the next activity will begin with a partner. You will talk through the steps before trying them on your own. This will help you use what you know more confidently during independent work.]", "191": "[The class average was about 77%. Students generally handled familiar operations and could often convert mixed numbers or identify multiplication and division in context. The results also exposed important gaps.", "192": "Several students set up fraction-to-decimal division by placing the larger number inside, rather than using the numerator as the dividend. On question 7, some treated 15 feet below the surface and 2 feet above it as a distance of 13 feet rather than 17, overlooking direction and signs. Sign errors also affected otherwise appropriate procedures. Some students unnecessarily sought common denominators for multiplication or division. On question 16, confusion about quotient led a few students to choose the wrong operation. These patterns show a need to strengthen both conceptual understanding and interpretation of mathematical language.]", "194": "[Guided questions and repeated explanations helped students connect sign rules with their reasoning. Partner discussion gave them time to say the process aloud before sharing with the class. Those approaches sustained engagement and gave me opportunities to clarify vocabulary and procedures, supporting the progress seen for much of the group.]", "196": "[I would include more independent practice during the sequence. Students often succeeded with a partner or my guidance but did not apply the same steps consistently on the final assessment. I would also use a formative unit check or study guide before the summative test. The review emphasized later operations, while conversion gaps from earlier lessons remained. An earlier check would let me identify and reteach those foundations before students faced the cumulative assessment.]", "199": "[FS explained mathematical ideas well during guided and partner work, including the activities in Clips 2–4. The independent assessment showed a different pattern. He had difficulty recalling conversion steps, changing mixed numbers to improper fractions, and multiplying or dividing rational numbers. Rushing or losing focus may have contributed. He showed partial success in addition and subtraction but made sign errors. The analysis identifies question 5 as a successful contextual response, suggesting that he can select an operation when he understands the situation clearly.]", "201": "[My feedback gives FS concrete ways to transfer oral reasoning into independent written work. Circling signs and following a short checklist will help him pause and track the process. I also explained that other students found conversions difficult and that we would revisit them together. Guided reteaching followed by independent practice should strengthen both accuracy and confidence.]", "203": "[Partner and group discussion kept FS active and drew on his strong verbal reasoning. Combining listening, speaking, and writing helped him process the operations and maintain attention. This section of the assessment reflection identifies question 7 as evidence that instruction on contextual language and the modeling in Clip 3 supported his problem solving. I would retain those opportunities while strengthening the move to independent work.]", "205": "[Questions 1–4 and 12–15 show a need for more support transferring understanding to independent performance. I would check in with FS more frequently, provide a step checklist to make his reasoning visible, and structure independent work in short timed intervals. These changes would address attention and organization while giving him practice recording the procedures he can explain aloud.]", "212": "[Students will convert fractions to decimals through long division and decimals to fractions through place value, simplifying the resulting fraction.]", "214": "[Students will listen actively and explain conversion steps during partner and class discussion using appropriate mathematical vocabulary.]", "216": "[I will reconnect the fraction bar with division, using 8/2 = 8 ÷ 2 = 4 as an example. Students will set up division for another fraction independently, compare with a partner, and explain which number is the numerator and which is the denominator.", "217": "For decimal-to-fraction conversion, I will review place value, model how the final decimal place determines the denominator, and simplify the fraction. Students will try another example, discuss it with a partner, and explain the process aloud. These checks will show whether they understand the setup as well as the calculation.]", "219": "[I will connect conversions with sports statistics, game scores, money, and probability. References to finance, medicine, and STEM careers will draw on interests students have expressed. For FS, examples involving batting averages, shooting percentages, and win–loss ratios will connect the task with sports and competition.]", "221": "[FS will sit near the front for convenient check-ins and work with a reliable peer during structured discussion. Visual steps and oral explanation will support processing, while the partnership will draw on his social strengths. I will monitor engagement and clarify the procedure before expecting independent application.]", "223": "[The summative work identifies conversion as a specific need. Presenting a fraction as division and examining the quotient and remainder will give FS a clearer conceptual basis for remembering the steps. Structured partner talk will let him use his oral reasoning before applying the process independently in writing. This responds to the difference between his supported classroom performance and his test work.]", "225": "[Formative discussions showed that students, including FS, could explain and solve problems with support. The final assessment revealed conversion gaps that those checks had not fully captured because they emphasized later operations. I therefore selected conversion for reteaching and changed the explanation to make the division relationship explicit. Visual models, partner rehearsal, and focused checks will help me assess whether students can carry that understanding into independent work.]", "236": "[I reteach conversion by connecting fractions with division and decimals with place value. At 00:19, I ask which number belongs inside the division setup, and students respond correctly. At 00:36, I address the larger-number misconception directly: the numerator is the dividend regardless of its size.", "237": "I then ask students to identify the final decimal place and use it to construct a fraction. At 1:51, an incorrect answer leads me to write out the place values and guide the conversion visually. I finish by reinforcing simplification. These linked visual, spoken, and procedural cues give FS a clearer structure for understanding and explaining the steps.]"}, "originalQuotes": {"612": "However, on the summative assessment, the focus student had difficulty applying these skills independently. He was unable to recall the steps for converting between different forms of rational numbers and often forgot how to convert mixed numbers into improper fractions. He also showed confusion when multiplying and dividing rational numbers, likely due to his tendency to rush or lose focus when working alone. Despite these challenges, he was able to correctly solve problem #5, demonstrating that he could decode mathematical vocabulary and apply correct operations when he clearly understood the context of the problem. Additionally, he showed partial proficiency with adding and subtracting rational numbers but, like many of his peers, made sign errors when determining the final answer..", "586": "A common area of difficulty involved converting rational numbers between forms. Many students forgot how to correctly use long division when converting fractions to decimals where several incorrectly assumed the larger number should be the dividend instead of the numerator. When converting decimals to fractions, students often remembered to use decimal place values but neglected to simplify the resulting fraction, showing incomplete procedural fluency."}, "reviewDate": "October 11, 2026"}};
